      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>MCI) OTIGA LL'S PATBRT SELF—RA I SING FLO UIR 'ell= USED</head>
        <p>MCI) OTIGA LL'S PATBRT SELF—RA I SING FLO UIR 'ell= USED</p>
        <p>PLEASANT P.ARAGRAPITS,</p>
        <p>KEEPS IN ANY CLIMATE—A miser. FAME is to have everybody know one's weak. messes.</p>
        <p>IF some men could save all the time they lose by hurrying,. they would have plenty of leisure. TURNED OVER.—Maxwell : How are you getting on in your law practice.?" Skinnem : " Splendid ; although I have had only one client so far." Maxwell : "Is he wealthy.' Skinnem : "Ho was."</p>
        <p>_ SATISFIED.—" And so your husband is richer than you supposed he was " "Yes, indeed ! Why he can afford to keep a wife. I certainly did not expect him to be able to do that.'' THE fire of genius would blaze more brightly if it didn't have so much cold water thrown on it.</p>
        <p>THE average man would rather carry a Saratoga trunk than a baby. NEvEn judge a man by the clothes he wears. Form your estimate from the clothes his wife wears.</p>
        <p>" Yrs," said the chairman, F.adly, "our tem- perance meeting laa night would have been more successful if the lecturer hadn't been so absent- minded." "What did he do l" "He tried to blow the froth from a glass of water." "You must leally have that hat, miss! It suits you beautifully, and the shape's quite new and very fashienable. Besides, it makes you look at least 10 years younger! " Old. Maid: Oh, then I'll take two!" AN IDEAL MOTHER-IN-LAW. —" YOU say you have found your ideal in your mother-in-law ?" "Yes ! All the jokes that ever were made about mothers-in-law exactly suit her." DEBTOR AND CREDITOR.—" should like to know when you are going to pay that bill; I can't come here every day in the week." "What day would suit you best ? " "Saturday." "Very well, then, yen can call every Saturday." SURE OF ONE THING. —Little Johnny: "The teacher said to-day that we belong to the animal kingdom. Do you b'lieve boys and girls are animals?" Little Ethel: " Boys is." NEW TO Him —Miss Scribble: "The heroine of my next story is to be one of those madern advhnced girls wig) has ideas of her own -.nd doesn't want to get married." The Colonel (politely) : " Ah, indeed, I don't think I ever met that type."' A GREAT, RECORD.—Trivvet : "Miss Flop claims to have made a thousand refusals of offers of marriage." Dicer: "That's easily explained. When young Callow asked her to marry him, she replied, No—a thousand times no.'" "AND you went up the Rhine, I suppose ?" said Mrs. Malaprop. "Indeed, yes; it was beautiful." "And did you see any rhine- oceroses ?" _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>SOCIAL AMENMES.—" My husband," said a lady, "is in the habit of presenting me with a new book on each of my birthdays." To which Tameghe made reply: "Ah ! what a stupendous library you must have 1" THE TEACILM;;S JOKE.—Teacher: "Why didn't your brother come to school to day, Johnny?' .Johnny: "He hurt his foot this niornin' so he couldn't hardly walk." Teacher: "That's a lame 'excuse; but I suppose I must accept it." ALL THE SlGs.—Carrie: "I thought he was _going to propose to me at one time last night." May: "What made you think so?" Carrie: "Both of us felt so embarrassed and silly." MAKING HIM COMFORTABLE.—New Boarder Ashivering) : "This stove is too small for this room." Landlady (kindly): "So it is. I'll have it moved in to a smaller room for you." THE QUICK AND THE DEAD.—Bob's Widow: "Do you dare to sit there and tell me you con- 15ider yourself a better man than poor, dear Bob ?" Her Brother: "01 course I do, for he's dead." BINKS : "I had my diamond pin stolen last night." Inspector How can it be indentified ?" Binks : "It had a patent thief-safety-chain ..attached." _ • -</p>
        <p>" OH, I'M in such trouble! My little Willie's Lot lost ! " " Well, well, it'll be all right ! Every one in the neighbourhood knows him ! " "Oh, nobody 'll know him to-day, because I've just washed him ! "</p>
        <p>THE REASON • OF IT.—Fond Husband: "Some- how, I don't seem to be able to enjoy those pies as .1 did those my mother made for me when I was a boy." Loving Wife: "Perhaps you would, if you hadn't ruined your stomach by eating so many of those same pies, when you were a boy." EXPERIMENTING.—" Why, how is this? You, your wife, and all the family have got your faces bandaged! Are you all afflicted with the tooth- ache? "You see, our oldest is learning to be a dentist and so he is drawing our teeth for a bit of practice." ANGELINA (anxiously) : , "Are you sure, dear, that you don't regret it, and that you don't some- times miss your life as a bachelor?" Edwin (with 'cheerful conviction) : "Not a bit, I tell you what, Angy, I miss it so little that if I were to lose you —a—l'm blessed if I wouldn't marry again." AFTER THIRTY YEAES OF MARRIED LIFE.— Monsieur shows Madame a lock of hair which -she ' presented to him on the day of their betrothal. "Ah !" he sighed, "how tenderly we loved each ,otherat that time !" "To be sure," blurted out his gentle mate, "we were young and foolish then." MATRIMONIAL. —The following advertisement :appeared in a foreign paper under the above bead- ing: "Wanted, a wife for my papa. He is sft. •in height, is fair complexioned, with dark eyes, has all his front teeth and a Roman nose, and dresses in the height of fashion: I .promiee good treatment to my future step-mother if she suits me. Young ladies (or widows) with, means, but without :children, will please address Miss X., Post Office, k". "JONES, BROWN, ;Dit SMITH? ".The patrony-! mic Miller lor Midler) is considered one of the hest known and 'most widely spread of German. .names. A Frenchman' who had travelled-through: ,Germany, sat clOwn'tohis desk and wrote a book. -which commenced with these , words: "The Germans are a people who are called Miller." The man was not so far wrong. According to the latest statistics there are now living in Germany no fewer than 62.9,987 Millers. Thus every seventy-third person in Germany is a Miller, whether he likes it or not. A SOFT ANSWER. —Frangois -is an excellent servant,and always sits up for his master. One night Viscount de B—, Fran nis' master, came home from his club at 3 o'clock in themorning; He 'found his faithful valet in his bedroom, reclinine fast asleep on the sofa near the fire,. Instead of waking him, he quietly Undressed and „got into bed. Ten minutes afterwards Franeois awoke and exclaimed, "Past 3 o'clock, and the beast hasn't come in yet ! " Tho viscount raised his head and said in a very gentle voice, "Yost may go to bed, Franeois, the beast has got back to its lair." " Jou.ss," said ,a wife who war supposed to be on her death-bed, "in ease of my death I think a • man of your temperament and domestic nature,: .apart fryna Vie good Of the c hitch-en; ought to marry a.gaip." you,- think so, my dear'?" "1 certa.iely_ do, after a reasonable length-ef time." 4' Well, now, do you know, my dear, that relieves( any mind of a preat; burden. The little widow -Jenkins has acted rather demure towards me ever kinte•ybu were taken sick.. She is not the woman that you are, of course, a strong-minded, intern- gent, woman of character, but she is plump and pretty, and I am sure she would make me a very desit able, wife." The next day Mrs. John was Able to sit up, the followine day she went down- stairs, and on the third day she was planning for</p>
        <p>new dress. Wurs ill, man is very susceptible to kindness, ..and a pretty nurse is apt to become dangerous to his peace. • Not long ago a man with a broken leg .and an attractive appearance languished in. a city hospital. The demure, white-capped nurse began to take an unusual interest in him, and, after a time, asked him if there was nothing she could do for him—no book she could read, no letter she _ _</p>
        <p>could write. The'patient gratefully accepted the .latter offer;and the nurse prepared to write from his dictation. He began with a tender address to his "dearest love," and the little nurse felt slightly emlArrased. But she continued through the most ardent declarations of all absorbing affection to the end, where he wished to be sub cribed an adoring lever for all time. Then she folded the letter and slipped it into an envelope. "To Stilolll shall I direct it? " she asked. The wicked young fellow said, amiably and tenderly, "What is your name, please ? ' They have been married more than a year now.</p>
        <p>All rights reserved.</p>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>CARD</head>
        <p>CARD</p>
        <p>IZNOWN as Rev. Joseph Holmes' Mexican Pre:cription -5-‘- is the only origins! gutranteed Remedy for all those who suffer from nervous wealtress, exhatuted vitality- kidney or bladder diseases, gravel, prostate, spinal weak • ness, brain fag and kindred coa3plaint3. It has stood the test for twenty years. Send a self-addressed stamped nvelope to Jos. HOLMES' Remedy Co. Bloomsbury Man eions, Bloomsburi Square, London, and get full particulars of this great specific fret; of charge. Mention whether for male or female, Beware of fraudnlent imitators.</p>
        <p>Cocoa is more than a mere ctimulating and refreshing drink, it is also a nutritious fooi, and one of the meat prec- ious gifts of nature—sustaining and Invigorating the system probably more than any other beverage. The Lancet refers to Cadbury's Cocoa as "the standard of highest purity at present attainable In regard to Cocoa." No alkalies used.—Advt. •</p>
