      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>TB.INGS A WOMAN WANTS TO KNOW.</head>
        <p>TB.INGS A WOMAN WANTS TO KNOW.</p>
        <p>feYr T)re.tty shirts is greatly on the increase just now, and no wonder, for, with the hope of a long Spell of ffile weather before us, we cannot have two many of them. For ray first sketch I am giving you quite one of the newest designs for a lady's shirt. It is made of a delicately-tinted French cambric, the back'being fashioned with the usual yoke. The front has three tucks on either side, and fald in the centre, edged With a finely kilted frill.</p>
        <p>There is a slight, alteration in the collars of shirts this year, many being made with a stand-up collar-band and a small round collar turning down over it. Such is the case in our model shirt. The cuffs again are treated in a similar way; the ordinary shirt cuffs are there, and a small cuff, edged with a frill, turned back over them.</p>
        <p>There is a certain advantage in a shirt of this kind, for it looks perfectly well when worn</p>
        <p>A 1.a,-:l,'</p>
        <p>Shirt. No. 3049. PaTer Pattern, cia.</p>
        <p>To 1,0 had from PATTERN DEZA.T.TMENT,IB, HEN.EIETTA 'STREET, LONLON, W.C.</p>
        <p>without a coat, a qualification which is not pcssessed by the stiff-fronted shirt with stand- up collar and cuffs.</p>
        <p>I notice that many of the new shirts are being made of a kind of cord muslin, and very pretty are they, especially with narrow inser- tion let in between the tucks. If you decide to ak this shirt for yourselves, I would advise you to purchase our pattern, which you can do tor the sum of 6A. Supposing that you are intending to make two or three shirts, it is quite possible to make a slight variation, and i‘ stead of having three tucks on either side, to have two small box-pleats with a piece of insertion let in between. _ _ _</p>
        <p>_ Do not be alarmed at the idea of letting in it:sertion; it is quite a simple process, and one which can be carried out by anyone who pcssesses moderate neatness. Lay the em- broi,...ery on the shirt exactly where you wish it to go, taking care that it is quite straight on the material. Stitch it neatly by machine, :Ind then cut away the materiallrom the back, •arefully over-sewing the edge with a fine cotton.</p>
        <p>Aty Eecond sketch shows the very latest model of a sleeve, and in sleeves there ig very marked change this sc ason. The full zigot sleeves are no more seen, but, instead, sleeves are made tight-fitting, with a small pull at the top. This, too, instead of being a balloon puff, is prettily draped, as shown in the sketch. _ _ _</p>
        <p>I can safely assure you that in copying this sleeve you will have the very newest design possible. When renovating our dresses, and this is it thing most of us have to consider, the sleeves generally show the date more than any-</p>
        <p>Sleeve. K0.^8050.! Paper Pattern, 41(1.</p>
        <p>To ij z.i rorn PATTER:: DEPARTAINNT, IS, HENIMITTA STREET, LO*DON, W.C.</p>
        <p>thing else, so if you have a last year's dress, remove the sleeves, inserting others similar to my sketch in their place, and. you Will have the •:,atisfaetion of knowing that your bodice is qt.ite up-to-date. - • Now tlipt grass muslin is so much used, it is nit,-!etaet4ble to renovate a last year's dress - easily. For instance, a black alpaca which did duty last year can be se-modelled, and be made into a smart gown for this year ia the l'ollowing way: Make a bodice of grass muslin trimmed with .Valenciermes lace, and last year'S gigot' sleeves make a high.tiA,h?i band which will •hook JOll thr:-: left s'de of the ft:Alt under a small heading of the itiateri;;.l. Paste buckles to fasten ,it wifl greall3,-enbance its beauty. ,Most.of the coAhmes this year are „treated ;tbis wax a light-Coloured blouse bodice being -Used, while the material to • match the skirt is also shown on the bodice. This can he managed either by means of the corsage belt, as described above, or „with, braces. of the material., Another pretty way is to have a Ok Nvith straps of. the material to the wait, showing the bodice of a4contrasting material underneath.</p>
        <p>-4 I notice that during the summer months many people prefer to Lave cold supper in the eveuino rather than dinner, anal am sure they will find the following recipe for Veal Gate .11 cxceedingly good:. Grease a plain mrntld, .line it-with ehopred ham, pistachio. nuts; ar d !in IL:l.t, so as to hare variegated ipealvaice. Fill the mould with slices uncooked real AiCet 'at!'" forcemeat, wah Very -thin layers of chopped ham at intervals. Pour over all one gill of:iitock so as to fill up crevices. Covor the nionld with a plate, and bake slowly for ',two horns. Leave till cold, turn out to and garnish to taste. Her a simple recipe for lemonailepowder: Take a pound of caster sugar and add to it, fifteen drcps of essence of lemon. 'When mixed add an ounce of citric acid and pass three times through a 'wire sieve. Place in a well-corked, bottle, and when required for use add half 'a teaspoonful of the mixture to half a tumbler ,-01 water, When a. refreshing drink will be 'obtained.</p>
