      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATUBD a MAY 9, 1896 THE HIDDEN HAND;</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATUBD a MAY 9, 1896 THE HIDDEN HAND;</p>
        <p>Town just now is looking very bright and gay. The trees in the parks and squares are radient with fresh green foliage, houses are looking spick and span in their new coats of paint and smart people may be seen in all directions. Church Parade last Sunday was a very large and fashionable affair and now the chairs are once more arranged on the lawn opposite Park-lane, it is possible to enjoy the many charming toilettes en evidence without the fatigue of stroll ng up and down. It appears the Queen has decided thit there are to be no State balls at Buckingham Palace this year on account of the death of Prince Henry of Battenberg, but there will be two State concerts, one of which is to be given a few days before the Royal wedding. About the same time the Marchioness ot Salisbury will give a garden party at Hatfield, to which a large number of distin-; guishe..l guests will be invited and the Prince and Princess II of Wales. will be present. The little son of Mr. George Gould has already been provided for in a most ample and luxurious manner,and ' never before was such exquisite finery tucked beneath a baby's 3hubby chin. The blankets belonging to this little millionaire's cot are like a mass at sott fluffy snow, and are bound with light blue taffeta, with rosettes ot baby ribbon at the edges. The tiny sheets are made of linen so fine they could be drawn tnrough a ring to fit the baby's finger, and trimmed with real Valenciennes lace. The quilts are of blfte silk quilted by hand, adorned with great bows of blue ribbon painted with rosebuds, and the pillow casings match the sheets. All is exquisite loveliness and yet I hear upon the beat autho- rity that the small millionaire cries as much as most babies. a</p>
        <p>We have heard much of "George Egerton," the author of " Keynotes " and "Discords," but tow know that her husband has literary aspirations—yet Mr.Clare- niont has arranged to publisn shortly a series of sketches of life in South Africa with the general heading of "The Afrikander." Mr. Claremont, who writes from personal observation describes the Boers and Kaffirs in peace and war, as well as sport, adventure, and mining operations in South Africa. "George Egerton" goes out to join her husband at the Cape soon.</p>
        <p>The Hun. Mrs. Henniker, sister of the present Lord Houghton and daughter of the famous Monvkton Milues, will issue shortly a volume of fiction, the title of which is "In Scarlet and Grey." The contents will consist mostly of military tales contributed to magazines, and beside these one story of a tragic order. Tnis is called "The Spectre of the Real," and has already appeared in Mr. Jerome's popular weekly.</p>
        <p>The various new means by which our descendants propose to be conveyed from point to point are now on view at the Imperial Institute in London at the inter- national exhibition of motot-cars. Beside automobiles of all kinds, the exhibition contains models of flying- machines including that of Mr. Hiram Maxim of which much has been heard during the past few years. Rapid flights from the ground to the top of the tower are to be included in the programme. Whether they come otT re- main, to be seen. Rapid flights from the top of the tower to the ground sound more probable. Hostesses are complaining with much bitterness again this season of the difficulty of finding men willing to dance. The complaint is not a new one. The whole thing is too much trouble for the gilded youth, who is worn to a thread with intellectual exertion. Dancing is a frivolity beneath his notice. He can gamble enthusiastically enough. Ile can eat supper and swallow champagne until the marvel is that ne still lives. H) can get up early to go racing, sit up late drinking innumerable whiskies and sodas, and ride in steeplechases, but danc- ing is altogether beyond him—at present. But depend upon it fashion will change. Dancing is not a pastime that will ever entire;y die out.</p>
        <p>Apropos, the Duch( ss of Saxe Coburg Gotha enjoys a cotillon above any other dances, while her daughter Princess Marie of Roumania, and the Duchess of Con- naught like waltzing. The Prince of Wales is also con- sidered a good waltzer. The Princess of Wales likes the lancers, and the Duchess of Fife is enamoured of a set of quadrilles. All the Royal family including the Queen, are fond of Sir Roger de Coverley.</p>
        <p>The smartest Parisian gowns are lifted well off the ground and many cf them are even shorter at the back than they are in frost. Skirts that rest on the ground at the back look dowdy and old-fashioned, and why our country women cannot fall in with a mode at once so sensible, convenient, and cleanly,it is difficult to under- stand. Buttons of all shapes and sizes play a prominent part as adornment this year. The square or lozenge form however is more favourably looked upon than the others, especially for skirts.</p>
        <p>The latest capes fit into the waist in a point at the back and two points in front,the only cape part of them being the sleeves. Sometimes these are single, some- times double and_sometimes treble. Quantities of ribbon are used for bows on the shoulders, at the waist, and down the front. The upstanding collars of these capes are assuming wonderful proportions, as if ambitious to meet the hat obligingly drooping forward. By the way a velvet lining to a plain or tabbed collar of just one's most becoming tone is an item of our toilette not to be neglected.</p>
        <p>Zouaves are enjoying considerable favour, a fact we should be grateful for on account of their very useful renovating and smartening properties. I have seen this season very dainty and becoming specimens fashioned in pretty silks lined with some contrasting colour,and these are worn with a gown that has become shabby under the arms and otherwise seen better days, is an improvement that cannot be appreciated until seen.</p>
        <p>Tea rooms, in the West end :of London are rapidly becoming the popular form of commercial enterprise for women, who have established, during the last year quite a net work of these little concerts in,and around Regent- street and Bond-street.. These dainty little establish- ments, where the tea, and cakes, and sandwiches, are of the best quality, are an immense improvement upon the ordinary confectioner's shop, and as soon as London is duly supplied the attention of enterprising ladies might, with advantage, be turned to other parts, where seduc- tive confectionery and good tea are unobtainable.</p>
        <p>It is satisfactory to learn that emigration offers some prospects for educated British women who find exisit- ence at home difficult and even desperate. It should not, however, be taken' haphazard but under the aus- pices of one or other of the Women's Emigration Societies. The Women's Colonial Training Home at Leaton in Shropshire does excellent work in this respect. The course of instruction includes bee-keeping, poultry- rearing, milking, household work, baking, dressmaking, and all the branches of knowledge that are required in colonial life. The pupils get up early and lead a simple farm life, taking it in turns to do the work of the house- hold. The terms are very moderate. and so far a better class of women, who can associate on terms of equality with the families of colonial gentlemen, whilst helping the wives in the ordinary household duties, have been much in request in South Africa, Canada,British Colum- bia and other places, and their position is a much happier and superior one to that of the lady help or nursery:governess here.</p>
        <p>The unusually mild winter and spring has brought us such a wealth of bloom from the sunny South, the Scilly Isles, and the Channel Illes.that our street corners have been one continual flower show, and even the poorest among us have been wearing buttonholes. The quant;- ties of narcissi that are estimated to have flowered this season in St. Mary's, the larv.est of the Scilly Isles, amounts in round numbers to 300,000,000. In Holland, abouE 90,000 narcissus bulbs are reckoned to the acre for he purpose of flowering. St. Mary's is about nine miles in circumference, and as a good deal of the land is pas- ture, the number quoted becomes all the more astound- ing. •</p>
        <p>SAGO SOUFFLE in eplicious. Take half a pint of milk 3 ounces ofaugar, 24 ounces of sago. and 24 ounces of, butter, place in a saucepan and let all boil together till the sago is transparent.stirring frequently. Remove the saucepan from the fire and let the mixture cool. Line ,the bottom of a cake tin with buttered paper, add the yolks of four eggs to 'the sago; end at the last moment the whites, previously beaten to a stiff froth. These should be stirred in lightly,not beaten. Tie down securely with a floured cloth, and steam for an hour. " MADGE."</p>
        <p>OR, CAPITOLA TIIE MADCAP. BY MRS. E. D. E. N. SO UTHWOUT 11.</p>
        <p>THE HIDDEN HOUSE.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXXV. The very stains and fractures on the wall, Assuming features solemn and terrific. Hinted some tragedy of that old hall Locked up in hieroglyphic! Prophetic hints that filled the soul with dread But to one gloomy window pointing mostly, The while some secret inspiration said, That chamber is the ghostly !"</p>
        <p>-HOOD.</p>
        <p>The Hidden House was a large, irregular edifice, of dark red sandstone, with its walls covered closely with the clinging ivy, that had been clipped away only from a few of the doors and windows, and its roof over-shadowed by the top branches of gigantic oaks and elms that clustered around and nearly concealed the building.</p>
        <p>It Might have been a long-forsaken house for any sign of human habitation that was to be seen about it. All was silent, solitary and gloomy. As Capitols, drew up her horse to gaze upon its sombre walls, she wondered which was the window at which the spectral light and ghostly face had beeir seen.. She soon believed that she had found it.</p>
        <p>At the highest point of the building, imme- di-itely under the sharp angle of the roof, in the gable end nearest to view, was a solitary window. The ivy that clung tightly to the stone, covering every portion of the wall at this end, was clipped away from that high-pla,ced, dark and lonely window by which Capitola's eyes were strangely fa scinated.</p>
        <p>While thus she gazed in wonder, interest and curiosity, though without the least degree of superstitious dread, a vision flashed upon her sight, that sent the blood from her ruddy cheek to her brave heart and shook the foundations of her un- belief!</p>
        <p>For while she (fazed, suddenly that dark window was ilumined by a strange, unearthly light that streamed forth into the gloomy evening air, and touched with blue flame the quiverinc, leaves of every tree in its brilliant line ! In the midst of this lighted window appeared a white female face wild with woe I And then the face suddenly vanished and the light was swallowed up in dark- uess</p>
        <p>Capitola remained transfixed! " brea,t Heaven !" she thought, "can these things really be! Have the ghostly traditions of this world truth in them at last? When I heard this story of the haunted window I thought some one had surely imagined or invented it! Now I have seen for myself! but if I were to tell what I have seen not one in a hundred would believe me !" _ _</p>
        <p>While these startling thoughts disturbed her usually well balanced mind, a vivid flash of lightning, accompanied by is tremendous peal of thunder and a heavy fall of rain, roused her into renewed activity. "Gyp. my boy, the storm is upon u sure enough! We shall catch it all around! get well drowned, beaten and buffetted here and well abused when we get home. Meantime, Gyp, which is the worst, the full fury of the tempest or the mysterious terrors of the haunted house l" Another blinding flash of lightning, a stunning crash of thunder, a flood of rain and tornado of wind decided her. "We'll take the haunted house, Gyp, my friend. That spectral lady of the lighted window looked rather in sorrow than in anger, and who knows but the ghosts may he hospitable? So gee up, Dobbin," said Capitola,, and urging her horse with one hand and holding her cap with the other, she went on against wind and rain until she reached the front of the old house.</p>
        <p>Not a creature was to be seen ; every door and window was closely shut. Dismounting Capitola led her horse under the shelter of a thickly leaved old oak tree, secured him and then holding up her saturated skirt with one hand and holding on her cap with the o• her. she went up some moulderin,, stone steps to an old stone portico, and seizing the heavy iron knocker of a great black oak double door, she knocked loudly enough to awaken all the mountain echoes. She waited a few minutes for an answer, but receiving none, she knocked again more 1 udly than before. Still there was no reply. And grow- ing impatient, she seized the knocker with both hands and exerting all her strength, made the welkin ring again. This brought a response. The door was un- locked and angrily, jerked open, by a short, squarely formed, beetle-browed, stern looking woman, clothed in a black stuff gown and having a stiff muslin cap upon her head. 'Who are you? What do you want here ?" harshly demanded this woman, whom Capitola instinctively recognised as Dorky Knight, the mo- rose housekeeper of the Hidden House. "Who am I? What do I want? Old Nick fly away with you, it's plain enough to be seen who I am and what I want. lam a young woman caught out in the storm, and I want shelter !" said Cap indignantly. And her words were endorsed by a terrific burst of the tempest in lightning, thunder, wind and rain. "Come in then and when you ask favours learn to keep a civil tongue in your head," said the woman sternly, taking the guest by the hand and pulling her in, and shutting and locking the door. " Favours ! plague on you for a bearess 1 I ask no favour! every storm-beaten traveller has a right to shelter under the first roof that offers, and none</p>
        <p>but a curmudgeon would think of calling it a favour!. And as for keeping a civil tongue in my_ head, I'll do it when you set me the example," said Cap. "Who are you !” again demanded the womay. "Oh, I see you are no Arabian in your notions of hospitality !—Those pagans entertain a guest without asking him a single question; and though he were their bitterest foe, they consider him, while he rests beneath their tent, sacred from in- trusion."</p>
        <p>"That's because they are pagans," said Dorky. "But as I am a Christian, I'd thank you to let me know who it is that I have received under this roof." _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>_ _ "My name," said our heroine impatiently "is Capitola Black ! I live with my uncle, Major Warfield, at Hurrimne Hall. And now, I should thank your ladyship to send someone to put away my horse, while you yourself accommodate me with dry clothes." While our &amp;alley little heroine spoke the whole aspect of the dark-browed woman changed. "Capitola Capitola," she muttered, gazing earnestly upon the face of the unwelcome guest. " Yes ! Capitola! that is my name; you never heard anything against it, did you T" For alt answer the woman seized her hand, and while the lightning dashed, and the thunder rolled, and the wind and rain beat down, she drew her the whole length of the hall before a back window that overlooked the neglected garden, and regard-- less of the electric fluid that inCessantly blazed upon them, she held her there and scrutinized her features. " Well ! I like this! upon my word, I do 1" said Cap, composedly. Without replying the strange woman seized her right hand, toreibty opened it, gazed upon the palm, ant then flinging it back with a .shudder, exclaimed: "Capitola, what brought you under this roof! Away! Begone! Mount your horse and fly while there is yet time." " What I exp 'se myself again to the storm? I wont, and that s flat, said Cap. "Girl ! girl ! there are worse dangers in the world than any to be feared from thunder, light- ning, rain. or wind." " Very well, then, when I meet them, it will be time enough to deal with them Meanwhile the stormy ni:ht and my soaked clothing are very palpable evils, and as I sec no good end to be gained by my longer enduring them, I will just beg you to stop soot h-saying—rsas I have had enough of that from another' old witeh)—and be so good as to permit me to change my clothes.' "It is madness! You shall not stay here," cried the woman, in a harsh voice, "And I tell you I will ! You are not at the head of the family, and I do not intend to be turnd emit by you.' While she spoke, a servant crossed the hall, and thewoman, whisking Capito'a around until tier back was turned and her face concealed, went to speak to the new comer. "When will your master be here?' Capitola heard her inquire. "Not to-night ; he saw the storm rising and did not wish to expose himself; he sent me on to say that he would not be here until morning ; I was caught as you see! I am dripping wet," replied the man. "Go change your clothes at once, then, Davy." "Who is that stranger ?" asked the man, point- ing to Capitola. "Some young woman of the neighbourhood,</p>
        <p>win) has been caught out in the tempest. But you had better go and change your clothes than to steel here gessipling," said the woman harshly. "1 iy," said the man, " the young woman IL Ged-,end to liss Clara ; nobody has been to see her yet ; nobody ever visits this house unless t;)ey an: driven to it ; I don't wonder the colonel and our young master pass as much as ten months in the year away from home, spending all the summer at the watering places, and all the winter in New York or W a,sh g ton ?" " Rohl your tongue ! what right have you to complain? You always attend them in their travels !" "True ; but you see for this last season, they have both been staying here, old master to watch the heiress, young master to court her, and as I have no interest in that game, I find the time hang heavy on my hands," complained the man. " It will haw, heavier if you take a long fit of illness by standing in wet clothes," muttered the woman. " Why, so 'twill, missus ! so here goes," as- sented the man, hurrying across the hall and passing out through the door opposite that by which he entered.</p>
        <p>Dorcas returned to her guest. Eyeing her closely for a while, she at length in- quired: " Capitola, how long have you lived at Hurricane Hall ?" "So long," replied Cap, "that you must have beard of me. I, at least, have often heard of Mother Dorkey Knight !" "And heard no good of her." "Well, no, to be candid with you, I never did,' said Cap. "And much harm of her r continued the wo- man, keeping her stern black eyes fixed upon those of her guest. "Well, yes—since you ask me, I have heard pretty considerable harm !" answered Cap, nothing daunted. "Where did you live before you came to Hurri- cane Hall ?" asked Dorcas. "Where I learned to fear God, to speak the truth, and to shame. the devil !" replied Cap. "And to force yourself into people's houses against their will!" "There you are again! I tell you that when I learn from the head of this household that I am unwelcome, then ~I will retreat, and not until then! And now I demand to be presented to the master." "To Colonel Le Noir ?" "Yes." "I cannot curse you with the the curse of a granted prayer Colonel Le Noir is away." "Why do you talk so strangely?' inquired Cap- itola. "It is my whim. Perhaps my head is light." "I should think it was, excessively so ! Well— as the master of the house is away, be good enough to present me to the mistress "What mistress? there is no mistress here !" replied Dorcas, looking around in strange trepida- tion. _ _</p>
        <p>"I mean the young lady, Colonel Le Noir's ward. In lieu of any other lady, she, I suppose, may be considered the mistress of the house! "Humph! we:l, young girl, as you are fully resolved to stand your ground, I suppose there is nothing to do but to put up with you!' said Dorcas.</p>
        <p>"And put up my horse," added Cap. "He shall be taken care of! But mind, you must depart early in the morning," said Dorcas, sternly. "Once more, and for the last, Mother Cerberus, I assure you I do not acknowledge your authority to dismiss me,' retorted Capitola, " so show me to the presence of your mistress !' "Perverse, like all the rest. Follow me," said the housekeeper, leading the way from the hall towards a back parlour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>CHAPTER XXXvI.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XXXvI.</p>
        <p>THE INMATE OF THE HIDDEN HOUSE. "There is a light around her brow, A holiness in those dark eyes, That show, though wandering earthward now, Her spirit's home is in the skies." —MOORE. Pushing open the door, Dorcas Knight • ex- claimed:</p>
        <p>. " Here is a young lady, Miss Black, from Hur- ricane Hall, come to see you, Miss Day. And having made this announcement, the woman retired and shut the door behind her.</p>
        <p>And Capitola found herself in a large, dark, gloomy, wainscotted room, whose tail, narrow windows afforded but little light, and whose im- mense fire-place and blackened furniture seemed to belong to a past century. The only occupant of this sombre apartment was a young girl, seated in pensive thought beside the central table. She was clothed in deep mourning, which only served to throw into fairer relief the beauty of her pearly skin, golden hair and violet eyes. The vision of her mourning robes and melancholy beauty so deeply impressed Capitola, that almost for the first time in her life she hesitated, from a feeling of diffidence, and said gently : "Indeed, I fear that this is an unwarranted in- trusion on my part, Miss Day." "You are very welcome," replied the sweetest voice Capitola had ever heard, as the young girl arose and advanced to meet her. "But you have been exposed to the storm. Please come into my room and change your clothes," continued the young hostess as she took Cap s hand aud led her into an adjoining room. The storm was still raging • but these apartments being in the central portion of the strong old house, were but little exposed to the sight or sound of its fury.</p>
        <p>There was a lamp .burning upon the mantel- piece, by the light of which the young girl furn- ished her visitor with dry clothing, and assisted her to change—saying as she did so: 'I think we are about the same size, and that Inv clothes will fit you ; but I will not offer you mourning habiliments ; you shall have this lilac silk.'</p>
        <p>"I am very sorry to see you in mourning," said Car itola., earnestly, "It is for my father," replied Clara, very softly. As they spoke, the eyes of the two young girls roet. They were both good physiognomists and intuitive judges of character. Consequently, in the full meeting of their eyes, they read, under- stood and appreciated each other.</p>
        <p>- The pure, grave and gentle expression of Clara's countenance touched the heart of Capitola. The bright, frank, honest face of Cap recom- mended her to Clara. The very opposite traits of their evally truth- ful characters attracted them to each other.</p>
        <p>Clara conducted her guest back into the wains- cotted parlour, where a cheerful lire had been kindled to correct the dampness of the air. And here they sat down unmindful of the storm that came much subdued through the thickness of the walls. And, as young creatures, however tried and sorrowful, will do, they entered into a friendly chat. And before an hour had passed Capitola, thought hez self well repaid for her sufferings from the storm and the rebuff, in having formed the acquaintance of Clara Day. She resolvel, let Old Hurricane rage as he might, henceforth she would be a freqnent visitor to the Hidden House.</p>
        <p>And Clara, for her part, felt that in Capitol&amp; the had found a fr.nk, spirited, faithful neighbour wit.) might become an estimable friend. While they were thus growing into each other's favour, the (icor opened and admitted a gentleman of tall and thin figure, and white and emaciated face, shaded by a luxuriant growth of glossy black hair and beard. He could not have been more than twenty-six, but prematurely broken by vice, he seemed forty years of age. He advanced, bowing, towards the young women. • As Capitola's eyes fell upon this new-comer it required all her presence of mind and power of self- control to prevent her from starting or otherwise betraying horself—for in this stranger she recog- nised as vrymanwho had stopped heruoton her night ride ! She did, however, succeed in banishing from her face every expression of consciousness. And when Miss Day courteously presented him to her guest, saying merely : "My cousin, Mr. Craven Le Noir, Miss Black,* --Capitol a arose and curtsied as composedly as if she had never set eyes upon his face before. Ile, on his part, evidently remembered her, and llt one stealthy, keen and scrutinising glance :too her face ; ut finding that imperturbable, he owed with stately politeness, and seemed satin- fic)t that .me had not identified him as her assail, t.</p>
        <p>Craven Le Ncir drew his chair to the fire, ,te,l himse;f, and entered into an easy conversa.- ii,ll with Clara and her guest Whenever he i,i,iressed Clara there was a deference and tender- n,,ss in his tone and glance that seemed very (ii•iplea3ing to the fair girl, who received all these ,ielieate attentions with coldness and reserve. The.se things did not escape the notice of Capitola, who mentally .concluded that Craven Le Noir was a lover of Clara Day, but a most unacceptable one..</p>
        <p>When supper was announced, it was eviaentty hailed by Clara as a great relief. And after the meal was over, she arose and excused herself to her cousin by saying that her guest, Miss Black, had been exposed to the storm and was doubtless very much fatigued and that she would show her to her chamber. Then taking a night-lamp she invited Capitola, to come, and conducted her to an old-fashioned upper chamber, where a cheerful fire was burning on the hearth. Here the young girls sat down before the fire and improved their acquaintance by an hour's conversation. After which Clara arose and saynig "1 sleep immediately below your room, Miss Black. If you should want anything, rap on the floor, and I shall hear you and get up.'' She wished her guest a good night's rest, and . retired from the room. Cap was disinclined to sleep; a strange super- stitious feeling which she could neither understand nor throW off, had fallen upon her spirits. She took the night-lamp in her hand and got up to examine her chamber. It was a large, dark, oals-pa,nnelled room, with a dark carpet on the floor, and dark green curtains on the windows and the k edstead. Over the mantelpiece hung the portrait of a most beautiful black-haired and olack- eyed girl of about fourteen years of age, but upon whose infantile brow fell the shadow of sonic fear- ful woe. There was something awful in the despair "on that fa e, so young," that bound the gazer in an irresis, ible and most painful spell. And Capi- tola remained standing belore it transfixed, until the striang LA the hall clock aroused her from her enchantment. Wondering who the young creature could have been, what hail een her histor‘,, and above all what had been the nature` Of that fearful woe that darkened like a curse her angel brow, Capitola turned almost sorrowfully away, and began to prepare fer bed, She undressed, put on the delicate night-clothes Clara had provided for her use, said her en ening prayers—looked ttmler the bed—a precaution taken ever since the night upon which she had discovered the burglars—and finding all right, she blew out her candle and lay down. She could not sleep— many poisons of neivous or mercurial tempera- ments cannot do so the first night in a strange bed. Cap was very mercurial, and the bed and room in which she lay were very straliqe ; for the first time since she had had a home to call her own, she was unexpectedly staying all night away„- from her friends, and without their having any knowledge of her whereabouts ; she was conjeetu, ing, half in fear and half in fun, how Old Hurricane was taking her ,escapade, and whAt lie would say to her in the morning t She was - wondering to find herself in such an unforeseen position as that of a night- guest in the mysterious Hidden House—wondering whether this were the guest-chamber in which the ghost appeared to the officer, and these were the very curtains that the pale lady drew out at night. While her thouglas were thus running over the whole range of circumstances around her singular positien, sleep overtook Capitola, and speculation was lost in brighter visions. How long she had 'slept and dreamed she did not know, when something gently awakened her. She opened her eyes calmly—to meet a vision that, brave as she was, nearly froze the blood in her warm veins! Her chamber was illumined with an intense blue flame that lighted up every portion of the apart- ment with a radiance bright as day; and in the midst of this effulgence moved a figure clothed in white—a beautiful, pale, spectral woman, whose large, motionless black eyes; deeply set in her death-like face, and whose long, unbound black hair, fallen upon her white raiment, were the only marks of colour about her marble.form. - Paralysed with wonder, Capitola watched this figure as it glided about the chamber. The appa- rition approached the dressing-table, seemed to take something thence, and then gliding 'towards the bed—to Capitola's inexpressible horror—drew back the curtains and bent down and gazed upon her! Capitola, had no power to scream, to move, or to avert her gaze from those awful eyes that met her own, until at length, as the spectral head bent lower, she felt the pressure of 'a pair of icy lips upon her brow, and closed her eyes! When she opened them again the vision had de- parted and the room wa.s dark and quiet. There was no more sleep for Capitola. She heard the clock strike four, and was pleased to find that it was so near day. Still the time seemed very long to her who lay there wondering, conjecturing and speculating on the strange adventure of the night. When the sun arose, she left her restless bed, bathed her excited head, and proceeded to dress herself. When she had finished ber toilet, with the exception of putting on her trinkets, she sud- denly missed a ring that she prized more than she did all her possessions put together—it was a plain gold band, bearing on the inner side the inscription —Capirda -- Eugenie and which she had been enjoined by her old nurse never to part from but with life. She had, in her days of destitution, suffered the extremes of cold and hunger—had been upon the very brink of death from starvation or freezing, but without ever dreaming of sacri- ficing her ring. And now for the first time it was missing. While she was still anxiously looking for the lost jewel the door opened, and Dorcas Knight entered the room bearing on her arm Capitola's riding-dress, which had been well dried and ironed. "Miss Capitola, here is your habit; you had better put it on at once, as I have ordered break- fast an hour sooner than usual, so that you may have an early.start." " Up.,n my Word, you are very anxious, to get rid of me; but not more so than lam to depart !" said Capitola, sti 1 pursuing her search. "Your friesds, who do not know where you are, must be very uneasy about you. But what are you looking for r' "A ring—a plain gold circle with my name and that of another inscribed on it, and which I would not lose for the world. I hung it on a pin, on this pin-cushion, last night before 1 went to bed—l would swear I did! and now it is missing," answered Cap, still pursuing her search. • "If you lot it in this room,lt will certainly be . found," • said Dorcas Knight, putting down the he it and helping in the search. "I am not so stirs of that. There was some one in my room last night." "Sonic one in your room r exclaimed Dorcas in dismay. " Yes ; a dark-haired woman; all dressed in Dorcas Knight gave two or three angry grunts, and then harshly exclaimed : "Nonsense ! Woman, indeed f There is no such woman about the house I There are no females here except Al iss Day, myself, and you—not even a wait ing-inaid or cook.-</p>
        <p>" Well," said Cap, "if it was not a woman, it was a ghost, for I was wide awake • and I saw it with my own eyes." " Fudge : you've heard that foolish story of the haunted room, and you have dreamed the whole thing." "1 tell you I didn't. I saw it. Don't I know?"</p>
        <p>" I say you dreamed it There is no such living woman here • and as for a ghost, that is all folly.!ittla I nius fiev, Miss 2.lack,. that you will not distress Miss Day by telling her this strange dreani of yours. She has never heard the ridiculous story of the haunted room, and as she lives here in soli- tude, I would not like her to hear of it." "Oh, I will say nothing to disquiet Miss Day. But it was no dream. It was rat, if there is any reality in this world." There was no more said. They continued to look for the ring, but in vain. Dorcas Knight, however, assured her guest that it should be found and re- turned, and that—breakfast waited. Whereupon Capitola went down to ;the parlour, where she found Clara awaiting her presence to give her a kindly greeting. "Mr Le Noir never gets up until very late, and so we do not wait for him," said Dorcas Knight, as she took her seat at the head of the table, and signed to the young girls to gather around it. After breakfast, Capito!a, promising to come again soon, and inviting Clara to return her visit, took leave .of her entertainers and set out for home. "Thank Heaven I have got her off in time and safety ?," muttered Dorcas Knight, in triumph. (To be continued.)</p>
        <p>FITS OR EPILEPSY, GIDDINESS, SENSATIONS, RUNTS.</p>
        <p>I will demonstrate t&gt; the whole world how that most dreadful of all c&gt;mplaints, " Epilepsy," which has been hitherto considered incurable, can be permanently cured (without the chance of failure). Write to the " SECRETARY," Burwood House, Burwood Place, Hyde Park, London. He willsend you, "Gratis," full instructions for cure.</p>
