      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>" CAMAL" ORAL</head>
        <p>" CAMAL" ORAL</p>
        <p>The most remarkable disclosure with regard to South Africa in the House of Commons on Monday, was the acknowledgement by Sir Hercules Robin- son that he had not the slightest knowledge or suspicion of what was going on until he heard of Jame-ma's invasion on the day after it took place. In Sir Hercules's own graphic language it came upon him like a bolt from the blue. Of course this is a perfectly accurate statement of the fact, but it suggests two inquirie3 first., how it happened that he was in such a state of dense ignorance about the imminence of the insurrection atJohannesburgyand, fartber, whether Sir Jacobus Da Wet or his repre- sentative at Pretoria was in a state of equally dense ignorance. Sir Hercules was far from the sphere the conspiracy. Sir Jacobus was almost on the spot, and his short absence of a few -days from Preto'ria will mot suffice to account for a grave situ- ation developing itself without his knowlPdge. The High Commissioner was entitled to trust to Sir Jacobus for information, and the silence of the latter is some justification for his ignorance.; but the ignorance of Sir Jacobus is 'unaccountable in the extreme.</p>
        <p>The opposition to the raising of the age of half- timers to twelv-3,as proposed by the Education Bill, comes from the•ropresentatives both of agriculture and of the textile industries. An amendment will be moved against the claufe, and it will receive support from a section of members on both sides of the House. It is one of the points on which f?.ir John 6-orst is least likely to give way. and, as he can resist the proposal wittiout any fear of defeat he has every encouragement to give effect to a view which he has long entertained. The bulk of the Liberal party will support 'him in tke, lobby. Mr. tutherford, who represents the Darwen Division of Lancashire, will second an amendment moved by an agricultural member, but he regards the rais- ing of the age as so hurtful to the textile industry that he 'would have been prepared to move an amendment himself if the agricultural Conserva- tives had lacked the courage to do so.</p>
        <p>hearthat Mr. Chamberiain exyressPs his views, or some Of them. in regard to the crisis in South Africapwith considetabie frankn,ss in society. He believes, he says,that the effects,of the shock given to confldeuce and the sense of security in different pats -sf South Africa by the Jameson raid and the conspiracy at Johannesburg will not pass away for a longtime, but he thinks that by consistently pur- suing a policy of good faith and regard for the rights and feelings ot the independent Republics much be done. at is mainly, however, to the heating effects of time that he look § for the restora- tion of good feeling and confidence, He this no more respect for the )financial conspirators than have other right-thinking men, and says so; and be will certainly exhibit no special tenderness for them. The folly and ineptitude of the whole of their proceedings have disgusted him.</p>
        <p>The followers of the late Mr. Spurgeon have been thrown into a farther state of commotion by the relinquishment, by 'Dr. 'James Spurgeon, of Croy- 1 don, brother of the deceased pastor, of the presi- dency of the Pastor' College, which he has held since his brothefs death This college, it may not be generally known outside the Baptist body, is a training institution for young men who are desir- ous of joining the Baptist ministry, and it was founded 40 years ago by Mr. Spurgeon, who always regarded it as second in importance only to his ministerial work. The present resignation has been caused by friCtion between the president and the trustees. So much is announced by Dr. Spurgeon himself, but thocause of the friction,though it may be.-guessed at, is not deeanitely known. It has its roots in the old,quarrel which took place when Thomas, the son of the deceased Mr. Spurgeon, was appointed to succeed his lather in the Metropolitan Tabernacle. At that• time the congregation was almost on the point of-splitting up into two see- ti on s, one of which wanted to perpetuate the name of Spurgeonin connection with the Tabernacleiand was, therefore, in favour of the appointment of the deceased pastoi's son, and the other, who had at- tached themselves to-the Presbyterian, Dr. Pierson, who had temporarily discharged the duties of pas- tor. These differences bode ill for the permanent cohesion of the large body of devoted followers whom the late Mr. Spurgeon left behind him.</p>
        <p>'The Parliamentary golf tournament has on Tues- day narrowed itself down to a point in which Lan- cashire players will have a special interest. The matches that were played off on Tuesday afternoon at 'Furzedown leave only two members of the Howe is the competition. and both of them are Lancasllire representatives. Mr. -Seton-Kerr and Mr. T. W. Leh have got into the semi-final. The luck of the hal:tit now brings the 'two members together, and they wil have to fight it out for the honour of play- ing in thellnal, in which the survivor will encoun- ter a representative of the House of Lords, in the person of 'Mr 'Liddell,•who is playing a good game with a long 'handicap.</p>
        <p>Within-the last few days Northumberland-avenue which is adjacent to Charing Cross, has been active with the usual signs that the coaching season has returned. It is the headquarters and starting place of that 'fine array of idur-horse vehicles which have conveyed daily, for some years past, immense numbers of visitors to the metropolis to some of the most delightful suburban places. For the most part they are driven by noblemen or gentlemen fond of the-reins, who seek in, this form of exercise relief from the monotony of idleness and ennui. The front of the two great hotels which almost monopolise one complete side of the Avenue is taken up with-rows of tine turn-outs every morning and the air is then blatant with the sound of the horn. Piccadilly also, which is another starting point, suffers a similar fate. Tnere is a slight de- cline in the 'number of the-coaches this season ; but the public have a choice of routes lying within an arc of a circle stretching from Brighton, by way of Windsor and Maidenhead, to Epping Forest.</p>
        <p>It is said that the total number of the cipher telegrams of whiCh the 'Boers obtained possession was sometbing like forty °l—fifty. Thirteen or four- teen of these have been published, so that there are plenty more available if 'President Kruger should think it desirable to continue his disclosures. Probably the most sensational as affecting the actual raid have seen the light already. it does not follow, however, that the others may afford inter- esting reading. especially if they let in light on the dark places of the general policy which led up to Jameson's adventure. The raid came like a bolt from the blue to the English publk. but in South Africa the possibility of a rising at Johannesburg and an advance into the Transvaal from the outside had been eagerly di3cussed for months beforehand. It is not at all improbable that the despatches which proved such a treasure-trove to the Boer Government may go back a very long way indeed.</p>
        <p>Miss May E. Abraham, whose engagement to Mr. H. J. rennant, M.P., is announced, has, though young, already had an interesting career. Miss Abraham is the daughter of an Irish physician,and came to London a few years ago as secretary to Lady • ilke. While acting in this capacity she ob- tained some insight into the conditions of women's labour through Lady Mike's condition with the Women's Trade Union League. Her experience was utilised when the Labour Commission was formed, and Miss Abraham was appointed one of the lady assistant commissioners. Travelling all over Eng- land,as well as in Wales and Ireland, her experience was still further enlarged, and at the 'clo,e of the Labour Commission's work Mi,s Abraham hart the honour of being appointed the first woman factory inspector. Since that date four other ladies have been appointed, and Miss Abraham has been pro meted to the rank of superintending inspector. She hag recently served on the Departmental Commit- tee on Dangerous Trades, of which Mr.Tcnnant was also a m3mber. A career of such public usefulness as Miss Abraham's may be pointed to as one of the most satisfactory results of the much-abused "women's emancipation" movement.</p>
      </div>
