      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>INTERESTING REMINISCENCES OF A CRIMEAN HERO.</head>
        <p>INTERESTING REMINISCENCES OF A CRIMEAN HERO.</p>
        <p>The " klorset Chronicle" publishes an intensely 'in- teresting interview with Mr. F. R. Everett, a veteran of the 'Crimea. Mr. Everett has had an adventrarous life, and although in his sixtieth year, and with hair turming silvery, is well and hearty, his ruddy cheeks teKing of an open-air life and good health. " Yes," said Mrs. Everett, in response to al compli- liment on her husband's looks, "except that my hus- band gets bronchitis and asthma in the wister, he is very well."</p>
        <p>"I was invalided out of the Royal Navy," joined in Mr. Everett, " where I served twenty-gix years, and when I left I was senior mate in the coastguard cruisers. I served on the " Hannibal " and the " Sans- pareil." My breath trouble was first caused by the legs. I was subsequently stationed at Portland. but</p>
        <p>I WAS PROMOTED, and sent to the North Sea, as senior mate to the "Hawk." My cabin, being close to We engines %reshot and when I went up on the bridge ix. the heavy snows, it was going from one extre ne to another. That Enished me up. I was invalided out of the tervice, and bought this villa."</p>
        <p>Mr. Everett added that for the severe recurrent attacks of his winter complaints he had tried &amp; number of remedies, but they hall done him little or no good. "And when he has had his cough," Mrs. Everett said, "I have thought that his breath would be gone entirely."</p>
        <p>11-E 'COULD 'NOFT STAND.</p>
        <p>It has frightened me very much, and of course we are glad to find any remedy. 'I had seen accounts of the great cures effected by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, in the "Dorset Chronicle," and so said, ' We'll try them.' That was just after Christmas. I took them too, for rheumatism, I had bad rheumatism dreadfully in my right leg, and at night the pain was most excru- ciating. I could scarcely 'walk across the room ; but all that is gone, thanks to the Pills."</p>
        <p>Here Mr. Everett took up the thread of the story. " And Loan go up and down the hill now ; but before I took the/11181 had to keep on stopping,and used to puff and blow like a billy goat. My sister-in-law has been taking them too for erysipelas." 4' (es," said Mrs. Everett, "She had a bad eye for two years and tried almost everything. She used eye ointment,but it did not seem to do any good. Then she tried Dr.Williams''Pills. Her eye before was very much! interned all reund owing to poorness of blood, but now, the inflammation is gone, and the eye well. VI, e recom- mended them to some friends of ours,and they have done them a lot of good." Asked if a long course of the Pitts had been taken,Mrs.: Everett.answered that they all found benefit before they i had finished the ffirst box. "And you must bear in ' mind;" she continuei, " that the first box was divided between three of us." The interviewer took his leave.. It is not only in cases like those described above how- ever, that Dr. 'Williams' Pills are so valuable. They have cured paralysis, lecomotor ataxy, rheumatismiand sciatica.; also all diseases arising from impoverishment, and vitiated humours of the blood,which cause scrofula, rickets, chronic erysipelas, consumption of the bowels and lungs, ansemia, pale and sallow complexion, general muscular weakness, loss of appetite, palpitation, pains in the back, nervous headathe, early decay, all forms of female weakness, and hysteria. These Pills are not a I purgative. They are genuine only with the full Aftene, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, and are sold by chemists and by Dr. Williams' Medicine Company., 146, Holborn-viaduct, London,,E.C., at 2s. 9d. a box, or six for 1.35. 9d. They are unrivalled as a tonic for both sexes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>WARRINGTON TOWN COUN-DIL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN COUN-DIL.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Council was held in the Coun- cil Chamber, at the Town Hall on Tuesday evening. The Mayor (Councillor Fairclough) presided, and ,-,here were also present,Aldermen Greenall, Wright, Hutchinson, Harrison, Sutton ;;Councillors Furness, 'ShiliCOOk, Evans, Duncan, Garner, Smethurmt,Loug- ton, Monks, Roberts, Shaw-Green, Heaton, Plump- ton, Baird, Lee, Bennett, Ellison, Rogers, Tinniou, Woods, Skelton, Shaw, Hepherd,Wilkinson, Cannel', Messrs. .J. Lyon Whittle (town clerk), S. Wilson (deputy town clerk),'Fairhurst (borough surveyor), J. Deas (wmer engineer), T. Longdis (borough surveyor.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>7flE,LAlBir MR. BRAMAN.</head>
        <p>7flE,LAlBir MR. BRAMAN.</p>
        <p>The Metros read a reply from Mrs. Beamar in reply to the vote of condolence passed by the Council at its ,previ- ous meeting in reference to the death of Mr. 8.4. Bea- man. Mrs. Beaman on behalf of her family and herself expressed thanks to the Council for its expression of sympathy which had been highly appreciated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>COBLIILIVENS MINUTES.</head>
        <p>COBLIILIVENS MINUTES.</p>
        <p>The minutes of the following Committees as printed in last week's " Examiner" were adopted without dis- CUSSioll::—Watch Committee, Finance and Estates Cm. mittee, Street Improvement and Lighting Committee, Health Committee.</p>
        <p>SANITIRY WORKS COMMITTEE.-TEIE PROPOSED EXPENDITURE OF £16,000.</p>
        <p>The principal item in the minutes of the Banitany Works Committee was the proposal to adopt the report of Mr.Deas with reference to tile extensions of Longford depit and the previsionscof a central depot at the Gas; works in Mersey-at. The total expenditure upon these improvements and extensions was estimated to be : £18,439, which was made up of the following sums . Longford new boundary wall, store, offices and mess .1 room. £7,51,7.; engine, pulleys, shafting, brackets, tve,, £1,140; chimney, flue. economiser, £1,294 ; destructor,' bniler, and roof, R. 1,500.; exhauat fan and connection, £2OO ;,discharge pipes, £135; contingencies, £.375 destructor and boilertnesv roadway, staging. &amp;c., expen- diture already incurred,23:94B ; total, £11.909. Central depot : stables, loft, and middenstead, £180: air engine and house.Cs2o ; destructor,approach road, and tipping platform,£B2s wheelwright, cooper, and smith's shop, £150; chimney and flues, £800; engine and dynamo h0u55,E1,475; mess and wash house,2llo; contingencies £200; t0ta1,4,290. Intercepting depot : howley mess and wash rooms.£110; Hardc-,treet wash rooms, £110; contingencies, £2O; total, £240. Councillor ?awns, in moving the adoption of the minutes of the Committee, explained at aome length the proposed improvements with the aid of a lantern and screen. He said that in that scheme of expansion the Committee was not embarking on any new line. They were simply proceeding on the lines on which they had been working during the last three or four years,because they felt thdt the Council as a whole bad confidence in the work now being done. It was perhaps hardly neces- sary to remind the Council of the circumstances under which the present Committee taoir charge of the opera- tions at Longford. To say the least they were anything but Intl factory. That was at the end of 1892. and it was not till last year, after the lapse of something like two and a half years, that they vents:ad to make a de- finite proposal of any kind whatever. It would there- fore be seen that the Committee bad not acted hastily, and they could not be charged with putting forward that scheme without the fullest consideration. The Committee had considered not only the present defici- ency of the plants which was incapable of dealing satis- factorily with the whole of the material they now had, hut also lylsoamthpeprobable requirements of the next ten or fifteen Years. The plant included in their present scheme provide for all the requirements of the town for fifteen years at least. Their difficulties during the last three sears had been great, and were not yet , over. On behalf of the Committee he would gratefully acknowledge the assistance which the Council had given them during the whole of that time, and he ventured to hope that they would show the same friendly feeling in considering the present scheme. Mr.Mnniss then entered into a detailed explanation of the whole scheme. In referring to the need of a boundary wall at Longfordshe said they had reason to believe that some malicious dis- posed person had been trying to interfere with the work- ing of the depot. They had found pieces of iron placed in such a position that they could come to no other con - elusion than that they had been placed to damage the machinery. The deputation which had attended from the Rural Sanitary Authority had also referred to the untidy and unkempt appearance of the depottand urged that they should put un a wall. With regard to the proposed electric plant, the only capital outlay would be to provide the necessary engine and dynamo. After the inrtallatine it would not be neces- sary to keep a skilled man. Their present gas bill was about £9O a year and he thought that by lighting with electricity they should save at least half of that if not 75 per cent. of it. They might also apply electricity to drive some of the maehinery, and if they erected a central depot in town it would only he necessary to take sufficient fuel to Longford to raise the necessary steam. With respect to placing a destructor in Mersey-street. he had no hesitation in saying that one of the new Bea-</p>
        <p>To MOTIIERS.—Mrs, Winslow's Soothing Syrup has been used over fifty years by millions of mothers for theirehildren while teething with perfect success. It will relieve the poor sufferer immediately. It is pleasant to taste; it• produces natural quiet sleep, by relieving the child from pain, and the little cherub awakes "as bright As a button." Of all chemists, Is. ljd. per bottle.—Advt.</p>
        <p>man and Deas' destructors ,might be placed in any neighbOurhood, however crowded,without any nuiSince whatever being caused. They could not,he added, have amore centralplaceethau Mersey-street and save cart- age. The MAYOR : And perhaps you will supply the town with electricity at the same time.</p>
        <p>Councillor MONKS said that would depend upon the quantity 'Of ashes they had to burn. This material had a c9nsiderable calorific value and it seemed a waste of money to burn them simply for the sake of getting rid of them,vvhen they might be used for creating power thatto Id be turned to various uses. It was proposed to grind up the clinkers from the furnaces and make them into mortar which had been proved to be particu- larly good. He might say that there was one building which he should particularly like to see lighted with electricity and that was the institution in Bold-street. The bindings of books were peculiarly subject to damage when kept where gas was burned, and the art gallery could not be opened in gas light because of the condi• tions which were imposed in connection with some of the loans. These restrictions would be removed if the elec- tric light were used. In conclusion, Mr. Monks added that the proposed expansions would b su:ficient for the next fifteen years. The expansion at Mersey-street de- pended,of courst,upon the time that the Gas Committee placed the premises at their disposal, and he understood that that would not be under two or three years. Councillor EveNs seconded the adoption of the minutes. _ . _</p>
        <p>Council.or ROBERTS asked whether in case the Council sanctioned that scheme in whole it would afterwards be necessary to submit it in detail when the work was to be carried out. Councillor MONKS said his idea was that it was better to submit the whole scheme at once rather than come with sections of it during the next two or three years. At the same time the tenders of each part of the work would have to be submitted to the Council.</p>
        <p>Councillor LONGTON said the proposal to adopt the whole of the report had come as a surprise,-and he sug- gested that they should be given more time to consider it. On,-rnany points the Committee had not themselves made op their minds. The MAYOR did not see how the Committee could work the scheme out unless they were given a free hand. If they had to come to the Council whenever they wanted to erect a wooden shed they would never do anything. He further pointed out that the matter had not been sprung upon the Council as Councillor Monks had told them time after time what they intended to spend. Alderman WRIGHT said that was the -first intimation he had had of it,and he was not going to vote in its favour until he had further information. 'Councillor LONGTOW 'moved that the consideration of the -scheme be Postponed till the next meeting of the I Council. Alderman SUTTON seconded this proposal,c nd suggested that they should have a special meeting at Longford. He rattler objected to what had been said by Councillor Monks about the state of things at Longford not being s/tisfactory when the present Sanitary Works Commit- tee was appointed three years ago. The Maxon pointed out that at the Council meeting on December 3rd it was resolved that the Town Clerk sheuld apply to the/Local Government Board and borrow .£15,000 for extensions at Longford depot. Alderman SIITTeti said he was in favour of the whole thing being considered at once, but he wanted further information. But, referring again to the old Sanitary Committee he thonght some credit at all events should be given to them for the work they did, as the present Sanitary Committee had benefitted very greatly by the experience and labours of the prievious Committee. The present committee had no doubt made vast improve- ments but previous to their existence everyone who came to Warrington, admited that Longford was the best thing that had been carried out anywhere. (Hear hear, and laughter.) Councillor Pruaterox : But nobody followed your ex- ample. (Laughter.) He also asked if they were going to produce electric light, what would become of the Gas Works. Alderman Weardnr said that three or four days were not sufficient to form a judgment upon a scheme costing 1216,000. The MAYOR said the Town Clerk informed him that al- -1 I ready some £4f,800 had been spent with the sanction of the Conceit. Councillor Straw GREEN also pointed out , that as the work was required tne Committee would come to the Council with;the different tenders. -</p>
        <p>Councillor WOODS said if that was the ease be would support the Cimmittee. Alderman HUTCHINSON remarked that they had al- ready spent some £40,000 on Longford, and he did not want them to go too far. He added that crude material will sent from Manchester and Salford into the neigh- bourhood of Warrinvton.</p>
        <p>CouncilloriacmEnTs : And abominable material it is. The MAYOR. (to Alderman Butchinsoa) Then you want to go back to the old system. Councillor BENNETT supported the Committee's scheme as a whole, with the exception of one portion. That por- tion was with reference to Mersey-st and he should ob- ject to any nuisance being created there. The Mrrou said there would be no nuisance there, as they did not intend to manufacture there. Be thought 'Councillor Bennett was wasting the time of the Council. Councillor BENNETT : You have told me that before, and with are3pect to you. Mr. Mayor, 1 contend that I am not wasting the time of the Council. a don't think it is quite courteous to me to say so. I have my consti- tuents to represent. The Tows z CLERK: Chair, chair.</p>
        <p>Councillor BENNETT : lam willing to obey the ruling of the chairas much as anyone. At the same time, I claim mv right to speak, as long as I do so courteously.</p>
        <p>The 111,ayea : You are wasting the time of the Council if you say making mortar and flags is a nuisance. Councillor BENNETT said he would oppose that portion .of the scheme it there was any probability ofa nuisance being created. the _ _</p>
        <p>Co-uncillor SMETHURST supported the motion for the adjournment of the discussion, and contended that it Council should not come to a decision upon it until a had been more carefully ccnsidered. This had been ed very extravagant committee,and someone was requir tr, to put a stop on the wheels. Whenever he ventured - offer a criticism upon the matter in committee., he wa told by a certain gentlemen, " We know ail about it.' ,(Laughter.-) Councillor ROBERTS : May we not have the great feli- city of knowing who this objectionable gentleman is? ,(Laughter.) Councillor ,-I&amp;KTIEURST added that they had -no -sooner ,built a place at the depot than it was pulled down again and the present instance would be the third time a chitn- luey had been built for Longford. j Couneillor aonEcTs ridiculed the suggestion of Alder- man Hutchinson that they should go back to the old sys- tem. Warrington, he said, was committed for good or awil to the pail closet system, and the report now under discussion simply provided for the further accommoda- tion that was necessary. They could not do any harm 'by accepting that report as every one of the proposals in it -.were open to discussion when they again came for- ward for practical application. (Councillor TaysuoN said there was not the slightest doubt that they should have to do something at Long- ford. He was in favour of further consideration although he believed they would come to the conclusion that the present scheme was the best one. Councillor Morisco preferred that the vote should not be taken at that meeting, and his reason for not rising earlier was because he wished to have the matter venti- lated by a discussion. It was at first taken into con- sideration whether a special meeting should be called, or whether the matter should be discussed at the ordinary meeting, but the Town Clerk advised that there was little else to be done that evening, and that ample time would be afforded for a full and free discussion of the subject. That was why the matter had been discussed that night, instead of at a special meeting. The MAYOR And we shall have it all again. (Laugh- ter.) Councillor MONKS said it was also under the considera- tion of the committee whether the Council should be invited to Longford. The committee would be pleased to receive the Council there, and they would be in a better position to appreciate a description given on the ' spot after the details he had put before them tbat night. (Hear, hear.) The MAYOR: Would it not be better to take only those gentlemen who object? (Laughter.) Councillor MONKS: We can only give the invitation, end if those who are satisfied don't think it necessary to come we shall not feel offended. Alderman SUTTON : Give them a lunch there. (Laughter.) Councillor Memo said the noise of the machinery made it impossible for oneself to be properly heard and understood at Longford, and he therefore suggested Saturday afternoon as a suitable time. Alderman WRIGHT: Cannot Sunday be selected? (Laughter.) Councillor MONKS Yes. I will go on Sunday if it meets the wishes of the majority. (Laughter.) Con- tinuing, Councillor Mcnk said he had no wish to de- preciate the labours of the old committee, but not with- standing what had been said about the pulling down and altering they had managed to reduce the cost of working the department from 4d. in the £to Id. (Ap- plause.) The MAYOR : I should like to mention that our place has been gutted five or six times during the last 12 years, and that is more than has been done at Longford. Councillor Ronnars : So it is with every progressive place.</p>
        <p>CARTER'S LITTLE I ER PILLS. PILLS. 111 Pill. BEAU fIFUL TEETH all Dom for oil who use daily on the tooth brice. I brush a few drops ot SOZODONT. the pleasantest dentifrice in the ,y Vegn-; world. able. Cleanses the teeth and spaces Can. ~rpid Liver, Bile, between them as nothing else Sallow Complexion, and will. Sound and pearly white Sick Headaches promptly :1 teeth, rosy lips, and fragrant and cure them so as to stay breath ensured. awed Chemists. a lid. Ask for SOZODONT. cu</p>
        <p>Counci bifoNics corroborated, saying that in con- nection with his firm's works they had altered one Portion no fewer than five times during the past fifteen years. Every manufacturing concern was constantly making, alterations, and the Sanitary Works Committee were fully pistified in all they had done in this respect. (Elear, hear.) After some further discussion the minutes were con- firmed with the exception of the one relating to Mr. Deas' report, and it was decided that a special meeting be called for that evening fortnight, at seven o'clock, to further consider it.</p>
        <p>PAVING AND SEWERAGE COMMITTEE.-THE STATE OF THE ROADS.</p>
        <p>Alderman WRIGHT proposed the adoption of the minutes of this committee. Councillor HEkrots seconded. Councillor WOODS asked the Chairman of the commit- tee if he could make a statement on the condition of Padgate-lane and the foot-path adjoining? Councillor ROBERTS asked the Chairman if he could get the committee to apply their energies to the macad- amised portion of Knutsford and Wilderspool-roads, which, for cyclists, were past description. Councillor REPIIERD also referred to the condition of Wilderspool-road and inquired what had become of the steam roller. Ile had not seen it for a long time. Alderman Whtorrr said that so far as Padgate-lane was concerned, the members of the committee had been ever it that day, and he, personally, thought there was something in the complaints that had been made with regard to its condition. If the inhabitants of the dis- trict would only exercise a little patience for a few weeks he thought they would be fully satisfied by the action of the committee. As to Wilderspool-road and other roads, the committee were making all the progress they possibly could. They quite recognized the state of the roads mentioned,and were endeavouring to put a good surface on them. Wear, hear.)</p>
        <p>MUSEUM, LIBRARY, ARTS AND TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION</p>
        <p>COMMITTEE.-THE PROPOSED EXHIBITION.</p>
        <p>The minutes of this committee stated that it had been resolved that a deputation be sent to London with the object of obtaining the support of Mr. Fades, R.A., and Mr. 11. Woods, R.A.,to the projected eahibition of works of old students of the Schaol of Art.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS, in moving their adoption,said the deputation had beei4 courteously received by Mr. Ftldes and Mr. Woods who had promised their help in getting up the exhibition of pictures. The exhibition would he said, give an immense amount of pleasure if successfully carried out. The minutes were confirmed.</p>
        <p>Liv smJ CARASim••• ,maP • 1:: !TILE Forty i pc/ If.L s pure', • ta 'e Torpid. Liv,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>CEMETERY.</head>
        <p>THE CEMETERY.</p>
        <p>Alderman HARRISON proposed the confirmation of the minutes of the Cemetery Committee, and Councillor EVANS seconded.</p>
        <p>Councillor BENNETT asked whether the committee bad considered the suggestion to make an additional en- trance to the Cemetery, and if so what was their de- cision.</p>
        <p>COUTICMOT LONGTON would like to know from Alder- man Harrison whether he thought the proposed altera- tions to the lodges would satisfactorily meet the present defects.</p>
        <p>Councillor WOODS agreed with the alterations proposed to be made, and felt sure that if all the members of the Council were as much aware of the details as were the committee-men then they would not at all object to the spending of the money. The proposed alterations would remove the defects, and make the house habitable. Alderman HARRISON in reply to Councillor Bennett, said the committee had decided not to have an addi- tional entrance to the Cemetery at the point referred to. The minutes were pasEed. TOWN BALL, PARR AND BATHS COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the above committee a letter was read from Captain Appleton, on behalf of the Church Lads' Brigade, asking for special terms for the use of the large plunge bath, when it was resolved that members of the brigade, when in charge of one or more officers, be al- lowed the use of the bath between the hours of seven and nine on Wednesday evenings upon the following terms :—Over 16 3d.. boys under 16 2i. Councillor Dr. CANNELL proposed the acceptance of the minutes, and mentioned that the above arrangement had been entered into with a desire that the baths should increase in popularity, and to encourage swimming on the part of boys. Couneillor ROBERTS hoped the same facilities would be held out to all schoolmasters.</p>
        <p>The TOWN CLERK replied that they were. Scholars, when accompanied by a teacher, were admitted at id. each.</p>
        <p>Councillor PLIMPTON asked if a gate into the Park could not be made from Froghall-lane end.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR objected to these continued questionings, and said he intended to stop them unless proper notice in writing had been given of the intention to ask them. Councillor TflozioN said be bowed to the ruling of the Mayor, and consequently would limit his question to the shortest possible space. He would like to say that ke knew gentlemen connected with Sunday schools, who collected many boys together in classes, and who were desirous that the boys should visit the baths. Councillor Dr. CANNEL', in answering Councillor Plurnpton's question, said the committee did not intend making another entrance to the Park, as great difficulty was already experienced ir maintaining order therein owing to the conduct of certain persons. As to the question of Councillor Tinnion, he might say that the committee would make special terms with anyone super- intending a number of boys if application was made to the committee.</p>
        <p>These minutes were passed. THE RIVES COMIHTTEE.</p>
        <p>The minutes of the above committee contained the re- port of Mr. J.Wolfe Barry on the Manchester Ship Canal Bili,lB93,and the report of Mr. J. Carter Bell on three sainples of water which had been taken from the canal. A supplementary minute was also submitted recom- mending the Council to approve of the agree- ments with the Ship Canal Company, and that the common seal of the borough be affixed to them. Further, that after considering the reports of Mr. J. Wolfe Barry and Mr. Carter Bell on the probable effect upon the Manchester Ship Canal and the river by the closing of the tidal openings, it was resolved that the Council be ree.ornmemded to support the Manchester Ship Canal Bill, 18S6. on the conditions mentioned in Mr. Wolfe Barry's report. It was also resolved " That the Town Clerk be authorised to ret in counsel and call witnesses and do what he may consider necessary to protect the interests of the borough in committee of the House "</p>
        <p>The minutes were passed, on the motion of Councillor Roszetrs,seconded by Councillor EINATON.</p>
        <p>GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE.--THE EDUCATION BILL.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of this cirmmittee the following resolu- tions were passed :—" That having regard to the terms of the Education Bill as introduced into the House of Commons, this committee, without expressing any opinion on the 2eneral principles of the Bill, strongly re- commends that all possible steps be taken to place non- county boroughs on a similar footing to county boroughs., that a petition be presented to Parliament praying for the powers and privileges under the Educa- tion Bill of a county borough." The MATO,R moved, and Councillor HEATON seconded, the adoption of the minutes, which was agreed to, and the Council rose.</p>
        <p>On Saturday Hugh At‘Lachlan, spirit salesman, of South Spiers Wharf. Glasgow, in a fit of insanity, snatched up his two-year-old daughter and jumped into the canal. Both were drowned</p>
