      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>NOTES AND NEWS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>An exceedingly interesting article, cortaining good news for the manufacturers and artisans of Lancashire and for lady readers, appeared in Saturday's " It describes nothing less than the supersession of the silk- worm. That industrious and unfortunate little animal will now be able to decide in his own glutinous person whether it is better to be and suffer, or not to be at all. For in future silk is to be made by forcing wood-pulp through a series of glass tubes, each ending in a minute hole, the filaments which issue from these being spun together, de-nitrified, and woven into artificial " silk" stuffs, richer,more brilliant, and more susceptible to the dyer's art than natural silk itself. It is,indeed, asserted that only an expert can tell the artificial from the natu- ral products, and that even the shopkeepers who are already selling them in London and Paris have not real- ised the change that has been brought about. A factory, to cost £30,000, is to be built near Manchester, and six weaving firms have already arranged to take its total output, even before a single brick has been laid. The patents control the manufacture in this country, and therefore the importation of the new fabric will not be possible, and its manufacture will thus be a British in- dustry. And as over £17,000,000 sterling of manufac- tured and raw silk was imported into Great Britain last year, it is confidently believed that a large proportion of this will be superseded by the "glass silk-worm," dc. pressed Lancashire is justified in hoping that a piece of good luck has at last come her way.</p>
        <p>Perhaps the most celebrated pack of cards in the world, the '• Tarocchi di mantegna," came under the hammer at Messrs. Sotheby's on Saturday, with the other playing cards of the late Lady Charlotte Schreiber, and fetched £l2O. This pack is of great interest as a series of Italian engravings of the fifteenth century ; unfortunately this set is not quite complete, inasmuch as five cards are wanting, but they are represented by collotype facsimiles from the originals. The English series in this collection contained many of great interest, notably two curious historical packs, each illustrating the events of the reien of Queen Anne (which realised ten guineas and £8 103. respectively), and a very curious pack displaying the arms of the Scottish peers as they existed in the year 1691, and this realised £lO. The 277 lots brought a total of nearly £4OO.</p>
        <p>The profits of Mr. Cecil Rhodes during the last twelve months are estimated at little short of half a million. Mr. Beit,too, has bad a year that counts among his best. No serious hule will be made, therefore, in the fortunes of these two millionaires, if they bear between them,as it is believed they will, the entire cost of Dr. Jameson's raid,as well as the legal expenses of the trial in London and the indemnity to the Transvaal Govern- ment. These will be heavy enough, but they will nut mean roads to ruin for either party.</p>
        <p>Mr. Joseph Pulitzer.the proprietor of the New York " World," is again in London,where he has taken for the season the same charming house that he occupied last year. Mr. Pulitzer,it will be remembered,has expressed the opinion like Alphonse Daudet, that London is a very quiet city,and that the comparative silence of its streets is a rest to him after the noises of New York. During the past few months in America Mr. Pulitzer has lived in an extreme tension of work, as he virtually headed the" World "himself throughout the exciting times of the Venezuela dispute and the long, intense, and highly successful controversy which he waged with Mr. Cleve- land's Administration on the question of making the re- cent gold loan a popular one instead of handin6 it over bodily to a syndicate of New York bankers.</p>
        <p>The most striking portrait of President Kruger that we have seen appears in the current number of " Black and White," and is from the brush of our contempor- ary's special artist in South Africa. Mr. Charles M. Shel- don. The artist said that he noticed a curious similari y of temperament between President Kruger and Mr. Gladstone, and "I should like," he adds, " to have seen them together."</p>
        <p>In consequence of the publication of the annual report of a Sussex village sparrow club boasting that it had de- stroyed 5,922 sparrows and tomtits, besides a number of hawks and jays, in the course of twelve months, the matter has been brought under the consideration of the committee of the Society for the Protection of Birds,who have thought it desirable to put forth a strong protest. It is pointed out that hawks and jays are the natural enemies of sparrows, and that, if left alone, they will keep the numbers of small birds within bounds in a far more judicious and proper manner that will the members of sparrow clubs. It is admitted that in someplaces the sparrows require to be kept under but it is urged that this is more humanely, as well as most effectively, done by destroying the nests before the young are hatched. To exterminate them altegether has been found disas- trous whenever tried, as their presence is required to en- sure the consumption of caterpillars and grubs. Again, in placing a price on the heads of tits, the farmers are warring against their own interests, as these birds feed mainly on ir.sects and latvre. As regards hawks, their principal food consists of rats, mice, and voles, " whose stwages the farmers would be powerless to with- stand without the help of nature's winged cats—the hawks and owls."</p>
        <p>Society in St. Petersburg is considerably agitated ever a Court regulation recently issued by the Tsaritsa pro- hibiting her ladies and female servants from using to- bacco in or about the Palace. Those who have lived in Russia will understand and appreciate the importance of such a prohibition to women who are rarely without a cigarette between their lips.</p>
        <p>The Marquis of Tweeddale will be the first settler at Mayville, the new Anglo-French watering place near Boulogne. He hag already selected his" acre;" and the building of the Chilet Tweeddale will be commenced at once. Mr. F. C. Burnand follows suit. Before long Mayville will be as big and flourishing a place as is al- ready its neighbour Paris Page, which can accommo- date between 3,000 and 4,000 strangers in the tathing season, although it is only four years old. Mayville, so called in honour, it seems, of the Duchess of York, is situated on the sea shore and In the vicinty of fine pine- woods. The neighbourhood is singularly picturesque, and full of historical interest connected with the Anglo- Norman period of our history.</p>
        <p>M. Diamanti, ex-European secretary to the late Shah's Foreign Office, has given the" Daily Naws " Paris eor- reipondent some information as the riches amassed by Nasr-ed-Din, the late Shah of Persia. The Royal Palace is, he says, called the Ark. , The treasures heaped up in it are of incredible richness. One Rees there enamelled pictures on gold tablets of great size. The diamonds, rubies, and pearls are ( f unheard-of splendour, and in such quantities that one sees in the presses where they are kept European decanters and tumblers filled with them. The !ate Shah liked them to be in transparent vessels, an that he could rapidly see if the bulk was notably diminished by P. thief. He bad a big aquarium glass case filled with nothing hut carefully sorted pearls of the finest Orient lustre. Side by side with these treasures lay tawdry " articles de Paris," hardly good enranh for a charity bazaar. The enamelled painting on gold had between them cheap German chromolitho- graphs. The women's part of the Palace accommodated 800 souls. Of these 80 were concubines, The wives of Royal blood were not numerous. The one who bore the first son was the senior wife. There were about 700 servants, but a ef.at number were engaged in fine needlework, distilling east:teens, and coafectionery. They were very clean. However, all from highest to lowest ate the mutton and rice raeont with their fingers. They sat on their heels in circles round low tables on which the dish was set. The Shah ate alone. He often gave European dinners, at which he sat, but of which he never partook. He was a ferocious monarch if he had to find fault, but a constant friend, and patronised merit wherever he found it. The Sisters of Charitr were generously patronised by him. He allowed their convent at Teheran a yearly stipend. Doctor Tholosan for 35 years enjoyed the Shah's confidence. He never asked anything for himself, hut was forward to ask for ethers. The mosque where the monarch was murdered is connected by a light railway with Teheran. The open- ing of this railway caused a revolt. It encroached on the ground belonging to the mosque* This was thought sacrilegious. Religious fanaticism in this case had the last word.</p>
        <p>The show of pet and foreign does opened on Wednes- day at the London Aquarium surpas.ed all RI predeces- sors in the number and variety of the entries. There are said to be over a thousand animals on the benches, and the catalag.ue gives partirulars of more than six hundred,classed under 216 different headings. To pre- nounce an opinion as to the species which carried off the honours of popularity would he difficult Black and tan tots. poodles, Dachshunds and Slaves were more plentiful than ever,tor spaniels, pugs. and Pomeranians still holding their own notwithstanding. Three little griffons from Brussels, the property of the Queen of the Belgians, were a mething ef a novelty, resembling in many respects the small Yorkshire terrier. Another curiosity in the shane of a toy bulldog was decidedly attractive—a good-looking version,in fact, of the larger breed. The oddities included hairless dog... Japanese pugs,and eccentric poodles shaven and rincletted. The chows had a quiet corner in the organ gallery, which they shared with other foreigners, and were quite a numerous class, some splendid E-quimaux and Norwe- gian Elkhounds proving no janworthy neighbours. Of Sehipperkes there was a gathering grander even than that of last year, when the increasing popularity of this ingratiating species was very manifest.</p>
        <p>A Simla telegram states that Lieutenant Stevens, or the 20th Bengal Infan•ry, has died from a stab inflicted by a Shinwari tribesman at Peshawut Railway Station. It is stated that an important amalgamation is about to take place in the Durham coal trade which will re- sult in one of the largest combinations ever known. The parties to the agreement are said to include a north- country Liberal M.P., already an extensive edliery pro- prietor, and a peer prominently identified with Durham County.</p>
        <p>IrTHERE IS ONE BEVERAGE SUITABLE for at SEASONS of the YEAH, Amid Spring's ethereal mild ness, summer's long and sunny days, autumn's golden. stores, and especially winter'sunkindly blasts, a</p>
