        <p>A MARKET AUCTIONEER ASSAULTS THE POLICE.</p>
        <p>On Thursday morning last week at the Warrington Borough Police Court,heiore Alderman Sutton, Aldertua a Wright;and Mr. J. H. Fleming, George Blake, 166, Great Jackson-stree:,Manchcster,was charged with retusieg to produce hi 3 auctioneer's license to Thomas Ronal), an officer of the Inland Revenue, when requested to do so on Wednesday : he was further charged with as.sa,ulting P.C.'s Chrimes and Bell at the same time.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. My prosecuted on behalf of the Inland Revenue Authorities and Said the proceedings were taken under the Summary Jurisdiction Act. On Wednesday, the officer of the I.l{. was in the Market about 6 o'clock and there saw defendant carrying on a sale by aucgon. lie watched him for some rime and saw him sell a silver watch by auction. 'The Act relating to auctioneers required every auctioneer to exhibit a card in his room or premises or store with his name and address in legible charact,irs thereon and also required him to Produce the excise license, and a non-production of that license involved an imprisonment of one month without the aiternati ve of a line. Tile officer, Mr. Honan,went quietly up to defendant, and asked for his name and address. Mr. Blake asked who he was and toDk no further notice of him. Mr. HOtrui was obliged to ask him' for his auctioneer's license, and whether he had one sr not. There was no 1-eply or production of the license, and the officer then demanded £lO deposit on the spot in accord- ance with the Act. 114endant replied that he would not pay 10 farthings. The officer then gave him into cus- tody and he was detained at the Police Station as long as was necessary.</p>
        <p>Thomas Honan, Inland Reven cfficer, gave evidence in corroboration m this statement.</p>
        <p>P.C. Bell said he received defendant into custody from Mr. Ronan. The latter asked defendant in witness's presence for the license, and Blake refused to auswer- him, and wanted to go on selling. Witness then asked defendant himself but he still refused. Blake was, brought to the Police Station and LispeetorSpinks asked him several times for the license and he at length pro- duced one.</p>
        <p>Mr. May said the license was correct a 3 an auctioneer's license.</p>
        <p>The defendant said when he was asked for his license he asked finnan his authority, mad he did not show it. He (defendant) bad been asked before during the past few years,and the officer had always produced his autho- rity. As Ronan did not producl his authority as an oflicer,lie (defendant) did not produce his license. Alderman Sutton : Then it was a very stupid thing for you to do. Mr. May explained that it was not necessary under the Act for the officer to produce his authority at 3.11,and besides, the police-officer bad also told defendant that Mr. Honan was an exsise-officer.</p>
        <p>Defendant asked for an adjr,urnment, in order that he might get the assistance of a solicitor.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable said the circumstances of the second charge were that P.C.'s Bell and Chritnes receivcd defendant into custody from Mr. Honan. The man then became very violent and resiited his apprehension by every means in his power. „He struck at the officers„ and knocked one of them down. Oae of the r.flicers came to the Police Si;ation without his helmet and they had as much as they could do to control the defendant. At the Police Station he still contioued very violent and troublesome. They did not detain him longer than was necessary to secure his appearance that morning, and he' had then time enough to get legal assis- ance. When he left the Police Station nefandant endeavoured to create another disturbance, ani he the Chief Constable, had to threaten to bring him in on another charge before he went away. P.C.'s Chrimes and Bell corroborated.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Mary Broughton and Honry Clark, also supported the statements as to defendant's violence.</p>
        <p>rile defendant again asked for au adjOurnment. and said that if the case was decided at once he would go to a hl2her court.</p>
        <p>The Chairman of the Bench (Alderman Sutton), said there was no doubt that defenthz,t had acted wrongly and very stupiey indeed, in refusing to show his license and for that he would Ix; comtnited for one day, to cease with the rising of the Court. For the assault on the police he would be fined £2 and costs (L 2 123. in all). Defendant : You'll give rn3 time to pay it I sup- pose.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable: You must see me about that. Detendant (anzrily) ; You have done as much as you can now. You chnno:, do any mire.</p>
