      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <p>TRH new Education Bill has been The Education very much to the front dining the Bill. present week, both in and out of Parliament. Sir JOHN GORST meved the second reading of the Bill in the House of Commons on Tuesday, and tho opportunity was taken by Mr. ASQUITH,WhO spoke on behalf of the Liberal party, to move the rejection of the Bill,or rather, accord- ing to Parliamentary; form, that it" be read that day six months." It is, of course, practically cer- tain that the Bill will be carried by a large majority. The second reading will be voted for by probably the whole of the Unionist party, and it is said that the bulk of the Irish Nationalist members, it not the whole of them, will on this occasion be found in the same lobby as Mr. BAL- FOUR and Mr. CHAMBERLAIN. The Government's majority will not, therefore, fall far short of three hundred. Yet, as Mr. ASQUITH pointed out, this Bill will not settle the educa- tion question, even though it may be carried by one of the largest majorities on record. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN has accused the. Opposition of being moved by a partisan spirit, but it may with a good deal more justice be retorted that the framers of the Bill have given more consideration to narrow denominational interests than to the general welfare of the country. Brom the point of view taken by enlightened and far-seeing educationalists the Bill contains innumerable defects which will need to be remedied in a not distant future. Mr. ASQUITH, in the course of his very complete and searching criticism, exposed the weaknesses and absurdities of the Bill in a fashion which seemed t 3 surprise and to some extent dishearten the omnipotent majority which has resolved to pass the measure into law. There is reason to believe that the education policy of this Government is dictated by the PRIME MINISTER, and there can be no doubt at all that Lord SALISBURY'S predom- inant feeling is one of hostility to the School Board system, or indeed to any system which gives an effective public control of education. A year or so ago Lord SALISBURY advised all his clerical followers to endeavour to capture the School Boards, in the first place; by securing majorities on the Boards, and in the second place by getting a new law passed. The attempt to cap- ture the School Boards by the first of these methods has signally failed in the vast majority of instances. Since that cynical advice was given the ratepayers have shown at numerous local elec- tions that they were not dissatisfied with School Board methods of education and have defeated the attempts made to secure a clerical domina- tion. But Lord SALISBURY'S alternative plan is decidedly more promising. No one will pretend that the Unionist majority was obtained by the advocacy of such a Bill as that now before Parlia- ment, yet the majority is being used for the purpose of " capturing" or at all events of weaken- ing and humiliating the School Boards. How marked is the bias against School Boards—which, it should always be remembered, lead the way in education—may be seen by some of the financial proposals of the new Bill. The DUKE of DEVON- SHIRE, an important member of the Government, so lately as last November, declared that there should be an equality in the State aid given to voluntary and Board schools,yet under the present Bill, according to Mr. AsQUITII, while no less an amount than £489,000 will be handed over to the voluntary schools, not more than £53,000 will be allotted to Board schools. The "grotesque absurdity "of the distribution of the additional State grants is shown by instances quoted by Mr. ASQUITH. Birkenhead has been raising a school board rate of only twopence half- penny in the pound and is. 10s. per child in volun- tary subscriptions, while Brighton with about an equal population has been paying sevenpence half-penny in rates and us. Bd. per child in sub- I scriptions, yet under this unfair Bill Birkenhead will get £2,832 and Brighton only £1,262. There are other equally glaring examples of the injustice of the principles upon which the money is to be allotted,all of which go to show that the domin- ating desire of the framers of the Bill was that of crippling School Boards and endowing denomina- tion schools, rather than the improving of the ,educations.l syetera of the country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>TH E EXAMINER SATURDAY, MAY 9 1896</head>
        <p>TH E EXAMINER SATURDAY, MAY 9 1896</p>
        <p>THE publication of the now famous The Cypher Telegrams telegrams has made a wonderful change in the South African situa- tion. No one—not even the strongest supporters of Mr. RHODES and the Chartered Corn pany— suggests that these telegrams are not genuine documents, yet they completely upset the whole case that was being laboriously made out in de- fence of the celebtated Raid. There were attempts made to justify JAMESON'S invasion on two grounds. One was that the foreign residents in the Transvaal were so harshly treated by the Boer Government that they had determined, to rise in rebellion, and JAMESON was invited to help them to secure their political rights. The other was that J AMESON'S motives were almost purely philanthropic. It was said that affairs were so unsettled at Johannesburg, and there was such great danger of a native rising, that the Chartered Company's troops had been despatched in order to protect the women and children. The telegrams that were published towards the end of last week completely demolish both of the plausible and attractive theories. It now:seems to be clear that the primary object of the invasion was neither to help the Johannesburgers to secure their poli- tical rights nor to protect the women and children from the horrors of native outrage. JAMESON'S troops were really set in motion as the result of a' conspiracy amongst a small number of very wealthy South African financiers who evidently had ends of their own to serve. That, at all events, is the only conclusion to which impartial observers can come on the evidence that has now been placed before the public. So far from there being any revolutionary fervour amongst the general popu- lace of the " Golden City" one of the telegrams distinctly indicates that there was no popular feel- ing in favour of an outbreak. It is quite clear that the matter can not be allowed to remain where it now is. There must be further investigation of a searching character. Dr. JAMESON is on his trial at the present time for the part he took in the Raid,but if there be any truth in these telegrams he is certainly not the most guilty party, and the Government is now in a position to lay its hands upon those who pulled the strings. If Dr. JAMESON is to be prosecuted, MT. CECIL RHODES, Mr. BEIT, and one or two officials of the Chartered Company, cannot be allowed to g 3 free. The Government has undertaken to make a statement in Parliament without unnecessary delay, and the country will watch with interest the course they intend to pursue. It will soon be known whether because certain men are immensely wealthy and are backed by great social influence they are to be allowed to besmirch the reputation of this coun- try with impunity.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn School Board seem to be seriously actuated by a desire to spare the • pockets of the ratepayers. Their latest scheme, which, properly worked, should prove successful, has been to com- plete a scale fixing the payment of their teaching staff at a given minimum, rising by proportionate increases to a stipulated maximum. This should have the effect the Board intend it to have—the just settlement of the claims put in by the teachers for additional remuneration. From an outsider's point of view the scale seems equitable to the Board, ratepayers, and teachers, though the opinion of the latter body is not yet known.</p>
        <p>In Runcorn the success of the football team has been the occasion of a most exuberant outburst of local pride. While, perhaps,local fervour is being a trifle overdone, there are obvious grounds for ex- cuse. It is also cheering in these days when money rules players, and companies exhibit the game to supine devotees as a matter of business, with its prospects of interest on capital, to see a genuine team of townsmen playing for the pleasure of the game and the honour of their native town.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the School Board was held in the Runelra Town Hall on Tuesday evening. Present: Mr. J. Pritchard (in the chair). the Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, Mr. S. Taylor, J.P., C.C., Mr. F. Norman. LP., and Mr. G. Moss (clerk). SCHOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The minutes Of the two meetings of this committee showed that 62 parents were summoned of whom 55 ap- peared and it was decided to take proceedings against five parents. The minutes of the committee were con- firmed on the motion of Dr. Gastaldi, seconded by Mr. Taylor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>TRINITY Cuuncri, Ruscons. Last Sunday two sermons were preached in this church, in the morning by the Rev. W. Shaw, of Gars- ton, and in the evening by the Rev. J. Fletcher, of Widnes. There were good congregations. The choir rendered special music, and collections were taken on behalf of the curates' fund. PRIMITIVE METHODIST SUNDAY SCHOOL ANNIVERSARY. The anniversary services in connection with the Primi- tive Methoilist Sunday school will be preached on Sun- day next. Two sermons will be preached by the Rev. W. E. Lead,of Widnes, and in the afternoon the Rev. C. D. Putron Gliddon.of Halton, will occupy the pulpit. RUNCORN CYCLING CLUB. The members of the Saturday section of the club turned out on Saturday and enjoyed a pleasant run to Cranage. The weather was perfectly suited to cycling and the roads were in excellent condition. The Wednes- day section had no fixed run, though a number made Chester : their headquarters and enjoyed a fine after- noon's racing. SUCCESS OF A RUNCORN STUDENT. Mr. Henry Nugent Linaker (eldest son of the late Mr. W. H. Linaker, solicitor of Runcorn) has been successful in passing the solicitors' final examination which was held at the Law Society's Hall in London last month.and will in the course of a few weeks be duly admitted as a solicitor of the Supreme Court. Mr. H. N. Linaker served his articles with his uncle, Mr. Arthur Linaker, solicitor, of this town. MOSS-STRRET WESLEYAN SUND LT SCHOOL, RUNCORN. In connection with the above Mr. T. Simister,of Run- corn, preached two special sermons on Sunday. In the afternoon a service of song was given by the cho;r and scholars, conducted by Mr. R. Brierley. Master J. Bras- sington accompanied. The chair was occupied by Mr. T. Dodd, and the connective readings were given by Councillor G.Davies. The collections were in aid of the school funds. SERIOUS ACCIDENT AT RUNCORN LAY-BYE. Between eight and nine o'clock on Monday morning, Edward joden, of Crescent Cottage, met with a serious accident at the Runcorn lay-bye. He was engaged in dis- charging grain from the vessel " Westgate," of Liver- pool, when one of the sacks fell frcin the upper decks upon him striking him on the neck and rendering him unconscious. He was removed in the ambulance carri- age to his home. and Dr. McKenzie and Dr. Robinson's assistant attended him, and found that the neck was slightly dislocated and the spine injured. For NAME BROOCHES, solid silver name brooches 100, (any name) Government stamped. Presents or Articles for yourself, you may visit DBIIMIT'S, 10, CHURCH-STRIM. LIVERPOOL, where you can get hundreds of articles such as Watches, Clocks, Jewellery. Electro-Plate, Cutlery. Purses, Pocket Knives, etc.. at Iwholesale prices. See advertisement (Illustrated Chron- graph watch. 6s. 11d.. etc.), on page 'Lf this Paper. •Advt</p>
        <p>SINGULAR BIGAMY CASE AT RUNCORN. At Liverpool Assizes, on Thursday, Harriet Annie Ellis (23), domestic servant, was found guilty of having con- tracted a bigamous marriage with Charles Lewis Geddei at Runcorn, in 1864. The first husband enlisted as a sold,er under an assumed name, and went abroad. The prisoner afterwards married Geddes, and lived with him for some time. Recently, she was charged with having stolrei property belonging to the second husband, and it was then discovered that the real husband had come back, and that she had gone to live with him again. The prisoner was sent to gaol for three months. BODY RECOVERED BY A RUNCORN GENTLEMAN. On Tuesday Mr. Shaw, missionary of the Seameti's Institute was called to Bootle for the purpose of recover- ing with his grappling appliances; the body of a boat- man named Draper of the ft st"Moon"who was drowned on Saturday in Brocklebank Bank, Bootle. Mr. Shaw did not, however, find the body until Wednesday, when it was found under a spot occupied by a steamer. The relatives of the unfortunate man, who is a native of IManchester. were profuse in their expressions of thanks to Mr. Shaw for his services in recovering the body. ST. PAUL'S WESLEY GUILD, RUNCORN. The closing night of the winter session was held on Wednesday night, in the Brunswick Schoolroom, in con- nection with the above guild. The meeting was taken as an opportunity of making a presentation to Miss Massey in recognition of her services as the leader of the Ladies' section of the Gymnasium. Mr. Burrows OR be- half of the lady members presented Miss Massey with a lady's dressing case, and in doing so referred to her ser- vices and the evident appreciation of them by the mem- bers. Miss Massey suitably responded, and 'concluded by wishing the Gymnasium success. On behalf of the gentlemen Mr. Sanders presented Miss Massey with a book, entitled "The Mighty Atom." The proceedings then became of a social character and proved thoroughly enjoyable. The Gymnasium during the season has been very successful. The institution was inaugurated by the male members of the guild and the appliances used were given by friends and outside members of St. Paul's. It is intended to keep on the Wesley guild through the summer,and outdoor recreations euch as tennis, cricket, and rambling clubs, will go to form an attractive programme. ' SUPPOSED DEATH AT RUNCORN THROUGH NEGLECT. The facts touclling the death of ElizAbeth Bridgewater wife of James Bridgewater, of 99. Shaw-street,Runcorn, a woman aged 32 years,have been reported by the police to Mr. Wortley, the coroner for the district. They &amp;low that the deceased gave birth to a child five weeks ago and that she was attended by her mother until the 29th ult. Her husband returned home last Saturday and see- ing that his wife was not well called in Dr. Ormsby on Tuesday. The doctor prescribed for her. Next morning the husband gave her a cup of tea and on his return at eight o'clock at night he found his wife dead. On Wed- nesday, the day of her death, Mrs. Bridgewater received the attentions of a neighbour who procured for her some beef tea i.nd port wine and she was also seen by the nurse. Death took place on the Wednesday and Dr. Ormsby has refused to certify the cause of death. An inquest will likely be held to-day (Friday).</p>
        <p>TERRIBLE FALL OF A STEEPLEJACK AT WIDNES. INQUEST AND VERDICT. On Thursday, at the Whines Police Station, Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner, held an inquiry into the cir- cumstances attending the death of Owen Jones, aged fitty-six, a steeplejack, 4, Crown-st., Pendleton, near Manchester, who died at the Widnes Accident Hospital on Tuesday from the effects of injuries sustained on Sunday by falling from a chimney at Pilkington's Chemi- cal Works of the United Alkali Company, Mess Bank, Widnes. Mr. H. S. Richmond, Government inspector, was present. Mr. J. W. Knowles represented the rela- tivds of the deceased, and Mr. Rideal, of Manchester. appeared on behalf of Mr. Bidder, in whose employ deceased was. William Johnson, of 3,Crumpsall-view,Hindham-vale, Queen's-road. Manchester, in the employ of Mr. Samuel Bidder, contractor, of Manchester. who was now en- gaged with Jones on the work at Pilkington's, said that on Sunday they were engaged fixing a lightning con- ductor on a chimney. The chimney was 120 feet in height. Witness and the deceased commenced the work on Friday, but as the deceased was not well on Saturday they did nothing that day, but resumed at eight o'clock on Sunday morning, and continued till half-past twelve. They recommenced at half-past two, and at a quarter to five the accident happened in consequence of the rope breaking, the deceased being precipitated to the ground, a distance of fifteen or twenty yards. Witness worked with the same rope a month before, but it had been on the roof of the saltcake shed for a fortnight. In witness's opinion, the rope was not a good one, and about three weeks ago witness declined to work with it. He conveyed his objections to Thomas Hargreaves. who superintended the work at which they were then engaged. When the deceased and wit- ness arrived at Pilkington's works on Friday it was their intention to affix the lightning conductor by means of ladders,but finding the conductor had been ordered by the manager to be placed on the opposite side of the chimney to where the ladders were fixed, the ropes had to be used. The deceased was in charge cf the job, and being told by witness that the rope was bad, replied, " We will work off the ladders." In reply to Mr. Richmond, witness said the rope bad been affected by the acids. Replying to Mr. Knowles, witness said Mr. Bidder knew on Saturday morning that witness and deceased intended to use the rope, and also knew that the rope was a bad one, but said ke "Dared say that it would do to finish the job." In answer to other questions,witness contradicted him- self, and said it was toe deceased who said he dared say the rope would do. The Coroner: From what the deceased said Mr. Bidder knew there was a suspicion about the rope?--Wit- ness : Yes.—ln answer to Mr. Rideal, witness said the deceased had worked for Mr. Bidder for fifteen years,and was a man of experience. The deceased had not, to wit- ness's knowledge, requested another rope from Mr. Bidder.—Further questioned by the coroner, witness stated that three weeks ago the tope was condemned, and another sent in its place, but subsequently the new one was taken to Warrington. On 'Saturday morning Mr. Bidder knew the con. emned rope was to be used. Mary Jones, of Parker-street, Warrington, sister-in-law of the deceased, gave evidence of identification, and said she saw the deceased on Monday morning at the hospital. He then told witness that it was through the rope that he sustained his injuries. He aided that he had told his master that the rope was a bad one, but he had said it would do for the job, and then they could have a new one. After the evidence as to the admission of the deceased into the Accident Hospital,Mr. Bidder was called by Mr. Rideal,and in reply to his questions, said he had been in business about eighteen years. He finished a big job at the chimney about a month ago, and left the rope in question at the works, and the ladder was left standing, and on Thursday night he gave the deceased orders to fix up a lightning conductor on the ladder side. Witness knew nothing about the men working with the rope on the Friday, as he had given orders that they were to use the ladders. If he had seen the rope on the Saturday morning, he would never have allowed them to use the rope. The jury returned a verdict that the accident was caused by th 3 breaking of the rope, and that the death was accidental.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>RUNCORN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A SHORT MEETING.</p>
        <p>The usual monthly meeting of this Council was held in the Town Hall on Thursday morning, present: Mr. J. W. Lightburn, J.P. (chairman), Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, Messrs. F. Wiswell, H. J. Grice, J. Wilson, G. H. Kirk- ham, W. Handley, P. Speakman, J.P., C.C., E. Irnison, J. Horsefield, M. Sweetman, D. Btsbrown S. Taylor, J.P.C.C., F. A. Lake (law clerk),E. Marshall (secretary), and J. Wilding (surveyor).: MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Dr. McDougall presented his monthly report which read as follows. The number of deaths registered dur- ing the past month amounted to 19. Of the 19 deaths 2 were due to cancer, 3 to croup, 4 to the heart disease, 2 to peritonitis, 2to premature birth, 2to skin disease, and 1 each to abdominal inflammation, enteritis,natural decay, and pneumonia. The 19 deaths show an average death rate for the 12 months of 1102 per thous- and ot the estimated population. Sixty-five births were registered. Sixteen deaths were registered during the corresponding period last year. The report was adopted. Dr. Gastaldi remarking that it was very satisfactory.</p>
        <p>HIGHWAY AND BUILDING COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The Highway Committee recommended that the pen- alty to which Mr. William Moore is liable under contract dated Ist August, 1895, for the making of Penketh lane and other streets be reduced to is. on the ground that this Council has not been able to keep him supplied with materials, also that the time for the completion of the contract be extended to May 31st, 1896 • that a water cart be purchased, and that the tender of Messrs. Henry Wadsworth and Son, Halifax,to supply one,he accepted; that the plan of Mr. D. Watkin of the addition of two single storey shops to the front of the house lately used as a model lodging-house in Church-street, adjoining the shop owned and occupied by Mr. D. Watkin, be ap- proved ; that the plan of Messrs. William Farrell and Son of 17 houses proposes to be erected in the Holloway be approved. The minutes were adepted. HEALTH COMMITTEE. Mr.W. Handley proposed the adoption of the minutes of the Health Committee. He said he did so with great pleasure after hearing Dr. McDougall's satisfactory re- port on the health of tbe town. The town was free from any severe form of epidemical disease. There had been no cases of smallpox notified for months and months, and there had been no cases of diphtheria and mess lea during the month. In other respects there was only a normal amount of minor contagious diseases.—Mr.Kirk- ham seconded, and the minutes were adopted. MARKETS AND CEMETERY COMMITTEE. This committee proposed that the tender of Messrs. Unsworth Bros. to supply uniform clothing for the Market Superintendent for the sum of four guineas be accepted.—Mr. Kirkham moved the adoption of the re- commendation, and Mr. Grice seconded the resolution, which was carried. TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>- This committee proposed that Mr.Posnett be appointed a member of the committee and that the sum of £2 be paid to Mrs. Ackerley tor services rendered in connec- tion with the cookery classes. Mr. Wiswall moved the adoption of the minutes and Mr. Sweetman seconded. The resolution was carried.</p>
        <p>FINANCE COMMIVEE. Mr. Irsaison,in moving the passiEg of the minutes of this committee referred to the fact that in the esti- mates provision had been made for the introduction of the telephone system into the offices. A committee had gone into the matter and having had the benefit of the advice of gentlemen who had made use of the system had decided in favour of subscribing to it feeling sure that it would expediate the work and at the same time it might be of financial benefit to the Council.—Mr. Sweetman seconded and the minutes were unanimously passed. The Council then resolved itself into committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
