      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>TEACHERS' SCALE OF SALARIES.</head>
        <p>TEACHERS' SCALE OF SALARIES.</p>
        <p>The following scale of salaries was decided by the Board :- 1, Master of Boys' School : (a) Average attend- ance 200 to 300—to commence at £l4O and advance by annual increments of £5 to a maximum of £2OO. (b)— Average attendance over 300—to commence at £l5O and advance by annual increments of £5 to a maximum ot £220. 2, Mistress of Girls' or Junior Mixed School: (a)— Average attendance 200 to:300—to commence at £95 and advance by annual increments of £3 to a maximum of £125. kb)—Average attendance over 300—to com- mence at £lO4 and advance by annual increments of £3 to £l4O. 3, Mistress of Infants' School: (a)—Average attend- ance under 150—to commeece at £7O, maximum £9O by the following annual increases :—£72, £75 , £7B, £Bl., £B4, £B7, £9O. (k) Average at- tendance over 150—to commence at £B5 maxi- mum, £llO by the following annual increases : £B7, £B9, £92, £95, £9B, £lOl. £lO4, £lO7, £llO. The yearly increases are dependent upon a favourable report from the School Management Committee, after consideration of H.M. Inspector's annual report, and will date from the let day of the month following the decision of the Board. There are also several extra pay- ments in addition to the preceding scale of salaries according to the grants received. Pupil teachers : Boys. Ist year £l3; 2nd year, £l6; 3rd year £l9; 4th year £22 ; sth year, when so extended £3O. Girls, Ist year £8; 2nd year £10; 3rd year £l2; 4th year £l5; .sth year, when so extended £2O. A grant of 30s. will be made to each pupil teacher who obtains the mark " Good " at any of H.M. Inspector's examinations. and £2 if placed in the first class at the 4.lueea's scholarship examination. Stipendiary monitors, 2s. 6d. per week. Mr. Norman in moving the adoption of the scale said that owing to the large .addition to the Board's staff some arrangements had become necessary. The bulk of the work was carried out by the Chairman and Mr. Imison and it had been compiled in no grudging spirit. They had taken as a basis 7 or 8 important towns and he wished to point out that it would not affect prejudi- cially any of the existing staff, but would rather form a basis for future appointments and future advances. He thought that in the result they had a scale which would protect them from the charge of extravagance from the ratepayers' standpoint by niggardliness so far as the officials were concerned.—Mr. Taylor had very great pleasure in seconding the motion which to him seemed to be a fair arrangement between the staff and the Board, and he thought it would be satisfactory both to the ratepayers and the staff.—The Chairman supported the resolution and said it was a matter of satis- faction that the Board had at last arranged a scale for the payment of its teachers.—The resolution was then put and carried unanimously. RESIGNATIONS.</p>
        <p>Notices terminating their engagements with the School Board were sent in by Miss Bessie Massey, mis- tress, Granville-street Infants' School; Miss Caroline Litter, mistress, Granville-street Girls' School, and Mr. W. Hennan, senior assistant, Victoria Road Schools. This was the whole of the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>HOW THE CUP WAS WON.</head>
        <p>HOW THE CUP WAS WON.</p>
        <p>A CHAT WITH THE RUNCORN CAPTAIN.</p>
        <p>While Runcorn is still talking of the success of the team and supporters of the club are still jubi- lant over their possession of the Lancashire section of the Northern Union handsome trophy it will be quite appropriate to give a few reminiscences of William Faulkner who for two reasons has so sac-</p>
        <p>cessfully captained the " linnets." Our repre- sentative on calling upon him on Tuesday morning</p>
        <p>tound the popular captain little or none ihe worse for the exciting episodes of the last few weeks. When asked to retail some of his past doings in the ranks of Runcorn football he expressed his willing- ness, though be regretted at the same time that he had not kept a diary and have been thus able to give the dates of matches, etc., more correctly. To use his own words "Had I known that I should have played so long. I would have done so, but at the end of the season players are generally done up and talk about never playing again, though the next season, after a summer's rest, sees them play- ing as fresh as ever." To describe Faulkner it may be said that he is an ideal of what a Rugby forward should be. Standing 5 feet 9 inches with a healthy weight of 14i stone. On the field he plays a thor- oughly honest game, characterized by coolness, pluck and judgment. Ile was born 28 years ago within 200 yards of the ground of the football club, and it is not extraordinary that the then youthful Faulkner should take to the game like a young duck takes to water. He seems to have played almost since he could walk, but gives to the Runcorn Recreation team the real honour of introducing him to the football world at a time when he would be about 16 to 18 years old. His powers with that team becoming justly known, brought him an invitation to join the premier club in the year the team took a prominent part in the old West Lancashire Cup Competition. At that time the team was captained by Hugh Hughes. Faulkner played with the first team that season, and he has continued to play for the team ever since. In that, his first year, Runcorn managed to reach the same— final stage ot the Cup Competition at which point they were overthrown by Warrington on neutral ground at Southport. From that time until the present the Runcorn team has been rarely made up without the insertion of Faulkners' name. As a for- ward he has shone particularly, though he has ap- peared with equal success in every position on the field, notably as three-quarter last year against St. Helens Recreations, when, though the Recreations were leading by two tries, he pulled the match out of the fire by dropping a goal, and afterwards gain- ing a try. Last year he was unanimously selected a captain, and this year has had the honour of cap- taining the team in the most successful period of its history. Apart from the pro- minent position occupied by Faulkner in local football circles, honours have fallen upon him in every degree of Rugby football. Thus ten seasons ago we see him representing Cheshire at Halifax against Yorkshire,and it is worthy of note that since hat time Faulkner's name has always been included in the County team. Three years ago he was made captain of the County representativts and this season he has figured for Cheshire four times under the rules of the Ncrthern Union in its matches against Lancashire and Yorkshire. Amongst other important matches in which he has taken part may be mentioned his appearance in the West of England v. Lancashire (champion county) in April, 1891; with North against south, December, 1892; and with West Lancashire v. Westmoreland in the old days of the cup. On being questioned on the match at Broughton, Faulkner smiled. tie considered that though it was a surprise to out- siders it was not to the players who went fit and confident of winning the match. "it certainly was a bit sensational at first," continued Faulkner. "I kicked off, and the ball was passed and repassed, so I scored before the ball was dead, much to the de- light of our friends from Runcorn. The Oldham team seemed disheartened from the time the try was scored. It had been said that Run?,orn in the second halt played purely on the defensive, but Faulkner in reply io a query on that point, said " We did not play MI the defensive, as we thought we had as good a chance of again scoring as Oldham had.' Speaking of the team the captain thinks that though Runcorn has had some good men in the past they have never had a better team than they have at the present time. According to the captain the men this year have been thoroughly in earnest and have kept themselves in conditions, Under similar conditions Runcorn will stand a got. chance of repeating the performance next yt. Faulkner isjalso pleased with the way the men have acted in the field, and the promptness with whichk. his orders have been obeyed. As our representativ( shook hands with him previous to leaving the cap- tain again repeated "that the team was never better than at present," and many will be inclined to think that a large measure of its success is cer- tainly due to the player who is not only a good captain, but a sound and cool exponent of the Rugby forward game.</p>
        <p>• THROAT IHRITAiIoN AND Co-uon.—Soreness •Ind Dryness tiokling and irritators, 'mincing cough and affecting the 7oice. For L . es. - %ILIA° ,II use Epps's Glyc•rine Jujubes. In contact wit') tie gi,ouls at the moment they are excited by the act of sucking, toe Glycerine in these agreeable con- tecticns uecomes actively healing. Sold only in boxes, 71d., tins Is. lid., labelled •Jaugs EPP.; &amp; Co., Ltd., Howe°. patnic Chemists, London." Dr. Moore, in his work on "Nose and Throat Diseases," says: "The Glycerine Jujubes prepared by James Hpps and Co., are of undoubted service as a curative or palliative agent," while Dr. Gordon Holmes, Senior Physician to the Municipal Throat and liar Infirmary, writes: "After an extended trial, I have found your Glycerine Jujubes of considerable benefit in almost all forms of throat disease."—Advt. IHE LA.TINT NOVKLTIES in Silk and Plush Capes and Mantles, now showing at HVANs', 17, Sankey-street, Warrington.-4 tut.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <p>PROSECUTION OF A NEWTON PROPERTY</p>
        <p>OWNER. A LINE OF FRONTAGE QUESTION.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Petty -"Sessions on Wednes- day. before Captain Withington (chairman), Major Pickmere, Mr. J. J. Bleckley and Mr. W. Houghton, Garrett Roloyne was charged with having on the 31st of October in Newton, contravened bye-laws published by the Urban District Council of Newton, by erecting a wooden building in Bridge-street contrary to the line of frontage approved and fixed by the Council. Mr. Stuart, solicitor, Newton, defended. Mr. Worsley prosecuted for the Newton District Coun- cil, and said that when the Council was the Board of Commissioners it was entrusted with the laying out of the new town and made bye-laws, the 9th of which said that "the line of frontage of all new buildings shall be approved and fixed by the Council, or Local Board, and no building shall be erected contrary to the line of frontage which has been so approved." He added that Mr. Ronayne's plans showed a departure from the building line, and the Council therefore would not accept them. Mr. Ronayne did not proceed to erect his buildings in contravention of the bye- law but put up a wooden structure. When this was in course of erection he (Mr. Worsley) was directed by the Council to caution him, and explain that he was liable te a penalty. He did 110, but defendant proceeded with the structure notwithstanding. Defendant was again written to, but the erection was nut up and the Council were obliged to take these proceedings. Though wooden, it was a building within the defitutien of the Act of Parlia- ment Mr. Worsley asked the bench to make an order for the removal of the building and to place a penalty upon defendant for each day the building remained be- yond a fixed time. Mr. R. Brierley, surveyor to the Council, gave formal , evidence in support of ,Oar. Worsley's statement about the plans.—ln answer to Mr. Stuart he said the Council had not passed a resolution fixing a certain line of fron- tage for the street but they had fixed the line for the property in question. Mr. Stuart contended that the prosecution had no case because the Council had not passed a resolution de- fining the line of frontage. On the summons the words were " contrary to the line of frontage fixed by the Council." He said none had been fixed. Mr. Worsley : The line of frontage was fixed when the original plans were submitted in 1891. Mr. Stuart's objection was over-ruled. Mr. Stuart then said that the level of his client's land was 10 feet below the street and he submitted that the erection in question was nothing more than the neces- sary fence. One woul I have to be erected if it was re- moved. The bye-law affecting them was, he said, ultra vires. It was not for the good of the township and in- terfered with Mr. Ronayne's facilities for business. If he was driven hack his shop would be in a corner—a ridiculous position and manifestly unfair,besides putting his client to a lot of expense. The bench decided, however, to order the removal of the building within a month, or in default to inflict a penalty of 20s. for each additional day it remained. Mr. Worsley was granted the costs of the summons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>WARRINGTON DISTRICT HIGHWAY</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON DISTRICT HIGHWAY</p>
        <p>" - BOARD. On Wednesday afternoon, the annual meeting of this Board was held at Bewsey Chambers, when there were present : Captain T. E. Withington, Messrs. J. Hough- ton, R. T. Lowe, R. B. Matthews, P. Llngton, P. Bate, T. Ormrod, J. Clare, R. Garnett, J.P.. H. Bate, B. Thornton, J. Ball, W. Fairclough, Stanier, and E. A. White (deputy clerk), and J. Thompson (surveyor.) THE CHAIRMAN. Mr. James Houghton proposed that Captain Withing- ton be the chairman for the ensuing year.—Mr. R. T. Matthews seconded, and the motion was unanimously elected.—Captain Withington, in thanking the members, said that ni looking round the room he could not see anyone present who was a member of 'the Board in 1/368 when he was first appointed chairman. The way- warden of limyon (Mr. Bate) was the oldest member of the Board and he hoped Mr. Bate would remain a mem- ber until the close of the Board. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>THE VICE-CHAIRMAN. The Chairman proposed Mr. James Houghton for the position of senior vice-chairman.—Mr. R. T. Lowe seconded and said that Mr.. Houghton had had a great deal of experience in the work.—The proposition was carried, and Mr. Houghton, thanked the members for electing him to that position. Had Mr. Matthews been c&amp;mpetent to hold the office after November, he would have asked him to take the office.—The Chairman then moved that Mr. P. Bate be appointed junior vice-chair- man.—Mr. James Houghton seconded the proposition, which was unanimously carried.—Mt Bkta then acknowledged the honour conferred upon him</p>
        <p>FINANCE COMMITTEE. It was decided that the Finance Committee should conQist of the chairman, senior and junior vice-Phair. men, Messrs. J. Clare, H. Bate, R. T. Lowe, .I'. 'l'. Ormrod, P. Longton, and W. Fairclough.</p>
        <p>THE SHIP CANAL SPECIAL COMMITTEE. It was decided to re-elect the special committee which was appointed last year to look after the interests of the Board in connection with the Ship Canpl and the new road over Arpley Meadows, consisting of the chairman, senior and junior vice-chairmen, and the waywardens for Great Sankey and Little Sankey.—ln reply to Mr. Houghton the Clerk said that the appeal of the Ship Canal Company with regard to the new road would pro- bably come on this week or next. "A GREAT INCONVENIENCE." Mr. MarrizEws said it seemed a great inconvenience that the meetings of the Board should cut across the Tarporley Hunt and the Chester Cup days. (Laughter.) He thought tho3e were two Warrington Holidays.—The Chairman : You don't complain, do you P—Mr. Mat- thews: I don't think I should have been in Warrington to-day had it not been for this meeting. (Laughter.) BARNES' BRIDGE, SANKEY. Mr. H. BATE said he had to complain at the state of the footpath of the bridge on the highway near the Board school at Great Sankey.— l'he Surveyor said he had seen the county surveyor with . reference to the mat- ter. and he had promised him to alter it as quickly as possible. He was coming again on Friday, and he (the Surveyor) would again tnentiun the matter. NOTHING FOR LITTLE SANKEY.</p>
        <p>The Surveyor had nothing to report with regard to work at Little Sankey.—The Chairman: They are doing nothing there.—Mr. Matthews: We never have anything to do, tut we have to pay for other people's roads. We have to stknd for everybody. (Laughter.) —The Clerk : Little Sankey has no roads. HALTON'S BRIDON ONCE AGAIN.</p>
        <p>The Surveyor reported that a portion of Chapel-lane had been channelled and paved.—Mr. R. Girnett : That wanted doing 30 or 40 years ago. It Is time it was done. Have you nothing referring to Halton's bridge.—The Chairman: We have had that question before us for a long time.—Mr. R. Garnett: I hope we shall have it behind us before long. He (Mr. Garnett) had been on this job for thirty or forty years. He once got permissien to use the laud required for the widening of the bridge from the Blue Coat trustees. He would like to see some way proposed by which the work could be done abso- lutely.— The Chairman said Mr. Garnett's pre- decessor had been most energetic in bring- ing the • matter to the front.—The Clerk said the Board were prepared to pay the balance, if the Courty Council who were in possession of the main road leading to the bridge.would contribute £1,300. The difficulty was that the County Council would not enter into an agreement under the Act of 1891.—N1r. Garnett said he would be glad to know if the same difficulty had arisen elsewhere, and if so he it had been met.—Tne Chairman said he had not heard of a similar difficulty.— Mr. Garnett asked whether the Board had ever borrowed money for work of that kind, and was the County Coun- cil repaying the money borrowed by voluntary instal- ments yearly to any district ?—The Clerk said he did not know, but it was quite possible.—Mr. Thompson (surveyor) said he was informed by the County Surveyor that Urban authorities borrowed money on the same conditions.—The Chairman : The Board will be expiring in less than two years.—Mr. Garnett thought there should be some way out of the deadlock.—The Chairman said the Board had done its best in the matter for the last three or four years. A deputation from the County Council had visited the place and it was expected that something would be done. But the County Council would not pay their share of the cost.—The Clerk : They want the Board to find the money, and then they will pay the amount by yearly instalments. It would be most difficult for that expiring Board to borrow the money.—Mr. Garnett : Suppose half-a-dozen people get killed there, or a bishop. (Laughter.)—The Chairman said he did not know about bishops, but he knew that a policeman was run over there by a bicycle. (Laughter.) —Mr. Houghton said it was the most dangerous place le whole district.—Mr. Garnett said if there was a irig, he though there should be a way to right it. le Clerk said the County Council was the autho- love in the matter. It was a main road nty ibridg,e.—Mr. Matthews said the parish tie Sankey sent a resolution to the County 'le matter. He suggested that all the in the neighbourhood of the bridge me. They were materially interested !cause it was the highway between two ulation—Warrington and Widnes. The 'ay to get the work done would be to ,uaty Council with resolutions from the He did not think the Board coutd the matter.—Mr. Garcett said he would see tat could be done.</p>
        <p>THE HIGHWAY RATE. The Clerk'read the Surveyor's estimate of expendi- ture which was as follows: secondary roads £lBOO, district roads £l4OO, improvements £BB6, salaries and superintendence at roads £483 143.1 general expenses £250, total £4,799 14s. The estimate for improvements was as follows : Buttonwood £3O, and £lOO in respect of secondary roads; Cuerdley £4s,Culcheth £127, Great Sankey £6O ; Little Sankey, nil ; Houghton £6O ; Kenyon £2OO ; Lawton £94 : (secondary roads), Pen- -I(etli £6O ; Poulton £6O ; Rixton £165; Croft £7O ; Winwick £36 ; Woolston £lOO : total £BB6 chargeable to the Board. The balance in hand was £615 93. 31, leaving a balance on the estimate of £4183 153. 4d.—On the motion of Mr. Houghton seconded by Mr. Clare it was decided to lay a 81. rate. This concluded the business.</p>
        <p>THE GULATION POST C Ew ARD N- RE WITH PRINTED HEADING,</p>
        <p>FROM 3/9 PER 1,000.</p>
