      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>PRIMITIVII 3111THODIST CHAPEL, KINGSWOOD.—The annual chapel sermons were preached at this place on Sunday afternoon and evening by Mr. J. Whitley, of Stockton Heath, to large congregations especially in the evening when the chapel was filled to its utmost c,apa. city. Mr. Whitley has conducted these services each year since the chapel was ergcted five years ago,and on Monday evening he gave a vh7 amusing and interesting lecture on " Epitaphs" to a goad congregation. Mr. S. Parkes, of Newton,was the chairman. At the close Of the lecture votes of thanks were accorded to the lecturer and chairman. The Missess Woodward sang solos dur- ing each service which were very much appreciated, ,and Mrs. Andrews. Ryley !Sank, presided at the organ with her usual ability ; and great credit is due to both organist and choir for their services. The collections taken up amounted to about a which is considerably in advance of last year. VISITATION BY CHANCELLOR ESPIN.—On Friday, Chan. cellos Espin centinuing his visitation of the diocese of Chester admitted into office the wardens and sidemen of the churches in the Frodsham Rural Deanery. In the course of his address Chancellor Espin remarked that when in 1874 he first began his visitations of the diocese there were 407 churches but now with the same area there were 478 or an increase of 71, and these mostly represented new parishes.</p>
      </div>
