      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>A VIOLENT CHARACTER-TWO SENTENCES IN _TWO DAYS.</p>
        <p>On Saturday morning at the Warrington Court before Messrs. Burgess and S. -Wright, Annie Smith, a woman with a reputation for violence, was chargel with com- mitting wilful damage to certain celf fittings, in the Police Station on Friday afternoon, and doing damage to the extent of 54.—The Chief Constable (Mr. Talbot) informed the bench that Prisoner was brow,ht before the magistrates on Friday morning on a charge or as- saulting a constable and was committed for one month's hnprisonmect. She made a remark to the Chairman that she would do it on her head. She was removed to the cells below and when left alone commenced to be very violent and committed an amount of dsmage to the seat of the closet, which she broke to pieces. Therefore instead of being sent on to gaol she was detained. So violent was her conduct that during the greater part ot Friday she was handcuffed with her hands behind her back. She said she was determined to have three months and not one. It was ner 20::h offence.—P.C. Dean cor- roborated the Chief's statement.—Prisoner who main- tained a defiant attitude in court was sentenced to a further two mon' ha hard labour, making the three months she expressed a desire for.</p>
        <p>ATTEMPT TO JuIN TEE POLICE FORCE WITH A FALSE</p>
        <p>CHALtecTER.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Court on Monday, before the Mayor, Mr. F. Monks, and Mr. J. W. Hay ward,John Richard Lyons, Brick-st., a smart military-looking man, was charged with having on the 20.-.11 April ogered his services to the Chief Constable as his servant, with a false certificate of character.—He admitted the offence. —The Cnief Conatable said that this was an unusual prosecution, and he regretted greatly that necessity had compelled him to take the step he had done. It was very necessary that he should protect the police forco from getting men in its ranks with doubtful characters. The circumstances were that defendant barne to Inspec- tor Spinks on Wednesday, the 20:h April, and offered himself as a candidate for the force. Ile had recently come out of the army, and seeing that he possessed all physical requirements Inspector Spiults brought him before himself (the Chief Constable). He produced his discharge from the King's Own Gloucester Regiment and his character sheet. This stated tidat his conduct with the colours had been good. This was signed by Major Griffiths. After defendant had gowe away he (the Chief Constable) had his attention drawn to the fact that an alteration had been made of the word, of the char :eter. He sent the form at once to Major Griffiths, who seat a reply which had been read to defendant on Wednesday last.lt said that the forms were genuine except the char- acter. Major Griffiths had bee unable to assess defend- ant's character at more than fair,and that word had been altered to " good" withaut his knowledge or consent. Defendant admitted that ha had made the alteration himself before he left the barracks. Mr. Talbot further explained that he had ascertained that defendant's father was an old soldier with an excellent character, Defendant admitted that he had been ashamed to shoat his father that dtPiious ch irite.ter and had altered it, from sudden temptation. Defendant was liable to penalty nf £2O or i default three months. A fair charac- ter would not admit defendant to the Police fsrce.— Defendant said ho had made the alteration to prevent his parents knowing. He did not do it for the purpose of getting a position.—The Chief Constable said he had other characters which served to show that defendant had been a good :ad when younger. He prosecuted more as a warning to others than to punish defendant.—Toe magistrates took a lenient view of the ca:e and ordered defendant to pay 2s. 6d. to the poor-box.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON ROROUGFI POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>FRIDAY.—B-toreitiderman Harrison (chairman), Alderman</p>
        <p>Weight, Councillor Roberts and Dr. Gorntill.</p>
        <p>ASSAULTI;G A POLICEMAN.—AnnIe Smith, no settled abide, was eh trged with ass suiting P.C. Pennington on -at- urdiy last.—P.C. Pennington said he was o-t duty on Satur- day nignt and when apprehending a woman named Savage, defendant Interfered and tried to rescue the woman. She struck him twici in the face .nd then disappeared. On Thursday he identified her outside the Cour; .—Defendant who had nothing to say and made her 19th appeatanee, was sent to gaol tor one month. QUAtitREL.9 BETWEEN WOMEN.—Bridget Tit chard Prestley- street, was charge 1 with assaultinfr and beating Catherine Hennessy, Coppers Brow, on the 25th April.—Mrs.Hennessv who appeared with a bruised and bloilshot eye, said she was eturck in the eyebv defendant with something. Previously she had been qeirreling with another women and de(endant interfered. - Evidence+ was teard on h th sides and the case dismissed.—Annie Ross Crosley-street, was char,ed with as- milting and beating Elizabeth Lomax, 17, Crosley-street.— Mrs. Lomax stateS that she was seized by the hair be rief•-nd- ant.—Defenthnt was bound over for six months and ordered to pay costs. WIFE ASSAULT.—Joseph rhillips, Catherine-a'reet. was charged with assaulting and heating his wife on 25th April Defendant pleaded guilty —Mrs. Phillips, a reap -ctsble look- ing person.said that on Saturday night defendant came in and struck her while she was sitting clown. Afterwa di he threw the pepper-box at her when she had her baby in her arms. lie had frequently abused her without little as she alw .ys did right by him. She did not want him to go to gaol. -The magistrates bound defendant over for six months to keep the peace, in two sureties of £5. and to pay costa. ALLEGED THREATS.—John Stanfield, 9?., Shame-street.. was chsrged with using threats to Mary Cogan on the 25th April. Mrs. Cogan said defendant hid threatened to kick her from one side of the street to the other. Witnesses for to defend- ant denied that he used any threats at all, and he himself said he had served 30 years in the Army and had a good charac- ter.—The case was clismis,ed.</p>
        <p>SITURDAT.—Before Mr. John Burgess (chairman) and Mr. S. Wright. _</p>
        <p>"A BIRD OF P.49.AGF,."—Ro3e Ann Burns Was Cliftrirri With begging in Minchester-road on Friday alternoon.—Eletective Roper proved the c.se. —The Chief Constable remarked that the woman WAS "n bird of passage." While in the c-lis she was troublesome and broke a window by tbrauing her boot at it.—Prisoner wits committed for 14 days' bar ' labour.</p>
        <p>ASSAULTING A B /MAN —Walter Warburton was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Buttermarket-street on Friday afternoon; he was farther charged with assaulting' Peter jtaley at the same time and place.—P.C. Wood *aid that at 2 o clock on Friday afternoon he taw defendant ejected from the ' Lower Angel" by the barman. Peter Jolley. Defendant struck him two or three times. tie to,e the hr- man's waistcoat. When witness seized him he threatened ti " go tor" Jolley.—Peter .Tolley corroborated and clefen•iant was fined 20a. and co•ta or I month's hard labour in default. THFFT FROM A DRAPER'S SHOP.—A youth named William Ferguson was charged with having strlen on the previoua afternoon from the shop of William Ilaylor,draper. 73. Priest- ley-street. six pairs ot stockings,value Is. 9 I.—The Chief Con- stable, said that the prisoner was seen by P.C. Lewis to leave the shop of Mr. Baylor, and Lewis, being somewhat sus- picious, entered the shop and asked the young lady in cliArgr, of it if she had missed anything. She answered that she did not know, but attled that she would look. Whilst doing so Lewis left the shop and fo lowed the prisoner, whom he over- took in Bewsey-road. Seeing he was bulky about the booly, he asked him what he had under his coat, at the same time pulling the garment aside and revealing the stockings pr t- duced. Prisoner at first said he had b night Them, but sub,e• quently admitted stealing them because he was hungry. He was taken back to the shop, where the stockings were identified as Mr. N iylor's property.—Prisoner pleaded He was committed to gaol for 14 days the Chief Constable remarking that he had been dismissed with ignominy for having struck a superior officer. He had also had twelve months' imprisonment.</p>
        <p>TOBACCONISTS eninmeneing ; Illuthatecl Guide and Cat.alo gue (259 pages) 31. TooAccomeTa' OuTFITTING Co.-186 Euston-road, London. (Igr), HENRY MYER.</p>
        <p>MoxpaY.—Before the Mayor (chairman), Mr. F. Monks and Mr. J. W. Hayward. THEY'. FROM A FELLOW-WORKMAN.--A youth,named John Edward Nester, 12, Dolman's-yard, was charged with steal- ing from the bark-room of Tinsley's tannery, Mersey-street on Saturday morning, a silver watch and guard value 208., the property of Michael Mannion.—Defendant pleaded guilty to the charge.—The Chief Constable in stating ,he circurn- etances of the case, said that Michael Mannion on the thy in question went to his work at 6 o'clock. Defendant was a tellow-workman of complainant's. Mannion hung up his watch in the bark-room and left it. He had occasion to go to another port of the premises and met defendant cominT towards the bark-room. About 8 o'clvk Mannion missed his tch and suspecting defendant asked him if he knew any- thing about it. Defendant stoutly denied having had it and told complainant he could search him if lie liked. Com- plainant informed his employers and they also questioned defendant but he still denied any knowledge of the watch. Tte police weie called in and Detective's Spinks and Monaghan saw defendant. As he could not account for certain things they at last compzlled him to admit having t•tken the watch and guard. Defendant conducted the officers to the place' where he litcl hid len them and ga.e them up. This was defendant's first offence and he bore a good character with his employ,Ts. The bench tined de- fen‘iant be. including costs and recorded a conviction against him.</p>
        <p>DISORDERLY TOPERS.—Frederick Waller and Margaret Waller, 3, Hill-street, were fined 2s. 6 1., for being drunk and disorderly in Buttermarket-street on Sitarday night, Hannah Kinsey. 6. Robin on's-yard, Fennell-street, for the s une off- ence was fined 2s. 6d., and Margaret McLnighlin, was sent to gaol for one month, GAMING FOR MONEY.—John Baguley. Friars' Green. wai charged with gaming with Cards for money in Golden Grove- street. on Saturday night at 7.40.—He pleaded not guilty— Detective Barlow said that at the time stated, in company w.th Dete .live Roper he saw defendant and a number of other lads playing banker " for money. On seeing them the lads ran away. They caught Baguley mid found in his pos- session a pack al.d a half of cards and 6ad. in copper. A lot of complaints had been made ai)out gambling in the neigh- bou-ho al of the market and Baguley was the ringleader of the lads who played.— This WAS his third stoi.,earance for the SAttle offence and the bench now c ,mmitted him for 2 months h ird labour.—Thomas Hardman (16). 12 Heaton-street ; Peter Murray (14), 3, Berry's-yard ; John Dutton (14), 6, Morris- yard ; Josenti Gerrard (13), 4, Bakehouse-yard ' • George Dickeason (13), 1, Chapel Court, ; 11o)ert Newell (15). 2, Hall street ; were all charged with committing a similar offence in the Market Piace on the 29th April.—Market-Inspector Place said thv, on Friday he was in his office and saw the defend- ants playing crude on one of the stalls Ile telephoned to two of his men and eurrounded the lads. Gerrard mats caught and he gave the n tmei end addresses of the others. He said he had lost lid. at '' banker "—Murray was fined Is. and the rest of the lads detained 48 hours in the cells on the minimum fare.</p>
        <p>BEGGING.—John Connor, a tramp, was sent to poi for 7 dAys tor begging in Sankey-st.. on Saturday night. THURSDA.Y.—Beforo Abler man Sutton (chairman), and Councillor Jtobe-t s.</p>
        <p>A Boy's ESCIPLDE —IIIOMHS Griffiths, age 9, of Keen- street, was charged with stealing from the Fair 'Ground at, 12.30 on NVeduesciay afternoon it pony, cart, horeeeloth, and set of h 'mess value £l5, the property of Edwin Parsonage, Winwick.—The Chief Constable said •* this is a very small boy with a very big charge a,gainst him." The circum- stances were that on Wednesday morning the boy was left in charge of the pony and cart n the Fairground, by Mrs. Parsonage. H.i appld for the jrb of holding the pony and it was given him. At 2.33 she went for it and found neither the horse. trap,nor boy. Information was given to the police and at 7 o'clock Detective Sergeant Monaghan met the lad and quf stioned him. He said that he knew nothing abut it and that he had left it in he Fairground. Inquiries were made, and it was found that the boy had taken toe pony and cart to it. Helens and had been dr.ving about, ultimately deserting it in Lovely lens where it was foun I. This kind 01 escapade was getting q.aite common among boys. They brought the charge of theft against him in order that he might be punished. Deendant was said to be beyond the control of his parents,and a truant. He had teen birched for truancy.—The lad was ordered to receive another six strokes of the birch. WIFE ASSAULT.—Thomas Hill was bound over for six months and ordered to pay ICB, and costs 1, r assaulting his wife Annie on the 6th M ty.—Mrs. Hill, a delicate looking woman asserted that defendant often Mused her when he came home without money and she complained. That was the case on the day in question, when he struck her and threatened to swing for her.</p>
