      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>WARRINGTON COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Captain WithinkOn (chairman). Major Pickmcre and Messrs. J 81 ckley, and W Houghton. SWE AR ENG IN OF A NEW MAW STR kTE. —Mr. William Houghton, (chairmaa of the Itura District Council), wag formal}, sworn in as a justice of the peace for the County of Lancastr.</p>
        <p>DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.— Henry Johnson, Sidney John- son, and George Moss, were charged with being drunk and disorderly in Great Sankey, on the 19th of April.—Abel Sing. an ex-constable, said he saw the two Johnsons atter the eccurrence and they were no drunk. —They were each fined 53 including costs.—Albert Etiw•nson and George Ed- winscn were similarly charge 1 and fined a like amount.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>RUNCORN PECTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PECTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>BioNDAY.—Before Messrs. j rundrett, A. Thomas and J. W Lfght'-urn. THE THEFTS OF IRON AT WESTON. -Fred floodall and Mary Aspell were again brought up to answer it charge of stealing on April 21st, two crow bars, one amrnr...r and one pick head', valued at 13s . the property of the Runcorn and He!shy Red Sandstone Co., and also about:2Blbs of old iron, valued at 95., the property of the Alkali ('o. at Weston. James Tudor, assistant manager at Weston, said that about five wee!rs ago, he saw a eart near Aspell's house and the two prisoners were loading it with iron. Ms suspicions were aroused and he reported the matter to the manager who since the coal strike had allowed women on the premises to pick cinders. The police were given instructions to watch defendant Aspen. He identified the iron iprodueeci) —P C. Morris stated that he saw Mrs Aspell on the 21st April coming from the Chemical Works with a bag ant he stopped her and asked her what she had in it. Defendant replied " Vothing only cinders." He examined the bag and found in it a piece of iron. He then went to the house end searched it fp:A found between 20 and 3) cwt. of old iron together with two erow-bars and a hammer and pick head. He charge i both prisoners with stealing iron and Goodall said he had bought it at Weston Point but after- wards admitted that a portion of the iron seas from the Chemical works. Aspen said she knew nothing about the iron.—Arthur Guest. m in iger of the Red Sandst me Quarries, said the crow-bars, the hammer and the pick head were toe property of his c imp Inv and were marked with t he company's stamp. He had given no one permission to take them away and they were not amongst the scrap iron which was usually sold in "big lots." Both prisoners pleaded not guilty and Goodall called limmo nlizibeth ttutton who stated that she had given defendant Goodall a crow-bar which she found in an empty house in which she removed Born... Wine ago.—Prisoners were fined 10s. and costs in each case or in default were given option of going to goo for 14 days. A THEATRICAL 9QtrA.nutm —William Bird WAS charged with unlawfuly assaulting Sarah Tubb on the let May at non- com. Mrs. Tub's said defen taut came to her apartments on the let May in Waterloo-ro pi. He had been advance agent fo: her huseand's company "The Hunting Day " for some time. When defendant got in he ask.d her hosbind if he would give up some red caps and thins, belonging to Mr. Scott. Her husband said he would if Mr. Scott would write him to th it effect and release him from furtive* liability. This did not satisfy the defendant, who s`rock her husband with a stick and afterwards hit tier when she interfered, and pullet her cut by the hair of her head —Rdith Poster and William Tuhb gave corroborative evilence.—For this defence Mr.Allen.solic•tor,who represented Bird. put in an agreement by which dilendant had a right to soinepropsrtiet used in the play. No evidence was offered in the matter of the assaull,, arid defendant was fined 10s. end 12s. 61. costs, or 14 days' Imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>CURVATURE IN CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>CURVATURE IN CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>When baby is just beginning to toddle about, you often say she is too heavy for her legs. Now, if you will only go at the subject from the other end, you will be nearer right. Baby is not to heavy for her legs, but the legs are too weak for baby. Consequently, if you Ire not careful, her bones will soon begin to bend, as thPy did in the following case :</p>
        <p>"I think that I should be neglecting my duty very much in not giving a true account of the wonderful re- sult of Scott's Emulsion in the case of our little girl. About two years ago, my wife took her to the doctor's as she was very unetsy about her. Her wrists were very much enlarged, her shins were quite bent, and she used to waddle along like a duck. The doctor said it was a ease of rickets, and advised my wife to take her to the hospital, which she did. The doctor there ordered her legs to be put in splints, and she was to wear them for two months ; but I very soon discontinued them, and my wife said she would try Scott's Emulsion. It had cured our Maud of bronchitis, and we thought it would do this one goal. So we started, and, after she had taken about two or three bottles, we could see a wonderful change for the better. We continued to give her Scott's Emulsion til she had taken eleven bottles. Her legs are as straight as an arrow,and she is as fine a child as you would with to see. Anyone is quite welcome to come to the above address and Se 3 her for themselves. lam never without Scott's Emulsion in my house. My children drink it like drinking milk, they are. that fond of it.—Yours truly, JOHN TARTE, The Woodbines, Washwood Heath. near Birmingham. January 21st, 1896." Children need nourishment for their growing bones as well as for making of healthy flesh. Scott's Emulsion supplies both. It contains the purest Norwegian cod- iver oil, combined with the hypophosphites of lime and soda. Both of these remedies are essential to children. who do not make satisfactory progress in growth, and in no other form than Scott's Emulsion can the most benefits from them be derived. This is because Scctt's Emulsion is a scientific remedy, perfect in manipulation, and better adapted to the needs of the system. It is perfectly palatable, and does not derange digestion. When children show, by weakness, paleness, thinness, or other evidences, that they are not deriving sufficient nourishment from their food, Scott's Emulsion wil quickly restore vitality, flesh and strength, stimulate! their appetite, and make them rosy and robust. It solidifies the bones, enriches the blood, and makes their growth strong and vigorous. On the wrapper of every genuine bottle of Scott's Emulsion, is the tradc-mark of a fisherman carrying a big fish on his back.</p>
