        <p>SANKEY WIRE MILLS V. ST. ELPHIN'S.-Thig match,the first in connection with a Cricket League in Warrington, was played on the ground of the former, an tesulted in an easy win for the homesters. The visitors won tha toss but decid,d to send in the homesters to bat. The first two batsmen offered such a stubborn resistance to the attack that they practically placed the matter out of the Saints' mach. Change after change was made, but for three parts of an hour without avail, till W.iodcoek unfortunately played on, after scoring a freely hit 26. Williams did no: stay long after, he retired for a capit- ally played innings of 25. 01 the others Elobqon played a fine game for his 21 nQt out. The Wire Mills declared their innings cipsed at five wizkets for the respectable score of 104. The visitors offered but poor resistance to the homPstcrs' bowling, Woods being the only one who reached donble flames, the innings closing for the poor tntal of 35, Simpson taking six wickets for 14 runs, and Hobson three for 16. Score :</p>
