        <p>EARLESTOWN V. Sr. HELENS RECREATION—At. Earles- town, in dull weather,the light being exceptionally bad. Earlestown went t) the wickets first, Husband and Dennis facing th.? attack of Robinson and Gregory. Dennis was dismissed early on for 4by Robinson, but Miller, who replaced him, made a persistent stand, and added 13 before Gregory removed his bails. Robinson easily dispo3ed of Nukes, who followed, for a " duck," and Husband, after contributing 30, which included five 4's, was bowled by Gregory. Clare ma:le a short stand, and was sent back by Robinson for none. Alder- sova after adding 4 was taken in long-off by Leyland. Crowther filled the s.acancy, and Harper and Shaw took up the bowling at 80. Barker hit out str9ngly,and both batsmen played the bowlers freely at 89. Crowther was bowled by Shaw for 14. Bell took the vacant place,and Gregory relieved flP.rper of the leather. After scoring a single Bell was taken by Nichol at slip. Emmett re- placed him with the score at 100. Emmett was run out, his contribution loving 7. The vacancy was filled by Mitchell, and he and Barker brought on another change of bowlers, Robinson replacing Shaw. Barker played with confi fence. and Mitchell hit out strongly, but was bowled for 10 by Robinson, the whole side being out for 115. The Recs. followed on, Robinson and Gregory taking first place, the former being taken by Miller in the slips without scoring. Gregory and Leadbett r made a lengthy stand, and before being separated had toge- ther piled on 63. A change of bowling affected a speedy downfall of the Recs.' team, Alderson shifting their bails quickly. Pen</p>
