      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>WARRING TJN.</head>
        <p>WARRING TJN.</p>
        <p>There is every reason to hope that the vexed question of wharf accommodation at the river side at Warrington, in which Messrs. Bishop and Co., borax mahufacturers, have played an important 'nit, will shortly be settled. A joint meeting of the Rivers and Street Improvement Committees was held last week at the Town Hall, and it was ultimately decided that the Corporation should construct a whart and lease a portion of it to Messrs. Bishop and Co. It is expected that the work will be completed in the course of about three or four months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>INTERESTING MARRIAGE Al ST. PAUL'S CHURCH.</head>
        <p>INTERESTING MARRIAGE Al ST. PAUL'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>A wedding in which considerable interest was manifested was solemnized in Sc. Paul's Church, on Thursday last week. The contracting parties were Mr. James S. Haynes, eldest son of Mr. Haynes, leather merchant, 67, Sankey-street, and Miss Nellie Aukland, daughter of Mr. E. Auklaud, stonemason, Rolleston-street, end residing in hroghall-lane. The ceremony which was performed by the Rev. 11. V. Pigott (vicar) was witnessed by a large congrega- tion. The bride, who was given away by her father, was elegantly attired in a dress of dove- coloured material trimmed with white satin and tulle. She wore a white hat trimmed with tulle and white tip. She carried a beautiful shower bouquet,principally compoAed of liliPs of the valley an white roses. There were four bridesmaids in attendance, and they looked very pretty. Miss Haynes and Miss Ethel Aukland wire dresses of pale blue, shot with pink, and trimmed with silk and lace; and Miss Minnie Haynes and Miss May Aukland were attired in dresses of grey, shot with silk, and silk and lace trimmings. White hats with white tips and tulle were worn. Each carried a bouquet of choice flowers, the gift of the bridegroom. The duties of best man were satisfactorily fulfilled by Mr. W. Haynes. There were also three groomsmen viz.: Messrs. Prank Aukland; William Diainett, and W. Litton. As the wedding party left the church, the organist, Mr. Wilkes, pla3ect music ap- propriate to the occision. The wedding breakfast was afterwards served at the house of the bride's father, when upwards of 40 guests were present. The newly-married couple later in the afternoon de- parted for London where the honeymoon is being spent. The bride's travelling dress was of cloth with blue covet coat.</p>
        <p>Wedding presents were received from the following : —Bridegroom to bride, diamond brooch ' • Mr. and Mrs. Haynes, walnut bedroom suite, kitchen fuiniture, uten- sils, ks. ; Miss Haynes (Daventry) dinner and tea ser- vices ; Mr. and Mrs. Haynes (Alderley Edge), table linen; Miss M. A. Haynss and Miss Minnie Haynes, cut- lery and silver spoons; Mr. W. A. Haynes and Miss M. Aukland, marble clsck and pair bronzns ; Miss A. Haynes, pair oil paintings ; Mr. F. Haynes, pair oil paintings ; Miss J. Harne.s, album ; Rev. J. and Mrs. Yonge, silver biscuit t;arret ; Mr. J. Tinsley, silver jam dish ; Mr. W. C. Litton. flower stand ; Mr. A. Jolley,cut glass ; Mrs. Cooper (London) spirit bottle ; Misses Cooper (London) salad bowl and servers; Dr. and Mrs. Braise, silver biscuit barrel ; Mr. E. and Mrs. Aukland, inn., carvers; Mr. Aukland. cheque ; Mrs. Aukland, house linen ; Mr. and Mrs. H. Tott, wring- ing machine; Mr. and Mrs. R. Denham, dinner ser- vice; Mr. and Mrs. IV. Aukland (St. Helens). tea service ; Mr. F. Ankland, ansroid barometer ; Miss E. Ankland. pictures ; Mr. D. Aukland, glass ; Mrs. Wain- wright, flower stand ; Miss Wainwright. silver teapot ; Mr. W. Dannett, salad bawl and servers ; Mr. and Mrs. Thsmas loft, IMPS .Mrs. Waller, plaques ; Miss Wag- staff, d'oyleys and tes cloths; Miss White, jsm disk ; Mr. and Mrs. J. Forster (Earlessown), silver sugar bowl and sifter ; Mr. and Mrs. J. Whittle, copper kettle ; Miss Whittle, cheese disk ; Miss Essenhigh (Cheltenham), table linen: Miss May Aukland, hot water jug ; Master Harold and Miss To!t, kettle ; Misses Gladys and Nora Toft, fire screen ; Mr. James Haynes (Daventry), rugs ; Mr. and Mrs. Bessvick, crust; Miss Beswick. nlarsce; Mr. and Mrs. Bs ker (Stamford), tea service; Mr. C. ;.3aldwell, carpet sweeper.</p>
