        <p>WARRINGTON TEAC HE RS AND THE EDUCATION BILL. .</p>
        <p>A general meeting in connection with the Warringten and District Association of the National Union of Teachers was held in Heathside Boys' School on Monday evening. The chair was occupied by Mr. John Wright ; and there were present—the Revs. E. C. E. Carleton, J. Davies, H. V. Pigott, Messrs. Finney, Cann, Upson, Glover, G. J. Brown. G. Wvkes, Wilcox, Edden, Malcolm, R.Clark, Blackwell, Gordon, Lawless,Knowles, the Misses Davies, Finney, Brown, Hoose, Rinnell, Brookes, and others. The meeting had been called to consider certain matters relating to the new Education Bill.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>ELECTION OF LOCAL AUTHORITIES.</head>
        <p>THE ELECTION OF LOCAL AUTHORITIES.</p>
        <p>Tile first resolution was moved by Mr. Gordon, as follows :—" That this meeting approves of the creation of local authorities for County and County Borough areas to administer both primary and secondary educa- tion, but is convinced that such authorities should be directly elected ad hoc by the electors in each County and County Borough respective!: ; that if the Local Authorities be appointed as is propo.sed in the Bill, they should by statute include invariably : members of School Boards ; meeebers of voluntary school committees ; teachers working in schools,and other persons interested in Education as Such, within the respective areas." He said the Bill was quite revoluntionary in character. First of all they came to tho change of authority. They saw that the Education Department was handing over to other bodies power to carry on and administer the work which the Education Department had been doing. They were quite satisfied with what the Education De- partment had been doing, but whilst that Department had had power to " black list " teachers, it had never been able to help teachers in cases of unjust dismissal. The new authorities would have more power. The Edu- cation Department had no power of differentiation, but if the authorities were allowed to carry on the work they would be able to alter the code so as to make it suitable for the schools in the different areas covered. It was a most vital part of the subject to cinsider who were go- ihg to form the local authorities. It was their duty to see that the men who were on these bodies were those who had qualifications and who were in sympathy with them. Let them take the committee at Warrington. Did they conoider that they were in sympathy with voluntary schools? He did not think so,and their latest action did not show it. If they had the authorities directly elected ad hoc they would be able to know whetner the candidates were right candidates, and it would be their duty as teachers and managers to run men who were in agreement with them. But if the Government would not allow them to be elected in that manner, then they de- sired that the members appointed by the County Council should be educationalists. Members of School Boards and managers of voluntary schools should be placed upon them. If the chairmen of voluntary schools were formed into a committee, it would be a committee of men who were in sympathy and touch with voluntary schools. He also contended that teachers wete well qualified to be co-optative members. Mr. Finney seconded the resolutbn, and stated that ho was most concerned about the constitution of the committee. If the Bill was passed in January next he anticipated the County Conncils and Borough Councils would be called upon to elect a committee, and that committee would be the educational authority in the counties and towns. That committee might consist either of the members of the Council, or they might bring in others who were outsiders ; at all events it would be largely composed of members of Town Coun- cils. The committees would not be able to raise rates or borrow money, and they must report their proceed- ings for confirmation to the Town Council appointing them. There would be some danger in these gentlemen. They might net always be in sympathy with education and so forth, but the teachers would be sufficiently en- lightened on the subject to get men who did take an interest in educational matters.</p>
        <p>The Rev. E. C. E. Carleton said he was not altonether in favour of committees being elected ad hoc. He did not think it was altogether the best way of maintaining the schools in which they were all so much concerned. He was afraid they were getting vary much in these days into the way of having nobody in power to manage anything unless elected ad hoc. He did riot think that that was altogether satisfactory. What they wanted were fair-minded intelligent men experienced in public business. He thought that they should simply pass on that occasion a resolution in favour of supporting the general Principles of the Bill, merely saying that they hoped the Government would see their way to 'make the financial proposals more satisfactory aria more advan- tageous for the adequate maintenance and working of the schools.</p>
        <p>Mr. Finney wished to explain that his sympathies were not altogether with the ad hoc principle. What they wanted was to get the hest men on the committee. Ho did not care where they came from. Let them have men with broad views, who took a sympathetic interest in education generally. (Hear, hear:, The Rev. J, Davies, thought that members of School Boards, voluntary schools, and teachers servinct in schools and other persons interested shou!d have the first claim on the Educational Committee, and be co-optative. He meved, therefore, as an amendment that the educa- tional authorities should be as proposed in the act, but that co-optative members must 5e appointed and should consist of members of School Beards, members of volun- tary schools, and teachers working in schools.—Mr. Up- son asked what had Warrington schools benefited by the administration of the County Council ? Here they had an object lesson which showed to them the absolute necessity of having a committee elected ad hoc for the distribution of the educational funds and for the setting up of the educational standard. Mr. Carleton proposed that they pass to the next reso- lution.</p>
        <p>Mr. Blackwell seconded the amendment. On a vote being taken the resolution was however carrie.d. THE DISMI.SAL OF TEACHERS, EX RANEOII tTASKS.</p>
        <p>Ms:Tiiy-ies moved "That in the opinion of this meet- ing the Education Authority, however appointed, should be empowered and required to appoint a representative or representatives as managers upon each committee of voluntary' schools receiving the special aid grant. The Bill should also include provision for means of appeal by teachers against dismissal from their employment, and prohibition against grants being given to any school in which teachers are required as a condition of appoint- ment to, or continuance in, employment to undertake or abstain from the performance of extraneous and non- scholastic tasks."—Referring to the proposed appoint- ment of a representative as manager upon each commit- tee of voluntary schools, he said he believed it was intendei that the representative should act in the capacity of A correspondent between the committee and the Education Department. With regard to the proposal for provision for means of appeal by teachers in case of dismissal, he said some people seemed to think that it was a new thing. But the matter had occupied the attention of ministers of education for many years. There wag a court of appeal for teachers in certain coun- tries. Scotland had one, and in the Isle of Man there was some means of appealing against unjust dismissal. Several of the colonies had special means of appeal for teachers, and the same was the case in a number of the Urtited States. He did not see why English seachers should not have the right of having their cases of dis- missal inquired into more thoroughly than was often done by small School Boards and managers of voluntary schools who were responsible to no-one hut themselves, and very often not responsible te themselves. (Laugh- ter.) The last part of the resolution, was a very deli- cate question. and one upon which all the teachers were not agreed. A teacher might undertake extraneous duties if he chose to dosn. hut managers of schools should not make their position the lever to extort from some unwilling teacher that which he or she would not do voluntarily. (Hear, hear.) He did not think that a teacher should he compelled to do that which he was disinclined to do in his leis tire hours. He did not say that they should not he Sunday school teachers or organista, but they should not be compelled to he such. Mr. Elden seconded the resolution. He said the first part would he a valuable mean of keeping in touch with the voluntary school managers. With regard to the suegested court of appeal for teachers, he might say that they did not wish for fixity of tenure, but they wanted reasonable protection against capricious dis- missal. (Hear, hear.) Cases repeatedly occurred in which teachers were dismissed for reasons not connected with school work. The National Society recommended an appeal court very recently, and Cardinal Vaughan had instituted a court of appeal. As illustrating the need of the last recommendation, the sneaker read a number of advertisements cut from the "Schoolmaster" in whirl: many extraneous duties were demanded from the applicants. They did not wish to prevent teachers from undertaking voluntary work so long as it was vo:- sinters%</p>
        <p>The Rev. E. C. E. Carlehown supported what had been said with regard to a court of appeal for teachers. hut ho thought some parts of the resolution were unnec.s- sary. The one relating to extraneous duties rFepiired safeguarding. They must be very careful heeause they did not want to preclude a schoolmaster from playing' the organ for which he received extra pay. Re did not think he had made any stipulation to the teachers in his sehools for extraneous. work. They did it voluntarily. (Bear. hear.) Be was very grateful for their help. It would be a very great hardship to enforce anything, of the sort. Rut there were very small village schools. where he did not th7nk day school teachers were fully employed and not altogether overworked considering they had a holiday on Saturday. (" Question.") It was not altogether unreasonable to expect such a taacher to assist in the Sunday school where teachers were difficult to get. Be thought they should be very careful in pass- ing that resolution. The matter might be left to the local kuthorities.</p>
        <p>The Rev. .1. Davies said he objected to any dismissal that was not done in open court. His experience was that the teachers in village schools instead of having less to do than teachers engaged in towns, were more worried. The resolution was carried. SCHOOL INCOME.</p>
        <p>Mr. Blarkwell moved the followinz reFolution "That this meeting protects against proposals to prc-</p>
