        <p>hibit School Boards from raising the sums rece=sarY tfr I ensure the efficiency of School Boards; and condel the proposal to limit in all schools the Parliamentary fee grants to the sums now reeeived or to a rn. axitoll; rate of £1 94. In view of the growing popular demalre for more complete and effectual primary edlication meeting considers that the income oi schools should adjusted from time to time to the necessities of the situation, and should not be fixed at any maximum ra. r though a minimum is justifiable and desirable; and tnr the Educational Authority should not dispense al grants to schools in which the salariei pad t 5 the eta are unsuitable and insufficient." J. Mr. Wilcox seconded the resolution.—The Davies said some School Boards spent lavishly ; n°6: much upon the teaching or apparatu., but upon cler , and ornate school bnildings. 140 thought when a school; rate had got to such a sum as 3. in the £ it was higo time to draw the line of expenditure somewhere. Wile,d. the Education Bill of 1870 was passed it was suggeg`—,,a that in no district would the school rate exceed than 3d. in the £. Mr. Blackweli said the electors should be the judgel°.! the expenditure. If they were dissatisfied with thet representatives who spent the money, they could throw them out. The resolution was carried.</p>
        <p>THE SPECIAL AID GRANT. Mr. Upson moved :—" That this meeting consider!, the propased special aid grant inadequite for its Ill; pose ;that the Bill does not afford strli tient safeguir?he that this grant shall be spent on improving the teach staff as regards number, cptalifloation, or salary ; no differentiation should tic set up between Vi and Board Schools, as such, in the dispensation of la Special Aid ; and that the aim of the Bill should 10.10 aid poor Voluntary and Board 8,-:hools on the same ba'of ja of distribution, rather than to give the same rate special aid to all, irrespective of local needs." a 5 that salaries were 8. 9f I. per 11ad !ess in volun:lbe schools than in Board schools. If the teacher go,.vbsa Whole of the 4s. special aid grant he would get les4,lod— nalf the sufficient amount to bring him up te the B—vrcr ard of the Board school.—Mr. Cann seconded tbel</p>
        <p>position which was carried.</p>
        <p>MISCELLANEOUS : SCHOOL ATTENDANCk nroyeS The Chairm,n moved :—That this meetina the proposed establishment of a local a.uthority, 10 -.and county and county borough area, for primal*/ -- secondary education alike ; appication of all tonds.rfp) cetvahle under the Local raxation (Customs and Escl-1 Act, 1890, to educational purposes ; raising of the s.:hoS attendance ac,,e to 12 years ; protision of better locP; authorities for school attendance.", and itnprovement educational facilities for children now attending Inder trial and Poor Law 5ch0015.,,.....Mr. Brown seconded! and said it was very important that school atentiaooT should be carefully- considered. Several attempts 1111't been madein Warrington lately to raise the standard °: attendance, but with very little success, so far as could see, for although the last provision caused a litw improvement it seemed ta be going back again. A hand was required in dealing with the lases of 13. attendance at school.—The resolution was agreed to. t A vote of thanks to the Chairman cr,ncluded the Wee"' mg.</p>
