      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>FATALITY AT MESSRS. MONKS, HAl'l° AND COMPANY'S WORKS.</head>
        <p>FATALITY AT MESSRS. MONKS, HAl'l° AND COMPANY'S WORKS.</p>
        <p>RUN DOWN BY AN ENGINE. 00 At the Parade }Were, Irlarn-s-treer, Warrington. id Tuesday afternoon, Mr. F. Jones, depul'y coroner, beko an inquiry into the circumstances of the death of Jel:g Wright, 67, Howard-street, Sankey Bridges, who killed at the works of Messrs. Moeks, Hall and C0mP.,411: on Monday morning. Councillor F. W. Monks and Burton (manager) represented the Company at inquest. Mary Jane Wright, wife e deceasel, said the 14tter ing was 40 years of age. She saw him on Monday morn when he left to go to his work a few minutes before , He was then In good health. He was not hard of beg'''. ing and his sight was good. John Hammond, 46. Foster-st., labourer at MOO; Monks. Hall and Co.'s works said on Monday rnorni°,4 about 10-30 he and deceased were going towarda closets. They had to cross the railway line runnill through the works. They saw nothing of the engine:l4g it was close to. Deceased was about two or three Y from witness and the enEene w 3. aleert Bor 7 card 3 "8 when witness saw it. Deceased did not see it until boo was on the line and then being a heavy mats he could oto throw himself back again. Ile put his hands OEO_O save himself. The engine struck him and he wta knocked down between the metals. Witness went him but he never spoke, though lie was net quite rlei_sof Both were perfectly sober. Close to him was a baneho "blue billy" which sloped down to the line and t:Pg together with deceased's weight prevented him gettelfa back. If the ground had been level he would have P-R a better chance. The engine was whistline• before...4f came up taut they did not see it. Witnees did net thlro there was room for a man to pass bstween the eegi and the bank of "blue billy." Hoseah Howard, driver of the engine, said he tue going about 4or 5 miles an hour. He was sounding.,ol whistle until he was close to the spot where dece7,t* was. He did not see deceased until he was abe-off yard off and he coald not then stop, theogit he :Abut steam. Witness went to deceased who died in his arcild Deceased would have had a better chance if the grtni had been level. 'deist A plan of the works showing the scene of the eel tot was shown the jury, to aid them in understanding nature and cause of the accident. A the. A verdict of accidental death was returned an -A 00 jury recommended that the closets should be move"ads that the men would not have to cross the line to re" them. tbef Mr. 'Monks intimated that they would do the bet e- est could to obviate the danger and would also remove " blue billy " which had been referred to.</p>
