      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>WARRINGTON TEMPERANCE FEDERATION.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TEMPERANCE FEDERATION.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL PUBLIC MEETING.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening the annual public meeting of the Warrington Temperance Federation was held in the Bold-st. Wesleyan schoolroom. There was a good attendance, and the chair was occupied by Mr. E. 3. Guest, J.P. On the platform with the Chairman were the Revs. Canon Hicks, G.D. Mason, C. Andrews J. Davies, E. T. Reed, A. M. Mitchell, W. L. Roberts (Liverpool), and Messrs. R. Garnett. J.P., W. Roberts, J. C. Dalton, S. B. Stringer, J. Bo! eland (Liverpool). and Sergeant Mantle.</p>
        <p>LETTERS or APOLOGY.</p>
        <p>The Secretary (Mr. W. Roberts) read letters of apology for non-attendance from the Revs. John Yonge, J. J. Langham, W. H. Drummond, and Miss 13. Dutton.</p>
        <p>The REV. J. YONGE wrote :—Dear sir,—Please be good enough to explain to the meeting of the Federation this evening the cause of my most uawilling absence. I hope that there will be a good attendance, and that something may be said which will stimulate our temper- ance friends to renewed diligence and zeal. I especially regret being denied the privilege of meeting and hearing Canon Hicks, whom we have so often tried to secure, but until now have been disappointed. I trust his words will have an earnest hearing and that they will be well reported and widely read. With heartiest wishes for a very successful meeting.—</p>
        <p>The Rev. W. H. DRUMMOND- wrote :—Dear Mr. Roberta—l am sorry that I shall not be able to be present at the meeting next Tuesday, as I shall be away from home. I trust that you will have a good and enthusiastic meeting. At present the political tempera- ture is very low indeed on this question, hut there is always room for earnest educational work. You will agree with me that some fresh interest and some new lines of activity would be beneficial to us hare. If I mention only one topic out of the large number to which I might allude, it is because it is one in which I take a special interest, and even the temperance mind does not always give it the prominence it deserves. I refer to the urgent need of legislation to prohibit the sale of intoxie- , ating drink to children and young persons. The facts are so startling and so horrible that I cannot doubt we should get a bill passed with the consent of the vast majority of right-thinking people of all parties, if once we could get the facts before them. It is my hope that in the coming season I may be able to do something in this direction for the public of our town.— The Rev. J.J.LANGHAM in his letter sail :—Dear sir.— I very much regret that an important engagement in my own parish will prevent my attendance at your meeting to-night in time to hear Canoe Hicks. I trust however that the meeting will be successful and well- attended.There is certainly-room at the present time for a good deal of educational temperance work. I cannot help feeling that something should be done by the tem- perance party throughout the country in connection with the proposed Royal Commission. The constitution of that Commission is perhaps not calculated to lead us to hope for any very heroic legislative suggestions. But , it is of importance that the question should be duly pre- sented to the Commission from a temperance point of view. An adverse decision would be used as a very valid excuse by legislators to pass the question over in future. A favourable decision from a body so consti- tuted would 'have exceptional weight, and would add immense force to the strength of our demands. Definite evidence therefore should be collected throughout the , country and laid before the Commissien,clearly showing the need of Sunday closing, of the prevention of the sale of intoxicants to children, of a better mode of dealing with habitual inebriates and defining also any injustice that may arise in the mode of granting licenses under the present system. I venture to suggest the possibility of collecting such evidence for the town and neighbour- hood of Warrington as a subject well deserving the atten- tion of the Federation at the present juncture. Should I have it in my power to render any assistance in such a matter I should esteem it a privilege to do so.— Miss Derreoer wrote from Harrogate :—Dear Mr. Roberta—That I am unable to be present at the Tem- perance Federation meeting to-morrow is a great dis- appointment to me. My stay here, unfortunately, en- tirely prevents me attending, but cannot lesson my hearty wishes for its great success. Jeaning from the weighty list of its supporters drawn I am glad to note from almost all denominations, I think the latter is aasured. May the members of every section of the Church of Christ know nothing but a prayerful unani- mity, which shall make them mighty when they go forth in the name of a common Lord to confront the sin and shame, the utterly needless ruin, which strong drink has brought in our midst. With many regrets that I cannot be with you, and be cheered as a weak but willing worker in the " Total Abstinence " cause, by the words of Canon Hicks and his supporters. The CHAIRMAN said he was sure that all reeretted very much the absence of their esteemed president, the Rev. John Yonge—(hear, hear)—and he should like it to go forth from the meeting that they had very hearty sym- pathy with him in his illness, and an earnest desire that he might soon recover to resume those duties which he performed so well. (Applause.) They had met together that night to censider a very important subject—that of temperance. Many of the enemies of the movement doubtless thought or hoped that a death-blow was struck to the movement when the result of the last elec- tion was known, but he was sure that they would be very much disappointed. (Hear, hear.) The future lay before them. The achievements of the movement had been very great in the past, and they would be consider- ably greater in the future. (Hear, hear.) Let them look for a moment at what had already been done. It was due to the temperance movement that the drunken- ness to-day was considered to be such a heinous sin. The party had done a great deal to show that elcehol was a poison, wag not necessary as a beverage, and was not even neceesary as a medicine. They were learninir, he thought, more and more, year by year. the fearaul extravagance of the drink bill incurred in this coentry from time to time and he thought also they we"m'arn- ing more and more that the manufacture alcoholic drinks was injurious to the material, me‘at.liocial. and spiritual well-being of the people. Th-1 were also learn- ing by the teaching of their Freez 67 and other societies of a kindred nature, that 16enses were not a vested interest but were greed 137 the Government for the supposed wek"u'eg of the community and that if thri would show that those licenses were not aecessary for the well being of the community r.os' would not be renewed. (Appleuse.) That was thought,a great deal to have accomplished in the The temperance movement was like a stre,.., which had been running and gaining in volume v..€ by year. Malty people seemed to think it had been stopped, but they believed it had only been dammed up for a little time, that none of its power had been lost. hut had accumulated and strengthened. (Applause). The achievements of the past were theirs, the duty of the present was theirs, and the victory of the future was theirs they believed. (Applause.; What they needed, was to educate the young. middle aged, and the old. They could not legislate beyond the wishes: of the people but he trusted that during the next five years, in which possibly no drastic remedy would be provided by the present Parliament, the temperance party would concen- trate its forces and gain power and strength for future efforts. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>REPORT FOR TICE TEAR.</head>
        <p>REPORT FOR TICE TEAR.</p>
        <p>Mr. ROBERTS read the report for 1198 its follows : "Although the Federation has not been as active during the past year as some of our friends wnuld desire,it may be reasonably expected that the year 1896 will st•P more vigorous work accomplished. Your committee has taken active measures which it is hoped, will make the Federation more representative of the various temper- ance organisations in the town and neighbourhood. Already a considerable number of societies, not prev- iously associated with the Federation, have appointed delegates to act upon its committee, and the nntloek for the future is of a more promising character. During the year several meetings have been held and addressee delivered by welt-known temperance advocates. Under the auspices of the National 'temperance Congress. a very successful meet ng was arranged for by the Federa- tion in the Co-operative Half, Sankey-street. Your committee is pleased te record the fact that at the last Brewster Sessions all new applications for licenses were successfully opposed by Mr.?. R. Roberts, aoliciter,who was instructed by ratepayers resident in the districts affected by the applic4tions. Our brethren connected with the Workingmen's Temperance Miseian are still carrying on their work vigorously, and your committee has again voted them a substantial sum of money towards their annilal expenses. Your committee recognise the importance of greatly in- creased effort in pushing forward the educational work of the Federation. Meetings of an attractive character should be held in various parts of the town and district, and we earnestly hope that all our friends will be will- ing to sacrifice both time and money in order that a large amount of work may be undertaken and great good accomplished. In concluaion your committee would urge the friends of temperance to increase their contri- butions. If every total abstainer would financially sup- port the mosement which has conferred such innumer- able blessings upon all of us, our opportunities for use- fulness in Walrington would be vastly increased. We could " command success" belause our united and earnest efforts•would deserve to obtain it. The ex- ppercee he said had been £39 164. 2d., and the income £4O 19s. 2d. leavtg a balance in hand of £1 3s. As president. the Rey. W. fl. Drummond, 8.A., had been eleltad : the vice-presidents were all re-elected, with the addition of Councilor Luke Etlison; Mr. S. B. Stringer was treasurer and Yeasrs. W. Roberts and J. C. Dalton hon. secretaries ; th 4 committee was as before.</p>
        <p>THE SKIN IS CONPOSED of three layers, and con- tains muscles,nerves,bood vessels, lymphaticsoebsceous eland, sweat glands, ett., while the hair and nails are its appendages. It is k most complicated piece of machinery, and subject, like any other organ to health and disease. To preservethe skinocalp,and hair,and to restore them to a condition of health when diseased, nothing is so agreeable or ro speedily effective as CUTI- CURA SOAP.MiSted in the Serer forms with mild appli- cations of CIITICCRA (ointraert), the great skin care.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. MITCHELL moved the adoption of the report, which he gaid he thought was a very nice one because it was brief, to the point, and because it ended in the usual way and that was to say with the almighty dollar (Laughter.) He would like to say that he theught every minister of religion no matter what denomination he was associated with, ought to be a total abstainer. (Loud applause). He had no patience at all with those ministers:, or parsons, or priests, or whatever they called themselves. whe could not deny wholly and entirely all drink, for the good of the people. (Applause.) They wanted a Roman priest on the platform to mak. the coal of relizion complete.. The Church of Rome did in some places a most excellent work in connection with the temperance movement. Next year he hope] that they 'night see a priest of the Church of Rome in the room. (Applause.) Mr. Mitchell concluded by wish- ing every success to the great cause they all had in hand.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. C. DALTON seconded the motion, and apologised for the non-representation of the Catholic clergy which he explained was unavoidable. He then spoke strongly about the need for active temperance work in this "beery borough," and advocated more open-air crusad- ing. He thought the courts. alleys, and yards of the town should he visited and made meeting places for the propagation of the temperance cause. The Rev_ Canon Elicits said it was his lot to attend a great many temperanco meetings and when he came to any town he was told it was the head-quarters of the drink trade. It was wonderful how many places in England were headquarters of the traffic. (Laughter.) He had also generally found that where the liquor in- terest was very strong therl was a strong and determined following of Temperance-workers. (Applasse.) The liquor interest was everywhere and it he was to total up the entire capital which the British people had Invested in the liquor trade it would be a really fabulous and , almost incredible amount. It was distributed over a vast number of persons, for owing to various circum- stances, some creditable and some less creditable a large number of the great l•rewery concerns had been turned into limited liability companies and wherever thcy went they did not know whether they were rot speaking to a brewery shareholder. (Applause.) At the last election there was that influence, and possibly in ;Warrinzton they had their opinions about the weight that influence lent to one particular party that eventually came to the top. It was extremely important that they should unite and concentrate their action. The party in power should devote attention to the poor,the outcast and helpless. When any party or sec- tion of a party proceeded to forget the poor he had found that the judgment of God was certain and downfall must ensue. He could not help feeling that the same spirit which made men at the general election laugh at the claims of the helpless and speak for the claims of the publican and brewer was the same spirit as the Jinzo spirit. He himself stood by right and by justice—(ap- plause)—by men of reason, conscience, and justice. In the kingdom there were 168,000 licenses and every one of those was a fountain from which issued the poisoning of her Majesty's subjects. Among the best customers of every licensed house were men and women who had ac- quired the drink craze, which medical men said was the most awful disease known to medicine and almost the most incurable. If in Warrington anything sold by the grocer, such as tinned meats, did anything like the amount of injury to health and detriment to the lives of the people as did drink.the whole town would be up and the evil promptly stayed. (Hear, hear.) Yet no such feeling was shown over the curse 4f drink. They could ask any mother if she was glad that her son had begun to go to the public-house and join his uncle and friends, and see what the answer would he. It would never be " yes." (Applause.) Child neglect too was in almost every case due to the drunken habits of father or mother or both. (Hear, hear.) If the drink traffic could be stopped the children would live comparatively happy and prosperous lives. Temperance sup- porters were not in good odour sitt the great ones of the earth. Lord Salisbury—,he was one of the great ones—he was a very clever man— but what a poor opinion he had of them. He considered them hair brained fools and further had the opinion that they were all in the North of Eogland and that the South was comparatively free and harmless. HOWEVer he was glad to see that many titled ladies werecomieg over to their side. (Applause.) When the ladies were interested they could often talk over .a father, a brother or a son. They must stand true to their principles. (Hear, hear.) With Mr. Dalton he agreed that more open air work was wanted. In municipal elections the teMperance men should make themselves active and offer their ser- vices to the ward committees as canvassers and bring as much influence to bear in the right direction as possible. The people should fote, he said, for admissive prohibi- tion. They demanded that liberty. The people wanted to have a word Co say. Sweden,once a very besotted coun- try had carried a prohibition bill over a large part of the country. In this country there were hundreds of places where they were sick of drink. Tne people should be in power. Let the drink-besotted neighbourhood have the power to say whether it wanted its chains snapped or not. (Loud applause.) Canon Hicks then moved the followirg resolution :—" That this meeting would very earnestly urge upon Her Majesty's Government and all members of Parliament the necessity of passing tha Sunday Closing Bill, which is down for second reading on the 6th of May,and thus secure to the people of Eng- land freedom from the Sunday sale of intoxicating liquors, a freedom similar to that enjoyed by the people of Scotland, Ireland, and Wa:es." 7he Rev. W. L. ROBERTS also gave an earnest and poetical address. In his opinion the defeat of the '1 cm- prance party at the last election was because the Eng- ICh people had not risen so as to look at their political duties+ and responsibilities from a sufficiently high and Christian standpoint. (Applause.) He seconded the Motion which was then unanimously carried. Councillor ROBERTS proposed a vote of thanks to the deputation and to Mr. W. R. Wallington for his songs. He was glad that he came in time to hear the eloquent sod able speech of Canon Hicks. He was desirous to hear nim because he had seen in the papers particulars of a little incident in which the Mayor ot Salford and the Rev. Canon Hicks conspicuouslrfigured. and in which lie did not think that Canon Hicks came worst off. (Ap- plause.) They, the Warrington Temperance party,were delighted to have him amongst them, as they needed the stimulus which men of his character and standing could give. (Hear, hear.) They could fight the -evils of drink when they met them face to face but when they came round a corner and attacked them unawares it was not quite Ito easy to come off best. Mr. Reherts was glad that Canon Hicks had referred to the fact that municipal elections should he fought to some extent on temperance lines. He was further pleased to say that though the brewing interest is largely represented on the Warrington Town Council they bad now got a fairly strong representation oLthe temperance interest. (Ap- plause.) He thought that those of them who had as- serted themselves in the temperance question had a right to appeal to the temperance spirit politically in this town to support it if they had to face it eve 7 three years. (Applause.) It w• s not an easy thing to fight it an temperanee linos when they had the hostility of the other side against them and when the men of their own thought and opinion did not come out so openly and give them the support which they bads right to expect at times. (Hear. hear.) Mr. R. Gaianwrr seconded the votes of thanks, which were then heartily accorded. Canon RICK 4 responded and referred to the interest ef drink and Temperance in Parliament and municipalities. What intereat had they in Temperance ? Not a pocket interest. They were losers and he himself was pecuni- arily a loser by it. Ths others were fighting for their pockets while they fought disinterestedly, not for self but for neighbour, religion, and God. (Applause.) Mr. BORELAND proposed a vote of thanks to the Chair- man which was also unanimously accorded.</p>
        <p>During the eventag Mr. W. H. Wallington gave admirable renditiorse of "Four Jolly Smiths" and "Th. Pedlar." Mrs. C. E. Parker accompanied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>RUNCORN RUGBY FOOTBALL CLUB.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RUGBY FOOTBALL CLUB.</p>
        <p>The success that the above team has achieved with the Lancashire and Cheshire section of the Northern Union is still the all prevailing topic in Runcorn. There is to be a fund commenced for the purpose of right royally entertaining the members of the club and as promises of support have been general the matter should be brought to a practical issue. At a meeting-of the com- mittee held no Monday evening an entertainment com- mittee was formed to arrange • ooneert at which the cup and medals will be presented to the players who have taken part in the competition. Ir. has been decided to invite Sir J. T. Brunner, But, M.P., to perform the ceremony.</p>
        <p>Tbe following table shows the doings of ths club dar- ing the last twelveseasons. Durinsr that time 43g matr,bes have been played out of which 297 have been won. 76 lost.and 59 drawn,certainly a very creditable record not often beaten loy other tmirna.</p>
        <p>. P:arod. Won. Drawn. Loot. 3833-4 1834-5 1885-8 1886-7 1887-8 1838-9 1889-90 1891-1 1891-2 34 1892-3 33 20 1893-4 1894-5 1895-6</p>
        <p>It may be interemting to know that the gate money taken at Broughton for admission to the final meeting was £174 85.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>HALTON.</head>
        <p>HALTON.</p>
        <p>SOCIAL EVENING. —On Thursday evening Mrs. Wray entertained Ulm senior members of Mr. Stokes and Mrs. Wray's classes and the teachers and church workers numbering about 100 to tea. The gathering was in every way pleasant, the rest of the evening being occu- pied with gams and songs rendered by Miss C. Wright, Mr. T. Haddock, and Mr. H. Walton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>EA.RLESTOWN A.ND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EA.RLESTOWN A.ND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>DRUNICINNEBB.</p>
        <p>At the Earlestown Police Station on Tuesday morning, before Mr. C. B. P. Borron, Michael Gilligan of Derby-street, Earlestown, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Houghton-st., on Monday, and in default of payment of a fine of ss. and costs went to prison for 14 days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>EARLESTOWN MISSION SUNDIY -SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN MISSION SUNDIY -SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>In aid of the above, special services were held in the Co-operative Hall on Sunday afternoon and evening. The services were conducted by Mr. W. Farndale of Manchester, and they were remarkably well-attended, especially in the evening, when the hall was crowded. A feature of the service was the reed and string band, which under the conductor- ship of Mr. S. Dring, played several selections in excellent style, including "Praise ye the Lord "; "Gloria"; "I was glad" and " Hallelujah." Miss Bircheal presiding at the piano. The children sang several hymns •in a pleasing manner, Master A. Dring giving the solo in the hymn "Room in Thine Arms." The collections amounted to .E 6 103. GOOD TIMPLARY.</p>
        <p>The weekly session of the Hope of Earlestown lodge of Good Templary was held in the Co-opera- tive Hall on Monday evening. Bro. Clarke, C.T., presided. The Secretary read an invitation from Bundle Branch, Latchfoed, to visit the lodge on Monday next. It was decided to defer the visit until a future date. Replies were read from the Government officials and from the Hon. T. W. Legh, in response to resolutions forwarded last week in favour of the Veto. and Sunday Closing Bills. The circular from the Grand Chief Templar containing inciting items was read by Sister Clarke, L.D., and reports of the Juvenile District Council were given by Bros. Cuttler and Q.NI. Sergeant Roberts. ITAYDOCK WESLEYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>Special anniversary services in aid of the above Sunday School were held in the Chapel on ::_unday. The services were conducted morning and evening by the Rev. Joseph Burrows of Hoylake, who preached to good congregations. Special hymns suitable to the occasion were sang by the choir. In the afternoon a service of sacred song entitled, "A rough diamond," was efficiently given by the choir, under the conductorship of Mr. G. H. Hunt The connective readings were effectively given by 'Councillor J. Forster, J.P.,,pf St. Helens. Mr. W. Briscoe presided at the organ. The collections amounted to. £23 12s. 31., which was a little in excess of last year. EARLESTOWN CO-OPERA.TIVE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Owing to failing health, the result of a paralytic seizure a few mOnths ago, Mr. Thomas Hargreaves, secretary and manager of the Earlestown Co-op- erative Society has been compelled to resign the position he has Ailed with success and credit for the past 11 years: At the time Mr. Hargreaves took up the position of manager, the 'society was not in a very flourishing condition, but by dint of careful management and by bringing to bear upon the affairs of the society his wide experience, he has brought the society into a•sound financial condi- tion, and he r ill leave it with the very bed wishes of the members for his ap4edy restoration to health. CHILD RCN OVER. AT EXRLESTOWN.—MARVELLOUS ESCAPE.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday afternoon, khorcly after half past fire, a iittle, boy named Arnold Clare, seven years of age, ion of Mr. William Clare of Nelsort,treet. Earles- town, had a miraculous escape from seems injury. The youngster with others was riding on lurry belonging to Mr. R. Morris, coal dealer, and when turning ant of a side street into Houghton-street, he fell off the lurry. and one of the front wheels passed over the lower part of his body. His screams attracted the notice of Mr. Morris who at once stopped the horse, and alter picking up the lad placed him on the Jury, but he was able to walk home after arriving at Mr. Morris's yard. Dr. Handy- side was sent for, and after thoroughly examining, the lad, said he had had a most marvellous (move and that he had not received any serious injury, GOOD TEMPLARY ( EBENEZER, LODGE). . _</p>
        <p>ThA Earlestown Ebenezer Lodge held its weekly session on Tuesday evening in the Co-operative Esrle-et. Bro. A. Morris, C.T. presided. One candidate was admitted to membership. The Sec- retary then read the quarterly report from G.C.T. and outline report of resolutions passed at Grand Lodge at Southport. The reports of the officers for the past quarter were given and adopted. The election ot officers for coming quarter then took place, the following being elected :—C.T. Bro. T. Dean, V.T. Sister Hopkins, Secretary Bro. John Tay- lor, Assistant-secretary Bro. J. Marsh, Fin.-secre- tory Sister Taylor Treasurer Bro. W. J. Taylor, Chaplain Sister Woods, Mar. Bro. Kenyon, D. Mar. Sister Owens, G. Bro. G. Wharton; Sen. Bro. J. Hall, P.C.T. Bro. A. Morris: They were then installed by Bro. W. J. Taylor, L.D., assisted by Bro. Nicolson and Sister A. Newman,' from Hope of Earlestown. The various committees were appointed, and the lodge closed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>VOLUNTRICR CHURCH PARADR AT NEWTON'.</head>
        <p>VOLUNTRICR CHURCH PARADR AT NEWTON'.</p>
        <p>Beautiful weather faveared the Annual Church Parade of the local contingents of Hussars and Volunteers at Newton. As on previous occasions there was a large gathering of spectators, many coming from Ashton, Colborne, and a bus Icad from Warrington. About two o'clock the boys from the Liverpool Farm School. headed by their band marched from the Reformatory to Newton. and took up a position in the open space near St. Peter's Church, where the band discoursed sacred and secular music until about three o'clock. The local Company of the 2nd. V.B. IC.L.R. mustered at the Orderly room in Southworth Road, 75 non-com- mii.sioned officers and men including the bugle band, parading under the command of Colonel Wood. The other officers present were Sergeant Instructor Hancker, Sergeant Houghton. Sergeant Appleton &amp;c.. and as the Company marched up to the street to the rendezvous opposite Mr. Parday's they presented a !smart soldierly appearance. .As they passed the church the " G Company of the let V. 13. Prince of Wales Volunteers numbering 61 non-commissioned officers and men, fell in behind the Company being in command of Lieutenants Hauer and Patterson, the other officers present being Sergeant Instructor Wooding. • Sergeant Major Sullivan. Q.M.. Sergeant Potter, and other eon-commissioned officers from Warrington. Their marching and dressing were excvtionally smart, and the oomments upon their appearance were of a favourable character. The "C " troop or Lanca- shire Huisars, paraded 44, exclusive of band, which was under the care of Band-master Batley. The troop was under the command of Lieutenant Willis, the other officers on parade being Sergeant Major Strange, and Dr. Street. Prior to the troop leaving their headquarters, the band took up a position opposite Mr. Pardey's and played "The Lost Chord." When all were assembled the order to march was given, and the whole body proceeded to St. Peters Church, headed by the Hussar band, playing a lively quick-step. 'I he service in church was con- ducted by the Rev. H. Monk, who preached an appropriate sermon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>PENKET'II.</head>
        <p>PENKET'II.</p>
        <p>CRUMMY To Honsits.—At the Warrington County Petty Sewsions. on Wednesday, a youth named Thomas Hignett was charged with having illtreated a horse on the 10th April, by working it when in an unfit condi- tion. William Day was charged with causing it to be so woiked. Both pleaded not guilty. Inspector Bilson,who prosecuted on behalf of the R.S.P.C.A., said that when he saw the horse it was towing a narrow boat on the canal at Ssiakey, and as he approached, the, younger defendant.who was in charge, detached it. The animal was very lame on both hind legs, and slightly lame on the fore legs. Mr. Dixon. vrterinery surgeon, of St. Helens, corroborated Inspector Bilson's statement.—The Bench discharged Higneet, stni 'fined Day 10s.and costa. —Two youthssnamed JamieVriglit and William Burke, were charged with similarly illtreating a horse at ?en- keth ; and Mary Cartlege,an elderly wonaan,was charged with aiding. and abetting. Inspector Billion said the youths were driving the pane, a Penketh about six o'clock on the evening of the 10th April. It'waa attached to a opring cart which was laden with rags,' bones, and other marine staff. The piny was lame in every limb and emaciated. Having craeationed Cartledge about the animal's condition,she admitted that she had bought it for £2 souse months ago, and that she had sent the boys out with it that morning. She offered to have the pony destroyed when she knew that proceedings would be taken against her, and about a quarter of an hour afterwards it was sold for 8&amp; and destroyed. Sergeants Devlin and Cheney corroborated the statement of In- spector !Waon as to the condition of the pony, and even- tually Cartledge was tined 20s. and costs, and the two youths 54. and costs each.</p>
        <p>CDMICI LADS' 13111GADE.—On Tuesday' night the members of the Great Sankey Company assembled at the head quarters, Sankey Sunday School, for a march out. Headed by the brass band the lads marched round the village. '1 here was a good muster of members, the officers present being Captain Mcliechnie, Leiutenants Patin and Ringer, and Rev. J. R. Jones. The Company will go int) camp with the Begade in August.</p>
        <p>OUR NOTED KID GLOVEB in Black. Brown and Tan, at le. 60., le. 110.. anti 2.5. 6d. par piir.—liVAN, 17, Sankey -etreet, Warrington. Advt.</p>
        <p>NEWTON DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The ordinary monthly meeting of this Council was held in the Town Hall, Earlestown, on Monday after- noon, when there were present—Mr. C. B. F. Borron, J.P., C.C. (chairman), the Rev. E. W. W. Kaye, and Messrs. McMahon,Gilmour, Wood,Bimpson, Slee,Beatty, Evans,Ormand,Brierley (surveyor), Worsley (law clerk), and Shirley (clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>CEMETERY COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>CEMETERY COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Simpson moved the adoption of the minutes of the Cemetery Cammittee.—The Rev. E. W. W. Kaye seconded, and it Was carried.</p>
        <p>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTER.</p>
        <p>The minutes of this committee, as published last week were adopted, on the rnction of Mr. Wood, seconded by Mr. McMahon.</p>
        <p>TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>These mit-lutes were confirmed on the motion of the Rev. E. W. W. Kaye. seconded by Mr. Wood.</p>
        <p>HIGHWAYS, PUBLIC WORKS, AND SANITARY COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This committee's minutes, as published in last week's "Examiner," were confirmed on the motion of Mr. Gil- mour, seconded by Mr. McMahon. In aidition to what was published last week the minutes stated that a sub-committee had visited the site of the suggested widening of Wargiave-road, and re- quested the Surveyor to get out an estimate of the cost of setting back the fence, and kerbing and channelling so much of that part of the road opposite to Wargrave Church as lies between Lawson's and Shuttle worth's cot- tages.—Mr. Collingwood promising after the estimate had been prepared, to see Mr. Pardey, with a view to the cost of setting back the fence being borne by Lord Newton.</p>
        <p>It was also decided with reference to the agreements in course of preparation by the nightsoil sub-committee that the clause requiring a sum of money to be deposited by Mr. Williams of New Hey Farm as an earnest of his faithfully carrying out his agreements' be not enforced. The quarterly report of the Medical Offices. of Health shows that the birth-rate was 36.2 and the death-rate 16 0 per thousand of the estimated population. There were registered 129 births and 57 deaths, leaving an excess of births over deaths of 72. The deaths are thus classified :—Enteric or typhoid fever 2, puerperal fever 1, erysipelas 1, measles 1, whooping cough, diarrhcea 1, rheumatic fever 1, phthisis 3, bronchitis, pneumonia, and pleurisy 10, heart disease 9, injuries 1, all other diseases 26.—Total, 57. The new cases of infectious sicknesi notified were 32 in number, 2 2 per 1,000 of the estimated population, and are thus classified : Scarlatina 18, diphtheria 5, enteric or typhoid fever 4, continued fever 1, puerperal fever 1, erysipelas 3. Total, 32.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>OAS AND WATER COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>OAS AND WATER COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The minutes of this committee contained a resolution that the Clerk be instructed to offer the No. 1 gas holder at the gas works to Mr. John Walker, of Leeds, for the suin of £35, to be taken down and removed at his expense. The Gas Manager's monthly report stated that the total consumption of water during the month by the Viaduct. Newton Bridge, and Parkside meters was 4,707,000 gallons being a decrease of 80,000 gallons on the corresponding month last year. It was further resolved that the Surveyor be instructed to prepare a plan of Park and other roads within the district, showing the position in which the gas mains will require to be laid,and gas lamps erected, for the use of the gas manager. _</p>
        <p>Mr. J. H. Tooney's apology for extinguishing a lamp in Crow-lane in February last,was accepted. Mr. Ormand moved the confirmation of these minutes. Mr. McM4hon seconded and it was carried.</p>
        <p>GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Borron moved that this committee's minutes be conrirmed,and on tke*Rev. E. W. W. Kaye seconding the motion.it was carried. Mr. ORMAND then moved that the Common Seal of the Council be affixed to a Highways and Sewers Rate of 10d. in the £, a General Improvement Rats of is. in the £, and a Water Rate of 21. in the £ (save ;the ex- cept the sub-district of Parkside, for which sub-district 0.. e Water Hate shall be at the rate of 3d. in,) ,and of the assessable Vallee upon the several owners and occu- piers, and other persons within the district liable by Law to btassessed te such rates. He said he thought it would be satqactory to learn that there was no increase in the rates. Reterring to the estimated expendi- ture and incon.l for the next year he men- tioned that tt.re would be an increase of £758 103. on the Highways' Rate and on the general improvemera. rate £769. The 10d. rate was estimated to produce £2,511, and the income from other sources would be £1,246. 'tile excess of expenditure over income would be £l,sa. The question might naturally be asked as to how thtv calculated to meet that expenditure. That would be tone out of the avail- able balance in hand. The expehlitare was much needed, and they had passed the minut,4, dealing with the additional works to be carried out. PrtAeipal among the items forming the increase of £1,534 was, R 592 for the footpaths in Park-road, Emmett's Brow, ana War- grave-road, and the probable purchase of a steam rort.r at £4OO. Taking everything into consideration they were in a happy position, and he put forth the motion above.—Mr. McMahon seconded.—The Chairman said it was very satisfactory to know that they were in the position of having a balance in hand to distribute in the improvement of the district next year and still to leave a balance in hand.—The motion was carried.</p>
        <p>This concluded the business after a record sitting of only 15 minutes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>The Council meeting on Monday was considered to be about the shortest on record. Meeting at four o'clock, the business was proceeded with briskly, and by 4.15 all was over. Mr. Stone drove up just 88 the members filed out, and he was greatly aston- ished to find that his presence on this particular occasion NM not needed.</p>
        <p>The weather during the past week has been exceptionally dry, and I am pleased to notice that the authorities have grasped the satuation, and dis- patched over some portions of the township, the water cart,for the purpose of laying the dust which has lately been flying about unpleasantly. I say sonic portions of the township, because although the residents of Wargrave pay their rates just as those who reside in the business parts of the town do, they have to endure the annoyance caused by the dust. 1, of course, do not understand why this is, nor wliy the wateriK process stops short at the Railway Station. I have on one or two ran and distant occasions seen the water cart on the War- grave-road,but that was when the Council had only one cart. How that they have two carts, I suppose it is expected, as Mr. liacitahon said,that they will do less work. However this may be, I think the Wargrave people are just as much entitled to have their dust laid at the expense of the township, as those in any other part of the town.</p>
        <p>The fancy dress ball which took place last week was a decided success in every way, and it is to be hoped that the affair will become an" annual." On this occasion the object was a charitable one, the proceeds being devoted to the funds of St. John'e Church, and I have not the slightest doubt these funds will benefit accordingly. Too much credit however, cannot be given to the parents of the children, who were at the trouble and expense of preparing and arranging the costumes of those tak- ing part. The parts which the parents and guardians play in matters of this kind are very often-over- lookedv but I should like to know what success would.attend a fancy dress ball or any other gather- ing where the presence of the children is necessary if the parents did not take an interest in it. Thus whilst affording some measure of praise to those who organize such undertakingr,l think the parents should take_ first place when credit is being awarded.</p>
        <p>The annual May Queen festival will take place on Saturday, and great preparations are being made to ensure success. There is no doubt the procession will be of the usual gigantic proportions, and that there will be a full representation of characters. Last year I called attention to the evident apathy on the part of tradesmen, and to paucity of the decorations undertaken by them. I pointed out then that they derived the greatest benefit from the gala, and that it was only right they should show that they took an interest in it.'apart from any monetary gain they derive., I trust this will refresh their memories and that, they will assist the private householder in an endeavour to make the town appear as gay as possible.</p>
        <p>THE Wlniunctrom TRADRB AND LAVIUR COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The half-yearly meeting of the above was held on Tuesday at the Waggon and Horses, Batter- market-street. There was a poor attendance of delegates. After the routine business a summary of the business brought before the Council during the past half year was given by the Secretary. It was stated that the financial position of the Council did not enable them to carry on the work so satis- factory Its desired, but it was trusted that the en- suing half-year would be more successful than the one just passed. A circular was read from the Ac- crington and District Trades Council making an appeal on behalf of a fellow trades nnioßist who had lost his whole life's savings through being generous enough to assist his fellow-workmen in time of need, the union afterwards failing with assets nil. The Warrington Council, considering the Case to be a very hard one, decided to forward one guinea towards the fund being raised for the re-payment of the loan. A vote of thanks to the Chairman concluded the business of the meeting.</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>RUGBY. NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Committee of the Rugby Union was held at Huddersfield on Thursday evening, Mr. Waller (Brighouse) presiding. The following clubs were re- ceived into the Union :—Walkden, St. Helens Recrea- tion, Salforcl, Crompton, Swinton, Rochdale St. Clement's, Werneth, Abbey Hill, Atherton Hornets, Old- ham Juniors, and Lees—all Lancashire; and Heckmond- wike and Horbury, Yorkshire. Quite a large number of clubs were fined various sums for late starts. It was decided to present the championship medals on the occasion of Manwingham sports on the 23rd May. Leave was given to the Manningham Club to present five extra medals to the reserves, and to Mr. Fattorim, secretary, and Mr. Freeman, president of the Club, as souvenirs. A sub-committee was appointed, consisting of Messrs. Hutchinson (Wakefield), Clifford (Huddersfield), Sewell (Leeds), Mills (Stockport), Warren (Warrington), and Grestv (Broughton Rangers), to consider the county management, cup rules, additional powers to touch judges. kc. It was suggested that touch judges should be empowered to draw the attention of the referee to acts of rough play, and that the referee should deal with them as if he had personally seen the rough play. The appeal of the Bradford Club against the decision of the referee in the Bradford v. Broughton Rangers' match was dismissed, Bradford not having complied with the rule as to the deposit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Monday, on the motion of Mr. J. W. Lowther,the Standing Order which forbids corporations from working their own tramways was suspended for the remainder of the session. One or two questions concerning the recent revelations at Pretoria were postponed,but Mr. Chamberlain, answering Mr. J. Ellis,said a rumour had appeared in one of the London papers to the effect that certain telegrams which had been published containing allusions to Mr. Rhodes and "the Chairman " were probably intended to refer to Mr. Rhodes and the High Commissioner. He had received a telegram from Sir H. Robinson in which the High Com- missioner stated that he understood " chairman " to mean Dr. Jameson, and" the letter of invitation " the letter addressed to him by the five members of the Re- form Committee. The High Commissioner added that he never had the slightest suspicion of what was going on,and the discovery that Dr. Jameson had entered the Transvaal came on him "like a bolt from the blue."</p>
        <p>In the House of Lords a prolonged discussion followed on Lord Dudley's proposal to go into Committee on the Companies Bill. It was suggested by Lords Leven and Melville that the bill should be referred to a Select Com- mittee, chiefly on the ground that some of the clauses were unduly stringent, and might have the effect of pre- venting men of good position and standing from accept- ing directorships. Lord Salisbury accepted the sugges- tion to refer the measure to a Select Committee,and this course was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>TUESDAY.</head>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Tuesday Sir M. Hicks- Beach, in answer to Mr. Pritchard Morgan,said the chief gold district in Wales extended to 30,000 acres. Within that district the area of the Crown lands and lands in which the Crown had all the minerals was about 13,000 acres. He did not think it would be advisable to expend money in prospecting. In reply to Mr. Hayden, Mr. Cuozot; said that Major Lothaire had apparently been acquitted on the ground of his having shown no criminal intention in the execu- tion of Mr. Stokes. By a decree of the Sovereign of the Congo State dated October 30, 1895, Europeans could not now be tried by a court-martial for offences punishable by death. Sir .1. GORST moved the second reading of the Educa- tion Bill. Mr. Asclurra, in moving that the bill be read a second time that day six months, said the opposition of the Liberal party to the bill was founded on the fact that it did not put education in the hands of local auth- orities, and that it destroyed the machinery by which education was at present carried on. In the opiniun of the Opposition the scheme contained in the bill would have the effect of revolutionising the foundations, dis- locating the machinery, impoverishing the results, and embittering the spirit of the whole system of national education. The Government would no doubt succeed in passing their bill, but they might be assured that they were not settling but only opening the controversy. Mr. Channing seconded the amendment. The debate was adjourned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>THURSDAY.</head>
        <p>THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Thursday, Mr. Rocas asked whether, having regard to the growing import- ance of Pretoria as a centre of diplomatic negotiation in South Africa,the Secretary for the Colonies would advise the Government to at once appoint as their agent some diplomatist of experience anti of English birth and train- kn. Mr. ClTAlomat-am said he fully recognised the im- portance of the subject to which Mr. Roam bad called attentive, but he would prefer to defer any statement until the ?Abate on Friday. Questionea by Mr. FENWICK as to the distress among the mining population of Northumberland, and asked whether the Government would take steps to remedy the distress by means of a subvention, Mr. CHAPLIN said he was aware that there had been much distress in the vill- ages of Bebside and Chevington, but he was glad to find that assistance had been given by the Northumberland Miners' Association, and also by persons in the neigh- bourhood. While he regretted that the distress existed, it was not in his power to take the course suggested. Sir M. litcxs-Bsaus,asked by Mr. D. Thomas whether he was aware of the distress among the miners of Aberdare and Rhondda Valleys, and whether he would take steps to relieve that distress similar to those pro- posed to be taken to assist the owners of agricultural land, said he was aware that certain collieries in the district had been closed because they could not be worked at a profit, and that a great reduction of wages had taken place in the last five years. The present rates, however, were higher than the rates in 1886.</p>
        <p>Another accident has occurred on the cliffs to the east of Dover—the fifth in five weeks. Two young men, visitors to Ramsgate, attempted to scale the chff, when one fell a considerable distance. He hai been medieally attended, but remained unecnscious, having sustained such injuries te the head that it is feared death may result.</p>
        <p>It Is now stated that the parties to the &amp;meg &amp;Elution in the Durham coal trade are the Earl of Durham and Messrs. James Joicey and Co. The output of Lord Dur- ham's collieries. 14 in number, is 12,000 tons per day. The purchase money is put down at from £800,003 to £1,000,000, and it is understood that the transfer will become an actual fact on June 1. Messrs. Joicey have at present under their control about 13 collieries employ- ing 4.000 men, and the number employed in Lord Dur- ham's pits is over 8,000, so that when the transfer is effected Messrs. Joicer, of which the leading partner is Sir James Joicey, M.P., will have control over nearly thirty collieries and 12,000 men. The L‘mbton fleet of steamers, which take in most of the coal won from Lord Durham's pits at Sunderland, are twenty in number.</p>
        <p>961 BOLD STREET,</p>
        <p>LIVERPOOL. HIGH CLASS PIANOS</p>
        <p>BY RRARD, STEINWAY. COLLARD AND COLLARD, BRINSMEAD, HOPKINSON, BORD, ALLISON,</p>
        <p>LND THR CELEBRATED DREAPER PIANOS, 4 CASH PRICES FROM 16 satTINEAS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>AMERICAN ORGANS</head>
        <p>AMERICAN ORGANS</p>
        <p>MASON AND HAMLIN, ESTY, BELL, BAUR, AND THB</p>
        <p>CKLEBRATRD NEWMAN ORGANS, FOR WHICH WM. H. AND G. H. DFLRAPER ARE THE</p>
        <p>SOLE AGBNTS. CASH, PRICE:FROM £7.</p>
        <p>PIANOS FROM 7S. PER MONTH. ORGANS FROM ss. PER MONTH.</p>
